{"parentUuid":"5f62736f-92f7-4546-b977-37f382358313","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_01CxvJWTg9BxK4XwgCnm4dYQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012tCh6dx9bouPezaKL9tXh5","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research multi-tenant agent hosting patterns","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Context: a startup hosts a free pilot where each signup instantly provisions a PERSISTENT personal AI agent (container on a single VM), which the user chats with over a dedicated Telegram bot. Agents are idle most of the time. Bottleneck: every signup = a full always-on Docker container. Research CURRENT (2025-2026) best practices for multi-tenant \"agent hosting\" / serverless-style architectures:\n\n1. Scale-to-zero patterns for per-user workloads: activation-on-message (message queue in front, container started on demand), Fly.io Machines suspend/resume (how fast? state preserved?), Cloudflare Durable Objects as per-user actor model, Modal sandboxes with memory snapshots, AWS Fargate/Lambda tradeoffs for this shape.\n2. The \"actor model\" approach: per-user state as data (files/db rows) + a small shared pool of worker runtimes that load a user's state on demand — precedents and pitfalls (who does this: Letta server, Durable Objects, Orleans-style virtual actors, Rivet Actors, etc.).\n3. Telegram-bot multi-tenant architectures: one bot with per-user routing vs bot-per-user; webhook vs long-polling at scale; message queuing/debouncing and idempotency (dedupe by update_id) — best practices to avoid duplicate replies.\n4. Capacity math precedents: roughly how many idle Docker containers (Node.js-based agent runtime, ~150-400MB RSS each) fit on typical VM sizes; techniques to raise density (memory limits, zram/swap, lazy start, kernel same-page merging).\n5. Provisioning-pipeline hardening for a public signup form: rate limiting, abuse prevention (free-tier crypto-mining and spam abuse of hosted agents is common — find documented cases/guidance), CAPTCHA/verification, quota per user, kill switches.\n6. Compliance/promise-keeping angle: if the product promises \"your agent runs on an isolated server that belongs only to you\", what architecture choices can still honestly satisfy that (dedicated microVM? dedicated container? marketing-vs-reality risk) — find how sandbox/agent-hosting vendors phrase isolation guarantees.\n\nBe honest about uncertainty. Structured English report, source URLs per claim. Your final message IS the deliverable."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":231,"cache_read_input_tokens":33873,"output_tokens":3704,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":231,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3704,"cache_read_input_tokens":33873,"cache_creation_input_tokens":231,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":231},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CcdQPJrSEuV9UBggfn3L3","attributionMcpServer":"nanoclaw","attributionMcpTool":"send_message","type":"assistant","uuid":"e357e07d-25f0-4c8d-8a65-e864595aa66b","timestamp":"2026-07-02T14:41:03.138Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"5f62736f-92f7-4546-b977-37f382358313","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a96b1813-8540-42d9-9d31-897bfe659ff9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MjXCEBpPnPpi6zVvGTQA37","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# NanoClaw Research Report\n\n## 1. The project is real and verified\n\n**Canonical repo: https://github.com/nanocoai/nanoclaw** — it exists, is public, and is actively developed (~30.1k stars, ~12.9k forks, 1,899 commits, latest release v2.1.17 on June 17, 2026, MIT license). The same repo is also indexed under **https://github.com/qwibitai/nanoclaw** — this is Qwibit AI, the agency of the creators; issues appear under both paths (a GitHub org transfer/rename — both resolve to the same project; fetching the qwibitai URL returned the nanocoai repo).\n\nTagline: \"A lightweight alternative to OpenClaw that runs in containers for security. Connects to WhatsApp, Telegram, Slack, Discord, Gmail and other messaging apps, has memory, scheduled jobs, and runs directly on Anthropic's Agents SDK.\"\n\n**Lineage (verified, and it differs from the hypothesis in the task):** NanoClaw is **not** a fork of OpenClaw. It was created by **Gavriel Cohen and Lazer Cohen of Qwibit AI** (Gavriel is ex-Wix) and launched **January 31, 2026 — one day after OpenClaw's rebrand** — as a security-first response. OpenClaw itself is Peter Steinberger's project: Warelay → Clawd/Clawdbot (Nov 2025) → Moltbot (Jan 27, 2026, after Anthropic trademark complaints) → OpenClaw (Jan 30, 2026). Sources: [DataCamp comparison](https://www.datacamp.com/blog/nanoclaw-vs-openclaw), [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/02/02/openclaw-open-source-ai-agent-rise-controversy-clawdbot-moltbot-moltbook.html), [OpenClaw Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenClaw), [VirtusLab blog](https://virtuslab.com/blog/ai/nano-claw-your-personal-ai-butler). Official sites: https://nanoclaw.dev/ and https://docs.nanoclaw.dev/ (note: nanoclaws.io appears to be a third-party/squatter site).\n\n## 2. Architecture (from README + docs + The New Stack/Pebblous deep dives)\n\n- **Host-container model:** one Node.js host process routes messages: `messaging apps → host process (router) → inbound.db → container (Bun, Claude Agent SDK) → outbound.db`. Entity model: user → messaging group → agent group → session.\n- **Container per agent group:** each group gets its own Linux container (Docker, or Apple Container on macOS), its own CLAUDE.md memory, workspace, and only explicitly allowed mounts. Bash runs inside the container, never on the host.\n- **Messaging/queues:** two SQLite files per session (`inbound.db`/`outbound.db`), each with exactly one writer — no IPC daemons or brokers. A 60-second sweep detects stale sessions and due scheduled messages. Channels: WhatsApp, Telegram, Discord, Slack, Teams, iMessage, Matrix, Google Chat, Webex, Linear, GitHub, WeChat, email (Resend).\n- **Claude Agent SDK:** runs Claude Code natively via the official SDK inside the container, including self-modification of the user's own fork.\n- **Credentials — OneCLI Agent Vault (documented):** credentials never enter containers; outbound requests route through OneCLI's Agent Vault, which injects credentials at request time with per-agent policies and rate limits. Sensitive paths (`.ssh`, `.aws`, `.kube`, etc.) blocked by default (per [Noma analysis](https://noma.security/blog/evolution-of-nanoclaw-and-enterprise-platform-risk/)).\n- **Scheduling:** recurring jobs that run Claude and can message back; supports pre-task scripts.\n- **Sub-agents:** billed as \"the first personal AI assistant to support agent swarms\" (Claude Code agent-teams); `create_agent` spawns long-lived companion agents, each with own container/workspace/memory, communicating over the same message queue.\n\nSources: https://github.com/nanocoai/nanoclaw (README), https://docs.nanoclaw.dev/concepts/groups, [The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/nanoclaw-containerized-ai-agents/), [Pebblous deep dive](https://blog.pebblous.ai/report/nanoclaw-architecture-deep-dive/en/).\n\n## 3. Known issues / roadmap (verified GitHub issues)\n\n- **Resource cost per agent:** ~200MB RAM per container (Claude Agent SDK + deps; +500MB if a transformers model is added); container spawn time is cited as the scaling bottleneck. [Issue #839](https://github.com/qwibitai/nanoclaw/issues/839): task containers that reply only via IPC `send_message` idle for 30 minutes until hard timeout (exit 137), blocking the group's queue meanwhile.\n- **Multi-tenancy / provisioning:** README explicitly positions NanoClaw as single-user/personal, not multi-tenant infra. [Issue #1184](https://github.com/qwibitai/nanoclaw/issues/1184) (open, Priority: Medium): hard dependency on local Docker socket makes restricted K8s (Sealos/EKS/GKE) impossible; proposes a provider-based container interface spinning up ephemeral K8s Pods/Jobs. No maintainer resolution yet. No first-class provisioning API found. [Issue #1067](https://github.com/qwibitai/nanoclaw/issues/1067): Apple Container runtime race on reboot. [Issue #554](https://github.com/nanocoai/nanoclaw/issues/554): no user-exposed cancellation of in-flight agent execution.\n- **Idempotency/duplicates:** [Issue #138] scheduled tasks execute twice (race between scheduler loop and queue); [Issue #293](https://github.com/qwibitai/nanoclaw/issues/293) scheduled tasks deadlock behind idle containers; PR #2123 suppresses duplicate text when `send_message` fires. The sibling OpenClaw has an extensive duplicate-reply problem class ([#33592](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/33592), [#34039](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/34039), [#37844](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/37844), [#42681](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/42681) — `deliver()` runs even after duplicate detection).\n- **Roadmap direction:** shrink the core further (\"a runtime so small an enterprise security team could audit it\"); expansion via skills/adapters rather than trunk features ([The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/nanoclaw-minimalist-ai-agents/)).\n\n## 4. Running MANY agents cheaply — what I found (and didn't)\n\n**I found no official maintainer guidance on shared runtimes, container pooling, or scale-to-zero.** I searched explicitly for \"shared runtime / scale to zero / pooling / lighter isolation\" — no hits in docs, README, or discussions surfaced. What the community actually does/discusses:\n\n- **Apple Container** as a lighter-weight native runtime on macOS (official README option).\n- **Ephemeral per-message containers** — the [nanoclaw-discord variant](https://github.com/jaypyles/nanoclaw-discord) spawns a short-lived container per message, self-destructing with zero persistent state (a de facto scale-to-zero pattern).\n- **Channel folding** — docs recommend sharing one agent group across many channels (unified memory, fewer containers) instead of agent-per-channel ([docs.nanoclaw.dev/concepts/groups](https://docs.nanoclaw.dev/concepts/groups)).\n- **K8s ephemeral Pods/Jobs** proposal in issue #1184 (open).\n- **Managed hosting** exists as third-party services, e.g. NemoClaw at $9/month per agent ([nemoclaw.computer](https://nemoclaw.computer/discord-agent)); [Railway one-click deploy](https://railway.com/deploy/nanoclaw).\n- Low-volume baseline: a Raspberry Pi 4 handles 1–2 concurrent sessions ([advenboost guide](https://advenboost.com/nanoclaw/)).\n\n## 5. Security analyses & incidents (this class of personal-agent gateways)\n\n**NanoClaw-specific:**\n- [HN: \"NanoClaw solves one of OpenClaw's biggest security issues\"](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46976845) — pushback that filesystem sandboxing addresses only one layer: prompt injection remains (\"lethal trifecta\": private data + untrusted content + external comms); suggestions included MCP proxies with permission checks, network allowlists, and \"surrogate credentials\" (which OneCLI's inject-at-request-time vault partially implements). Commenters flagged one pro-NanoClaw article as likely sponsored PR.\n- [Airia analysis](https://airia.com/nanoclaw-ai-agent-security-risks-shadow-ai/) — shadow-AI/governance risks: local credentials with no centralized revocation, data exfil to external models, no centralized audit trail.\n- [Noma Security](https://noma.security/blog/evolution-of-nanoclaw-and-enterprise-platform-risk/) — praises mandatory (vs optional) sandboxing and per-group isolation, but flags indirect prompt injection and agent-generated skills (backdoors/typosquatted deps) as persisting risks.\n- [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/gilpress/2026/03/13/dont-trust-ai-agents-says-nanoclaw-now-fully-integrated-with-docker/) covers the \"don't trust agents\" positioning.\n\n**Relatives (OpenClaw/Clawdbot/Moltbot lineage) — documented incidents:**\n- **Exposed instances:** SecurityScorecard found 135,000+ exposed OpenClaw instances (Feb 2026); a later Censys scan found 63,070 live.\n- **Live prompt-injection attack:** Zenity demonstrated a crafted message making an OpenClaw agent silently add an attacker-controlled Telegram channel, enabling remote command/exfil ([The Hacker News](https://thehackernews.com/2026/03/openclaw-ai-agent-flaws-could-enable.html)).\n- **Skill supply chain:** Snyk's ToxicSkills study — 36% of ClawHub skills contain prompt injection; 1,467 malicious payloads confirmed ([Snyk](https://snyk.io/blog/toxicskills-malicious-ai-agent-skills-clawhub/)); the \"ClawHavoc\" campaign planted 1,184+ malicious skills; a ClawdHub skill contained a credential stealer (1 of 286 audited). Exfil targeted plaintext keys in `~/.clawdbot/.env`.\n- **Autonomy failures** cited by Airia: an accidental $400,000 disbursement, inbox deletion, malware installed via prompt injection (all OpenClaw, not NanoClaw).\n\n**Honesty notes:** Issue #138's exact URL wasn't directly confirmed (referenced via search summary); star/download figures vary across sources (20k+ per one article vs 