{"parentUuid":"8fdc9ec2-7fd1-431d-b556-992a7e924a2b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a96b1813-8540-42d9-9d31-897bfe659ff9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HxghjNCmp1QriGssPm9AnM","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Report: Secure, low-cost sub-agent architectures for a persistent primary agent (2025–2026)\n\n## 1. Claude Agent SDK subagents: what they actually are\n\n**Documented facts** (from https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents — the canonical page; docs.claude.com/platform.claude.com redirect there):\n\n- Subagents are **separate LLM conversation contexts, not separate containers or sandboxes**. The docs describe them entirely in context terms: \"Each subagent runs in its own fresh conversation. Intermediate tool calls and results stay inside the subagent; only its final message returns to the parent.\" The parent→child channel is solely the Agent tool's prompt string; the child does not receive the parent's history or system prompt.\n- Defined three ways: programmatic `agents` parameter in `query()` options, markdown files in `.claude/agents/`, or the built-in `general-purpose` agent (plus built-in `Explore`/`Plan`). Programmatic definitions take precedence over same-named files.\n- Per-subagent knobs directly relevant to cost/security: `tools` / `disallowedTools` (least-privilege tool subsets, incl. removing MCP servers), `model` (alias like `haiku`/`sonnet`/`opus`/`inherit` per subagent), `effort` (`low`…`max`), `maxTurns`, `permissionMode`, `background` (non-blocking), `skills`, `mcpServers`. Nested subagents are allowed to depth 5 (since Claude Code v2.1.172); block nesting by omitting `Agent` from the child's tools.\n- Subagents are **resumable**: the Agent tool result includes an `agentId`; resuming the same session with that id continues the subagent with full history. Subagent transcripts are separate files, survive main-conversation compaction, cleaned up after `cleanupPeriodDays` (default 30 days).\n- Session **forking**: `forkSession` + `resume` copies a session transcript into a new session, leaving the original untouched (https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/sessions, https://platform.claude.com/cookbook/claude-agent-sdk-05-building-a-session-browser).\n- **Worktree isolation**: the Agent tool accepts `isolation: \"worktree\"`, which gives the subagent a separate **git worktree** so parallel children don't clobber each other's files (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees). Note this is *filesystem-collision* isolation, not a security boundary. Known bug: worktrees fork from the default branch, not the caller's HEAD (https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/45371).\n- For dozens–hundreds of agents, docs point to the `Workflow` tool (TS SDK ≥0.3.149), which moves orchestration into a script executed outside the conversation context (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/workflows via the subagents page).\n\n**My inference (not an explicit doc statement):** subagents execute inside the *same* Claude Code runtime process (the CLI process the SDK spawns), sharing the parent's filesystem, credentials, and network stance. Evidence: tool inheritance from the parent, shared session transcript files, `parent_tool_use_id` threading, worktree isolation being the *only* isolation option offered — and it's git-level. So SDK subagents cost ~0 infrastructure: they are additional API-call loops, not containers. Community write-ups agree (e.g., https://www.ksred.com/the-claude-agent-sdk-what-it-is-and-why-its-worth-understanding/).\n\n## 2. Ecosystem patterns\n\n- **Orchestrator–worker** is the dominant production pattern (~70% of deployments per https://gurusup.com/blog/multi-agent-orchestration-guide). Anthropic's own Research system: a lead agent plans and spawns 3–5 parallel subagents, then synthesizes (https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system).\n- **Stateless task agents vs persistent companions**: the SDK Agent tool / OpenHands SDK style is stateless one-shot workers; Letta-style agents are persistent rows in a DB (see §3). The emerging hybrid is exactly what the NanoClaw-style design implies: cheap stateless subagents for lookups, persistent named agents only when memory must accumulate.\n- **\"Agent = data, runtime = shared\"**: Letta is the clearest exemplar — the server is stateless between requests; all agent state (memory blocks, messages, tools) lives in Postgres, and any number of load-balanced server replicas serve any agent (https://www.letta.com/blog/letta-v1-agent, https://sudoall.com/letta-stateful-agents-nodejs/). Under this model an \"agent\" costs a few DB rows + a prompt/memory dir, not a container.\n- **Warm pools / snapshot-restore**: for the sandbox layer, E2B and similar providers keep pre-booted Firecracker microVM snapshot pools: cold boot 125–200ms, snapshot restore ~5–30ms, E2B advertises ~150ms provisioning; persistent sessions reuse one VM for ~16ms steady-state exec (https://www.spheron.network/blog/ai-agent-code-execution-sandbox-e2b-daytona-firecracker/, https://memo.d.foundation/breakdown/e2b, https://addozhang.medium.com/ai-agent-code-execution-sandboxes-isolation-from-containers-to-microvms-e80848effea5). Lesson: hard isolation doesn't require an always-on container per agent — it can be attached on demand in ~100ms.\n- **Queue-based dispatch / multiplexed runtime**: LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen/AG2 all run their agent graphs in-process by default; \"agents\" are objects, not processes (https://dev.to/agdex_ai/crewai-vs-autogen-vs-langgraph-which-multi-agent-framework-in-2026-51m6). LangGraph Platform-style deployments multiplex many logical threads over a worker pool with checkpointed state (inference from their persistence model; I did not verify a specific page for this claim).\n\n## 3. How comparable projects handle isolation\n\n| Project | Agent granularity | Isolation of tool execution |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Claude Agent SDK** | Logical context in one runtime process | Optional OS-level sandbox for Bash (srt), git worktrees for FS collisions |\n| **OpenHands** | One Docker \"runtime\" container **per conversation/session**; backend (agent loop, LLM calls via LiteLLM) is a separate server talking to the runtime client over REST (https://docs.openhands.dev/openhands/usage/architecture/runtime). The newer SDK makes the workspace pluggable: local, Docker, or remote API-based sandboxes (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.03690v1) | Full container per session; agent loop outside the sandbox |\n| **Letta (MemGPT)** | Agent = rows in Postgres; server stateless, horizontally scaled behind a load balancer — explicitly *not* container-per-agent (https://railway.com/deploy/letta-ai-agent, https://sudoall.com/letta-stateful-agents-nodejs/) | Tool execution sandboxing is optional/pluggable (E2B etc.); default is server-side execution — check before trusting (partly inference) |\n| **AutoGen/AG2** | Conversational agents in one Python process | Docker-based code executors are the recommended path for generated code (https://scalexi.medium.com/comparing-llm-agent-frameworks-code-execution-capabilities-langgraph-vs-autogen-vs-crew-ai-8bb1aa8c07e0) |\n| **LangGraph / CrewAI / Agno** | In-process objects/graphs; Agno's AgentOS is one FastAPI runtime hosting many agents (https://www.agno.com/agentos) | No built-in hard sandbox; delegated to external sandbox services |\n| **ElizaOS** | Multiple character-defined agents multiplexed in one TypeScript runtime; core manages memory/events for every agent (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.06781v1) | Weak — plugins run in-process; the paper itself flags runtime overhead and trust issues |\n\n**Takeaway (documented pattern across projects):** nobody who scales runs a full container per *agent identity*. The container/microVM, when used, is scoped to a *session or code-execution job*, while agent identity/memory is data.\n\n## 4. Security: the layered split\n\nAnthropic's own guidance (https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-sandboxing, https://github.com/anthropic-experimental/sandbox-runtime, https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sandboxing):\n\n- **sandbox-runtime (srt)**: OS-level sandboxing \"without requiring a container\" — bubblewrap on Linux, sandbox-exec/Seatbelt on macOS — used to sandbox the Bash tool, local MCP servers, and arbitrary processes. Filesystem: read/write limited to the working directory. Network: *no* direct egress; all traffic goes through a Unix domain socket to a **proxy outside the sandbox** enforcing a domain allowlist.\n- Their stated principle: \"Effective sandboxing requires *both* filesystem and network isolation\" — network-only lets secrets be exfiltrated; FS-only lets the sandbox be escaped. This is precisely prompt-injection blast-radius reduction: an injected agent can't reach unapproved domains or touch `~/.ssh`.\n- **Credential brokering**: Claude Code on the web keeps git credentials *outside* the sandbox in a proxy that validates and attaches scoped tokens per request — the agent never holds the key. Internally this cut permission prompts by 84%.\n- The wider ecosystem converges on the same three controls: (a) credential-injecting egress proxy, agent sees placeholders only (https://for-agents.com/essays/secrets, https://pipelab.org/learn/ai-egress-proxy/); (b) default-deny egress with per-credential destination allowlists (NVIDIA red-team guidance via https://www.armosec.io/blog/what-is-ai-agent-sandboxing-kubernetes-native-enforcement-explained/); (c) trusted control plane / untrusted execution plane split — GKE Agent Sandbox, Perplexity Sandbox API, Northflank all run the agent loop on trusted long-lived infrastructure and only the *code/tool execution* inside microVM-class sandboxes (https://dev.to/gde/untrusted-code-trusted-cluster-scaling-secure-ai-agent-workspaces-with-gke-agent-sandbox-1mk1, https://northflank.com/blog/how-to-sandbox-ai-agents).\n- Community consensus for genuinely untrusted code: shared-kernel Docker is *not* a sufficient sandbox; Firecracker/Kata microVMs or gVisor are the floor (https://northflank.com/blog/how-to-sandbox-ai-agents, https://blaxel.ai/blog/container-escape). But note Anthropic deliberately ships a *lighter* answer (srt) for the \"my own agent, my own machine\" threat model — the threat is prompt injection, not a hostile tenant.\n\n## 5. Cost/token control\n\n- Multi-agent is expensive by construction: Anthropic reports agents use ~4x chat tokens and multi-agent systems ~**15x** chat tokens; token spend explained ~80% of performance variance (https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system, summarized at https://blog.bytebytego.com/p/how-anthropic-built-a-multi-agent).\n- Documented levers in the Agent SDK (subagents page above): per-subagent `model` (route grunt work to Haiku/Sonnet — the docs' own example uses \"opus for strict reviews, sonnet otherwise\"), `effort` levels, `maxTurns`, tool restriction (fewer tools = smaller tool schemas + fewer wasted calls), context isolation itself (intermediate tool spam never re-enters the parent's growing context — this is the biggest saving for a long-lived primary agent), and prompt caching on the shared prefix.\n- Runaway risk: recursive spawning and oversized tool results can multiply the 15x by another order of magnitude — cap nesting depth and result sizes (https://fountaincity.tech/resources/blog/anthropic-multi-agent-blueprint-production/).\n\n## The layered answer to the key question\n\nThe convergent 2025–2026 model, combining Anthropic's practice, OpenHands, Letta, and E2B:\n\n1. **Multiplex safely (no hard isolation needed):** the LLM agent loop, logical agent identities, memory/prompt files, transcripts, channel I/O, and scheduling. These are data + API calls. One runtime process (or a stateless replicated server + DB, Letta-style) can host many logical agents; SDK subagents give you parallelism and context isolation for free. This layer should hold **no raw secrets**.\n2. **Hard-isolate per *trust domain*, not per agent:** what needs a real boundary is (a) arbitrary code/shell execution and (b) separation between mutually-untrusting *users/tenants*. Scope containers/microVMs to the execution session or the tenant, attach them on demand from a warm snapshot pool (~100–200ms), and let all of one user's agents/subagents share that one sandbox (OpenHands-style: one sandbox per conversation, many logical agents against it).\n3. **Middle layer — OS-level sandboxing without containers:** for a single-tenant personal-agent setup (the NanoClaw case), Anthropic's srt shows you can drop per-subagent containers entirely: run subagents in-process, sandbox only Bash/MCP tool execution with bubblewrap + FS scoping.\n4. **Secrets and egress always live outside every sandbox:** a credential-injecting proxy with per-destination allowlists (Anthropic's Claude-on-the-web proxy; Agent Vault; OneCLI-gateway-style designs). Agents get placeholders; blast radius of prompt injection is then bounded by the allowlist, not by hope.\n\n**Honest uncertainties:** (a) \"SDK subagents share the parent process\" is strongly implied but not stated verbatim in docs; (b) Letta's default tool-sandboxing posture and LangGraph Platform's exact multiplexing internals are partly inferred from secondary sources; (c) several search-result summaries above came from aggregator/blog sources rather than primary docs — the Anthropic, code.claude.com, OpenHands, and GitHub links are primary; treat Medium/dev.to numbers (e.g., the \"70% of deployments\" figure) as indicative, not authoritative."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7b31300519e778c8 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7b31300519e778c8', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 58622\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 213622</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"a1476f0e-6bcc-428d-a897-b9da85a0469a","timestamp":"2026-07-02T14:44:12.422Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively to research CURRENT (2025-2026) architecture patterns for multi-agent LLM systems, focused on the question: how to give one \"primary\" persistent AI agent the ability to use sub-agents SECURELY and with MINIMAL resource cost, rather than spinning up a full Docker container per agent.\n\nResearch and report on:\n1. Anthropic Claude Agent SDK (formerly Claude Code SDK): its subagent mechanism (Task/Agent tool, .claude/agents definitions) — are subagents separate processes/containers or just separate LLM contexts within the same process? What isolation do they have? What are session forking / worktree isolation options? Cite docs (docs.claude.com / anthropic docs, claude-agent-sdk repos).\n2. Patterns from the ecosystem: orchestrator-worker, stateless task agents vs persistent companion agents, agent pooling, warm pools, queue-based dispatch (one runtime, many logical agents), \"agent = data (prompt+memory dir), runtime = shared\" pattern.\n3. How comparable open-source projects handle multi-tenancy and sub-agents: e.g., OpenHands (formerly OpenDevin), Letta (MemGPT), AutoGen/AG2, LangGraph, CrewAI, Agno, and any \"personal agent server\" projects (e.g. Omnara, ElizaOS). Specifically: do they use one container per agent, one process per agent, or multiplexed runtimes? What sandboxing do they apply to tool execution?\n4. Security guidance for agents executing code/shell: principle of separating the AGENT LOOP (trusted-ish, holds no secrets) from the TOOL EXECUTION sandbox (untrusted); credential brokering/proxy patterns (secrets injected at proxy, agent never holds keys); egress allowlists; prompt-injection blast-radius reduction. Cite Anthropic's own guidance on sandboxing for Claude Code / agent security if findable (e.g., anthropic engineering blog posts on sandboxing, bash tool sandboxing, sandbox-runtime / srt).\n5. Cost/token implications: sub-agents as extra API calls — patterns for controlling token spend (model tiering per subagent, effort levels, budgets).\n\nKey question: what is the RIGHT layered model — which parts of a per-user agent need hard isolation (container/microVM) and which can be multiplexed safely (LLM loop, memory files, channel I/O)?\n\nBe honest about uncertainty; distinguish documented facts from your inference. Return a structured English report with source URLs per claim. Your final message IS the deliverable.","agentId":"a7b31300519e778c8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Report: Secure, low-cost sub-agent architectures for a persistent primary agent (2025–2026)\n\n## 1. Claude Agent SDK subagents: what they actually are\n\n**Documented facts** (from https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents — the canonical page; docs.claude.com/platform.claude.com redirect there):\n\n- Subagents are **separate LLM conversation contexts, not separate containers or sandboxes**. The docs describe them entirely in context terms: \"Each subagent runs in its own fresh conversation. Intermediate tool calls and results stay inside the subagent; only its final message returns to the parent.\" The parent→child channel is solely the Agent tool's prompt string; the child does not receive the parent's history or system prompt.\n- Defined three ways: programmatic `agents` parameter in `query()` options, markdown files in `.claude/agents/`, or the built-in `general-purpose` agent (plus built-in `Explore`/`Plan`). Programmatic definitions take precedence over same-named files.\n- Per-subagent knobs directly relevant to cost/security: `tools` / `disallowedTools` (least-privilege tool subsets, incl. removing MCP servers), `model` (alias like `haiku`/`sonnet`/`opus`/`inherit` per subagent), `effort` (`low`…`max`), `maxTurns`, `permissionMode`, `background` (non-blocking), `skills`, `mcpServers`. Nested subagents are allowed to depth 5 (since Claude Code v2.1.172); block nesting by omitting `Agent` from the child's tools.\n- Subagents are **resumable**: the Agent tool result includes an `agentId`; resuming the same session with that id continues the subagent with full history. Subagent transcripts are separate files, survive main-conversation compaction, cleaned up after `cleanupPeriodDays` (default 30 days).\n- Session **forking**: `forkSession` + `resume` copies a session transcript into a new session, leaving the original untouched (https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/sessions, https://platform.claude.com/cookbook/claude-agent-sdk-05-building-a-session-browser).\n- **Worktree isolation**: the Agent tool accepts `isolation: \"worktree\"`, which gives the subagent a separate **git worktree** so parallel children don't clobber each other's files (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees). Note this is *filesystem-collision* isolation, not a security boundary. Known bug: worktrees fork from the default branch, not the caller's HEAD (https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/45371).\n- For dozens–hundreds of agents, docs point to the `Workflow` tool (TS SDK ≥0.3.149), which moves orchestration into a script executed outside the conversation context (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/workflows via the subagents page).\n\n**My inference (not an explicit doc statement):** subagents execute inside the *same* Claude Code runtime process (the CLI process the SDK spawns), sharing the parent's filesystem, credentials, and network stance. Evidence: tool inheritance from the parent, shared session transcript files, `parent_tool_use_id` threading, worktree isolation being the *only* isolation option offered — and it's git-level. So SDK subagents cost ~0 infrastructure: they are additional API-call loops, not containers. Community write-ups agree (e.g., https://www.ksred.com/the-claude-agent-sdk-what-it-is-and-why-its-worth-understanding/).\n\n## 2. Ecosystem patterns\n\n- **Orchestrator–worker** is the dominant production pattern (~70% of deployments per https://gurusup.com/blog/multi-agent-orchestration-guide). Anthropic's own Research system: a lead agent plans and spawns 3–5 parallel subagents, then synthesizes (https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system).\n- **Stateless task agents vs persistent companions**: the SDK Agent tool / OpenHands SDK style is stateless one-shot workers; Letta-style agents are persistent rows in a DB (see §3). The emerging hybrid is exactly what the NanoClaw-style design implies: cheap stateless subagents for lookups, persistent named agents only when memory must accumulate.\n- **\"Agent = data, runtime = shared\"**: Letta is the clearest exemplar — the server is stateless between requests; all agent state (memory blocks, messages, tools) lives in Postgres, and any number of load-balanced server replicas serve any agent (https://www.letta.com/blog/letta-v1-agent, https://sudoall.com/letta-stateful-agents-nodejs/). Under this model an \"agent\" costs a few DB rows + a prompt/memory dir, not a container.\n- **Warm pools / snapshot-restore**: for the sandbox layer, E2B and similar providers keep pre-booted Firecracker microVM snapshot pools: cold boot 125–200ms, snapshot restore ~5–30ms, E2B advertises ~150ms provisioning; persistent sessions reuse one VM for ~16ms steady-state exec (https://www.spheron.network/blog/ai-agent-code-execution-sandbox-e2b-daytona-firecracker/, https://memo.d.foundation/breakdown/e2b, https://addozhang.medium.com/ai-agent-code-execution-sandboxes-isolation-from-containers-to-microvms-e80848effea5). Lesson: hard isolation doesn't require an always-on container per agent — it can be attached on demand in ~100ms.\n- **Queue-based dispatch / multiplexed runtime**: LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen/AG2 all run their agent graphs in-process by default; \"agents\" are objects, not processes (https://dev.to/agdex_ai/crewai-vs-autogen-vs-langgraph-which-multi-agent-framework-in-2026-51m6). LangGraph Platform-style deployments multiplex many logical threads over a worker pool with checkpointed state (inference from their persistence model; I did not verify a specific page for this claim).\n\n## 3. How comparable projects handle isolation\n\n| Project | Agent granularity | Isolation of tool execution |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Claude Agent SDK** | Logical context in one runtime process | Optional OS-level sandbox for Bash (srt), git worktrees for FS collisions |\n| **OpenHands** | One Docker \"runtime\" container **per conversation/session**; backend (agent loop, LLM calls via LiteLLM) is a separate server talking to the runtime client over REST (https://docs.openhands.dev/openhands/usage/architecture/runtime). The newer SDK makes the workspace pluggable: local, Docker, or remote API-based sandboxes (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.03690v1) | Full container per session; agent loop outside the sandbox |\n| **Letta (MemGPT)** | Agent = rows in Postgres; server stateless, horizontally scaled behind a load balancer — explicitly *not* container-per-agent (https://railway.com/deploy/letta-ai-agent, https://sudoall.com/letta-stateful-agents-nodejs/) | Tool execution sandboxing is optional/pluggable (E2B etc.); default is server-side execution — check before trusting (partly inference) |\n| **AutoGen/AG2** | Conversational agents in one Python process | Docker-based code executors are the recommended path for generated code (https://scalexi.medium.com/comparing-llm-agent-frameworks-code-execution-capabilities-langgraph-vs-autogen-vs-crew-ai-8bb1aa8c07e0) |\n| **LangGraph / CrewAI / Agno** | In-process objects/graphs; Agno's AgentOS is one FastAPI runtime hosting many agents (https://www.agno.com/agentos) | No built-in hard sandbox; delegated to external sandbox services |\n| **ElizaOS** | Multiple character-defined agents multiplexed in one TypeScript runtime; core manages memory/events for every agent (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.06781v1) | Weak — plugins run in-process; the paper itself flags runtime overhead and trust issues |\n\n**Takeaway (documented pattern across projects):** nobody who scales runs a full container per *agent identity*. The container/microVM, when used, is scoped to a *session or code-execution job*, while agent identity/memory is data.\n\n## 4. Security: the layered split\n\nAnthropic's own guidance (https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-sandboxing, https://github.com/anthropic-experimental/sandbox-runtime, https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sandboxing):\n\n- **sandbox-runtime (srt)**: OS-level sandboxing \"without requiring a container\" — bubblewrap on Linux, sandbox-exec/Seatbelt on macOS — used to sandbox the Bash tool, local MCP servers, and arbitrary processes. Filesystem: read/write limited to the working directory. Network: *no* direct egress; all traffic goes through a Unix domain socket to a **proxy outside the sandbox** enforcing a domain allowlist.\n- Their stated principle: \"Effective sandboxing requires *both* filesystem and network isolation\" — network-only lets secrets be exfiltrated; FS-only lets the sandbox be escaped. This is precisely prompt-injection blast-radius reduction: an injected agent can't reach unapproved domains or touch `~/.ssh`.\n- **Credential brokering**: Claude Code on the web keeps git credentials *outside* the sandbox in a proxy that validates and attaches scoped tokens per request — the agent never holds the key. Internally this cut permission prompts by 84%.\n- The wider ecosystem converges on the same three controls: (a) credential-injecting egress proxy, agent sees placeholders only (https://for-agents.com/essays/secrets, https://pipelab.org/learn/ai-egress-proxy/); (b) default-deny egress with per-credential destination allowlists (NVIDIA red-team guidance via https://www.armosec.io/blog/what-is-ai-agent-sandboxing-kubernetes-native-enforcement-explained/); (c) trusted control plane / untrusted execution plane split — GKE Agent Sandbox, Perplexity Sandbox API, Northflank all run the agent loop on trusted long-lived infrastructure and only the *code/