sage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4588,"cache_read_input_tokens":48509,"output_tokens":5523,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4588},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5523,"cache_read_input_tokens":48509,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4588,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":17}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8fdc9ec2-7fd1-431d-b556-992a7e924a2b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"a417d5d4-c5d7-4d3d-bbb4-6128c9f4a772","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a96b1813-8540-42d9-9d31-897bfe659ff9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GcGCbXUpxUiJipdk53niR7","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Lightweight Isolation for Many Mostly-Idle AI Agents on One Linux VM — State of the Art, 2025–2026\n\n**TL;DR for the key question:** If your agents are idle 95%+ of the time on one VM, the two winning patterns are (a) **hardened containers + cgroup-v2 freeze + swap/zswap reclaim** (near-zero CPU, RAM pushed to compressed swap, ~instant resume, but shared-kernel isolation), and (b) **microVM or sandbox snapshot/restore to disk** (true zero RAM/CPU when idle, sub-second resume, VM-grade isolation — but on most clouds this requires nested virtualization or bare metal, which is the single biggest practical constraint). gVisor's own checkpoint/restore is an underrated middle path: stronger-than-container isolation, works on any VM without KVM, and idle state can be written to disk.\n\n---\n\n## 1. gVisor (runsc)\n\n**Model.** gVisor interposes a userspace \"application kernel\" (Sentry, written in Go) between the workload and the host. Every syscall is intercepted — via the default **systrap** platform (seccomp + signals) or KVM — and re-implemented in userspace, so most syscalls never reach the host kernel. The host attack surface is reduced to the small set of syscalls Sentry itself makes (further confined by seccomp) ([gvisor.dev performance guide](https://gvisor.dev/docs/architecture_guide/performance/), [Northflank: What is gVisor](https://northflank.com/blog/what-is-gvisor)).\n\n**Overhead.**\n- Memory: commonly cited at **~15–35 MB per sandbox** for the Sentry (third-party comparisons say ~30 MB/pod; I could not find an official per-sandbox number from Google, so treat this as approximate) ([onidel comparison](https://onidel.com/blog/gvisor-kata-firecracker-2025), [Northflank comparison](https://northflank.com/blog/kata-containers-vs-gvisor)).\n- CPU/syscall: raw syscall microbenchmarks are ~2–3x slower than native; realistic workloads see **~0–30% overhead**, dominated by syscall frequency. Google reports the majority of their internal workloads see <3% ([gvisor.dev](https://gvisor.dev/docs/architecture_guide/performance/), [systemshardening.com](https://www.systemshardening.com/articles/linux/linux-container-runtime-alternatives/)). I/O-heavy workloads (compiles, npm installs — i.e., exactly what coding agents do) sit at the bad end of that range.\n- Startup: ~50–100 ms typical, essentially container-like ([onidel](https://onidel.com/blog/gvisor-kata-firecracker-2025)).\n\n**Docker integration.** Trivially good: install `runsc`, add it as a runtime in `/etc/docker/daemon.json`, then `docker run --runtime=runsc ...`. This is the lowest-friction \"real\" isolation upgrade that exists ([gvisor.dev production guide](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/production/)).\n\n**Limitations.** Of 351 amd64 syscalls, ~277 are fully/partially implemented, **74 unsupported** ([gVisor compatibility table](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/compatibility/linux/amd64/)). Things that occasionally break: exotic ptrace users, some io_uring, certain /proc and netlink features, FUSE edge cases, some eBPF. For a typical AI-agent workload (shell, git, python, node, network calls) compatibility is generally fine — Modal runs its entire sandbox product on gVisor at 20k+ concurrent containers ([Amplify: Behind the scenes of Modal sandboxes](https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/behind-the-scenes-of-modal-sandboxes)).\n\n**Bonus for your use case:** gVisor has **native checkpoint/restore** (`runsc checkpoint --image-path ... / runsc restore`), including async lazy page loading, and works without KVM — i.e., on any cheap cloud VM. Caveats: it's wired to raw runsc commands, not cleanly exposed through Docker (`--checkpoint-dir` not supported by Docker), and CPU-feature matching matters across hosts ([gVisor checkpoint docs](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/checkpoint_restore/), [containerd issue #12280](https://github.com/containerd/containerd/issues/12280)).\n\n**Maturity:** very high (Google-internal use, GKE Sandbox, App Engine). Operational complexity: low.\n\n## 2. Firecracker microVMs\n\n**Numbers.**\n- VMM overhead: **< 5 MiB per microVM** (official claim — note this is the *VMM's* overhead; the guest still needs its own kernel + userspace RAM, realistically 50–128+ MB per agent guest) ([firecracker-microvm.github.io](https://firecracker-microvm.github.io/), [NSDI'20 paper](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/nsdi20-paper-agache.pdf)).\n- Boot: **~125 ms** to guest userspace code officially; snapshot-restore gets you to **~25–200 ms** (one practitioner writeup: 28 ms; AWS Lambda SnapStart is built on this) ([firecracker repo](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker), [dev.to 28ms sandboxes](https://dev.to/adwitiya/how-i-built-sandboxes-that-boot-in-28ms-using-firecracker-snapshots-i0k)).\n- Density: designed for thousands of microVMs per host (Lambda-scale) ([Marc Brooker, \"Seven Years of Firecracker\"](https://brooker.co.za/blog/2025/09/18/firecracker.html)).\n\n**Security model.** Hardware virtualization (KVM) — separate guest kernel, minimal device model (virtio-net, virtio-block, few others), VMM written in Rust, typically wrapped in a jailer (namespaces + seccomp around the VMM itself). This is the gold standard for multi-tenant untrusted code (AWS Lambda/Fargate, E2B, Fly.io).\n\n**The critical constraint — KVM availability.** Firecracker requires `/dev/kvm`. On a typical cloud VM this means **nested virtualization**, and the support matrix is the deciding factor:\n- **AWS EC2:** historically no nested virt on regular instances — you needed `*.metal` (~expensive, large). In **early 2026 AWS announced limited nested-virtualization availability on C8i/M8i/R8i** — new and not yet broadly available; verify region/instance support before depending on it ([InfoQ, Mar 2026](https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/03/aws-ec2-nested-virtualization/), [firecracker dev-machine-setup](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/dev-machine-setup.md)).\n- **GCP:** nested virt supported on Intel VMs (enable a flag); works, with a documented **~10%+ perf penalty** for nested guests ([alexellis.io](https://blog.alexellis.io/how-to-run-firecracker-without-kvm-on-regular-cloud-vms/)).\n- **Azure:** supported on many v3+ series. **DigitalOcean, Hetzner, OVH, Vultr (bare-metal/некоторые plans):** generally yes — dedicated/bare-metal providers are the usual home for self-hosted Firecracker fleets. E2B's self-host docs historically targeted GCP for exactly this reason.\n- So: **on many \"typical\" cloud VMs (especially AWS non-metal), Firecracker simply won't run.** This is the #1 reason teams fall back to gVisor or hardened containers.\n\n**Snapshots.** Full VM snapshot/restore (memory + device state) is a first-class feature; diff snapshots and UFFD-based lazy page loading exist. Security caveat: **restoring the same snapshot more than once duplicates entropy, UUIDs, and cryptographic state**; Firecracker injects a VM Generation ID so Linux ≥5.18 reseeds its PRNG on resume, but application-level state (cached tokens, seeds) is your problem ([snapshot-support.md](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/snapshotting/snapshot-support.md), [random-for-clones.md](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/snapshotting/random-for-clones.md)).\n\n**Tooling.** This is Firecracker's weak spot — it's a VMM, not a platform. Options: **firecracker-containerd** (AWS project; maintained but niche, notable ops complexity — devmapper snapshotter etc.), **Kata Containers with the FC hypervisor** (works but devmapper requirement is a recurring pain point — see [kata issue #12558](https://github.com/kata-containers/kata-containers/issues/12558)), **flintlock/Liquid Metal** (community-owned since Weaveworks' collapse; alive but low-momentum) ([flintlock repo](https://github.com/liquidmetal-dev/flintlock)), or rolling your own against the REST API (what E2B, Fly, and most sandbox startups did). Expect to own guest kernel builds, rootfs images, and CNI networking yourself ([some-natalie: \"Please stop saying just use Firecracker\"](https://some-natalie.dev/blog/stop-saying-just-use-firecracker/)).\n\n## 3. Kata Containers\n\nKata runs each container (or pod) in a lightweight VM with a standard OCI/CRI interface — the \"managed\" way to get VM isolation. Overhead depends on hypervisor backend: **QEMU ~130–200 MB/pod, Cloud Hypervisor ~130 MB, Firecracker backend ~50 MB** (third-party benchmark figures; Kata's own default pod overhead in K8s is typically declared as ~130–160 MB RuntimeClass overhead) ([Northflank comparison](https://northflank.com/blog/kata-containers-vs-firecracker-vs-gvisor), [container-runtime-benchmarks](https://github.com/bikramkgupta/container-runtime-benchmarks)). Startup ~150–300 ms+; near-native CPU/IO once running (no syscall tax — syscalls hit the *guest* kernel natively). Requires KVM, same nested-virt constraint as Firecracker. Best when you're already on Kubernetes/containerd and want RuntimeClass-level choice; heavier and more moving parts than either gVisor or raw FC. Actively maintained (OpenInfra Foundation; used by AWS for K8s isolation guidance) ([AWS blog](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/containers/enhancing-kubernetes-workload-isolation-and-security-using-kata-containers/)).\n\n## 4. nsjail / bubblewrap / raw namespaces + seccomp + cgroups v2\n\n**What you get.** All of these compose the same kernel primitives: user/PID/mount/net/IPC namespaces, seccomp-BPF syscall filters, rlimits, cgroups. nsjail (Google) adds the Kafel BPF language and is battle-tested for CTFs and code-judging platforms; bubblewrap (Flatpak's engine) is setuid-less and ubiquitous. Seccomp is the real security layer: a process that can only call a small syscall whitelist can't reach most kernel bug surfaces ([google/nsjail](https://github.com/google/nsjail), [morphllm nsjail writeup](https://www.morphllm.com/nsjail-sandbox), [Windmill's isolation docs](https://www.windmill.dev/docs/advanced/security_isolation)).\n\n**Honest assessment.** You still share the host kernel. Isolation quality is proportional to how tight your seccomp profile is — and AI agents that run arbitrary shell commands need a *broad* syscall surface (fork/exec, sockets, mount-ish operations for package managers), which forces you to leave most of the kernel exposed. Known risk classes: kernel LPE bugs reachable through allowed syscalls (Dirty Pipe-class), **unprivileged user namespaces as an attack-surface multiplier** (Qualys demonstrated bypasses of Ubuntu's userns restrictions in 2025; multiple 2025–2026 CVEs leaned on userns) ([search summary; see also container-security.site breakout list](https://www.container-security.site/attackers/container_breakout_vulnerabilities.html)).\n\n**When it's \"good enough\":** semi-trusted workloads (your own agents doing dumb things, not adversarial tenants), defense-in-depth inside a VM you already own, or short-lived evaluation of code where you can afford a tight syscall whitelist. Not good enough as the *sole* boundary between mutually distrusting customers. Overhead is effectively **zero** — this is the cheapest option per instance by far.\n\n## 5. Hardened Docker as the middle path\n\nThe standard hardening stack for agent workloads: **userns-remap or rootless mode** (container root ≠ host root), `--cap-drop=ALL`, `--security-opt no-new-privileges`, default-or-tighter **seccomp** profile (Docker's default blocks ~40+ dangerous syscalls), AppArmor, `--read-only` rootfs + tmpfs for scratch, `--pids-limit`, cgroup CPU/memory quotas, no docker.sock mount, egress firewalling ([virtua.cloud hardening guide](https://www.virtua.cloud/learn/en/tutorials/docker-security-hardening-rootless-seccomp), [Ken Muse on rootless trade-offs](https://www.kenmuse.com/blog/rootless-docker-and-its-hidden-security-trade-offs/), [dev.to: sandboxing AI coding agents](https://dev.to/alanwest/how-to-sandbox-ai-coding-agents-without-crippling-them-116c)).\n\n**How far it gets you:** quite far against *accidents and opportunistic malware* — an agent that `rm -rf`s, forks-bombs, or curl-pipes something nasty is well contained. What it does not stop: a determined attacker with a kernel 0-day (shared kernel = one bug owns every container on the host), and runtime bugs like **runc CVE-2024-21626 \"Leaky Vessels\"** (container escape via fd leak, patched in runc 1.1.12) recur every couple of years ([Snyk Leaky Vessels](https://labs.snyk.io/resources/leaky-vessels-docker-runc-container-breakout-vulnerabilities/), [Wiz](https://www.wiz.io/blog/leaky-vessels-container-escape-vulnerabilities)). Notably, **Anthropic's and OpenAI's own coding-agent sandboxes are namespace/seccomp-based OS sandboxes, not VMs** — the industry treats this level as acceptable for agents acting on behalf of the same user whose machine it is, but hosted multi-tenant products (Modal, E2B, Fly) all chose gVisor or microVMs instead ([Medium: How Claude Code and Codex sandbox untrusted code](https://medium.com/@Koukyosyumei/how-claude-code-and-codex-sandbox-untrusted-code-ba39b493046a)). That split is the clearest signal of where the line is.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** hardened Docker is the right boundary *within one trust domain* (all agents belong to you/one org). Between paying tenants or truly adversarial code, add gVisor or a VM.\n\n## 6. Making idle agents cost ~0: pause, CRIU, swap tricks, snapshots\n\nThis is the crux for a 95%-idle fleet. Options, cheapest-to-resume first:\n\n| Technique | Idle CPU | Idle RAM | Resume | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `docker pause` (cgroup freezer / `cgroup.freeze`) | 0 | **full RAM retained** | ~instant (<10 ms) | Kernel freezes the process tree; memory stays resident ([oneuptime pause guide](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-08-how-to-use-docker-pause-for-container-freeze/view)) |\n| pause + **swap/zswap/zram + `memory.reclaim`** | 0 | ~0 resident (pages compressed in RAM via zram, or pushed to disk swap/zswap) | ms–seconds (page-fault-driven, gradual) | cgroup v2 `memory.reclaim` lets you *proactively* force a specific idle container's pages out (`echo 1G > memory.reclaim`); zswap now has per-cgroup accounting and writeback control ([kernel cgroup-v2 docs](https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html), [LWN: zswap cgroup accounting](https://lwn.net/Articles/892839/)) |\n| **CRIU checkpoint** (`docker checkpoint`, Podman, or containerd) | 0 | **0** (state on disk) | ~100 ms–seconds, depends on image size | True zero-footprint. Fragile spots: TCP connection restore, GPU state, some fd types; `docker checkpoint` is still marked experimental; Podman support is better. Checkpoint images can contain secrets — encrypt at rest ([criu.org](https://www.criu.org/Main_Page), [docker checkpoint docs](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/checkpoint/), [DevZero CRIU writeup](https://www.devzero.io/blog/checkpoint-restore-with-criu)) |\n| **gVisor checkpoint/restore** | 0 | 0 | sub-second, async lazy page load supported | Same idea as CRIU but the Sentry checkpoints itself — much more robust than CRIU-on-arbitrary-processes because the \"kernel\" state is fully owned in userspace. Needs raw runsc orchestration ([gVisor C/R docs](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/checkpoint_restore/)) |\n| **Firecracker/Kata VM snapshot to disk** | 0 | 0 | ~25–500 ms (UFFD lazy loading makes it fast) | Strongest isolation + zero idle cost; needs KVM; entropy-duplication caveat on repeated restores ([FC snapshot docs](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/snapshotting/snapshot-support.md)) |\n\nPractical composite that works well for a single-VM fleet of container agents: run each agent hardened + memory-capped; on idle, freeze the cgroup and `memory.reclaim` its pages into **zram** (compressed RAM, typically 2–3:1 for agent heaps) or disk swap with zswap in front; on an inbound message, unfreeze — the process faults pages back in transparently. Idle cost drops to roughly (RSS ÷ compression ratio) with zram, or near-zero RAM with disk swap, and resume is effectively instant for the first response with a short warm-up tail. This requires no snapshot tooling and no process-restore fragility. (Assessment mine; the primitives are documented in the kernel cgroup-v2 docs above. I did not find a published end-to-end benchmark of exactly this pattern — treat the compression ratio and warm-up figures as typical rather than verified.)\n\n## 7. Hosted sandbox services (for calibration or offloading)\n\n| Service | Isolation | Cold start / resume | Idle cost model | Sources |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **E2B** | Firecracker microVM, dedicated kernel | ~150 ms create (reported 78 ms p50 as of early 2026); pause ≈ 4 s/GiB, resume ≈ 1 s | Per-second while running (~$0.05/vCPU-hr + $0.016/GiB-hr) + monthly plan ($0 hobby/$150 Pro); paused sandboxes bill storage only | [e2b.dev/pricing](https://e2b.dev/pricing), [persistence docs](https://e2b.dev/docs/sandbox/persistence), [Beam pricing analysis](https://www.beam.cloud/blog/e2b-pricing-explained) |\n| **Modal Sandboxes** | gVisor | sub-second; memory snapshots give 3–10x faster cold boots (feature still maturing) | Pure usage-based; sandboxes at a premium CPU rate (~$0.0000394/core-s ≈ $0.14/core-hr, mem ~$0.024/GiB-hr); auto-shutdown on idle | [Modal memory snapshots](https://modal.com/docs/guide/memory-snapshots), [sandboxes docs](https://modal.com/docs/guide/sandboxes), [Blaxel pricing guide](https://blaxel.ai/blog/modal-pricing-alternatives-guide) |\n| **Fly.io Machines** | Firecracker, one VM per machine | cold start ~2 s+; **suspend/resume: a few hundred ms** | Stopped/suspended = rootfs storage only, **$0.15/GB-month**, zero CPU/RAM billing. Suspend limits: ≤2 GB RAM, no GPU/swap | [fly.io suspend-resume](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/), [pricing](https://fly.io/docs/about/pricing/) |\n| **Cloudflare Sandboxes/Containers** | container on Cloudflare's edge (Workers-adjacent) | \"milliseconds\" claimed; sleeps after idle (default 10 min) and **loses state on wake** (fresh container) unless using newer persistence features | Active-usage billing (CPU $0.00002/vCPU-s since Nov 2025); pay only while awake | [CF Sandbox pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/platform/pricing/), [lifecycle docs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/concepts/sandboxes/), [InfoQ GA note](https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/04/cloudflare-sandboxes-ga/) |\n| **Daytona** | container-based (OCI images), not microVM | ~90 ms, some configs ~27 ms claimed | usage-based; enterprise pricing less transparent | [Superagent benchmark](https://www.superagent.sh/blog/ai-code-sandbox-benchmark-2026), [Medium sub-90ms piece](https://medium.com/@kacperwlodarczyk/sub-90ms-cloud-code-execution-how-daytona-replaced-docker-in-our-ai-agent-stack-b6f343e4e547) — vendor-adjacent numbers, treat with care |\n| **Northflank** | Kata + Cloud Hypervisor/Firecracker microVMs (also gVisor) | ~1–2 s to full readiness | per-second: ~$0.0167/vCPU-hr, ~$0.0083/GB-hr; BYOC option | [northflank.com/product/sandboxes](https://northflank.com/product/sandboxes), [their pricing blog](https://northflank.com/blog/aws-lambda-microvms-vs-northflank-pricing) — vendor numbers |\n\nPattern to note: **every hosted provider converged on the same shape you're trying to build** — snapshot/suspend idle sandboxes to storage (billed at ~$0.15/GB-mo-ish), resume in 0.1–1 s, bill CPU/RAM per-second only while awake.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line: recommendations by scenario\n\n**Fleet of per-user agents, one VM, idle 95%+:**\n\n1. **Same trust domain (agents are yours/one org's), any cloud VM:** hardened Docker/Podman (userns, cap-drop, seccomp, pids-limit, memory caps) + **freeze + zram/zswap + `memory.reclaim`** for idle agents. Idle ≈ 0 CPU, RAM compressed 2–3x or paged to disk; resume effectively instant. Isolation: container-grade (kernel-shared) — accept this only within one trust domain. Lowest complexity of all options.\n2. **Untrusted/multi-tenant, but no KVM available (typical AWS non-metal VM):** **gVisor (`--runtime=runsc`)** on top of option 1's resource tricks, or gVisor checkpoint-to-disk for long-idle agents. ~30 MB/sandbox overhead, 0–30% syscall tax, dramatically smaller kernel attack surface, no hardware requirements. This is the best isolation-per-ops-dollar on commodity VMs.\n3. **Untrusted/multi-tenant and you have KVM** (bare metal, Hetzner/OVH, GCP/Azure nested, new AWS C8i/M8i/R8i nested): **Firecracker with snapshot/restore per agent** — true zero idle footprint, ~25–500 ms resume, VM-grade isolation. Budget real engineering time for images, networking, snapshot hygiene (entropy!), and orchestration; or use Kata to soften the ops burden at ~50–130 MB/pod overhead.\n4. **Don't want to run it at all:** Fly Machines (suspend/resume, storage-only idle billing) or E2B (pause/resume) match the requirement almost exactly.\n\n**Confidence notes / unverified items:** per-instance memory figures for gVisor (~30 MB) and Kata (~50–200 MB) come from third-party benchmarks, not official docs, and vary with config; Daytona's 27–90 ms and Northflank's pricing are vendor or vendor-adjacent claims; the 2026 AWS nested-virt launch is very recent and its practical availability/limits for Firecracker workloads is not yet well documented; the freeze+zram composite pattern's numbers are engineering estimates from the underlying primitives, not a published benchmark."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a619f62775c693bf5 (use SendMessage with to: 'a619f62775c693bf5', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 61170\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 333350</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"3dfac175-fc3d-4fd1-a4aa-55d7f477a37d","timestamp":"2026-07-02T14:45:56.617Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively to research the CURRENT (2025-2026) state of lightweight isolation/sandboxing technologies for running many untrusted or semi-trusted workloads (AI agents) on a single Linux VM, minimizing per-workload resource cost while keeping strong isolation.\n\nCover, with concrete facts and numbers where available (memory overhead per instance, cold-start time, security model, maturity, operational complexity):\n1. gVisor (runsc) — overhead, syscall interception model, Docker integration (runtime class), known limitations.\n2. Firecracker microVMs — memory footprint (~5MB claim?), boot time, snapshot/restore, what's needed to run it (KVM, bare metal vs nested virt — IMPORTANT: does it work on typical cloud VMs without nested virtualization?), tooling (firecracker-containerd, Kata with FC, flintlock, etc.).\n3. Kata Containers — overhead vs gVisor/FC, requirements.\n4. nsjail / bubblewrap / plain Linux namespaces + seccomp + cgroups v2 — how much isolation do you really get, when is it \"good enough\", known escape risks.\n5. Docker hardening as a middle path: userns-remap/rootless, seccomp/AppArmor profiles, read-only rootfs, cgroup CPU/memory quotas, pids-limit, no-new-privileges — how far this gets you for AI-agent workloads that run arbitrary shell commands.\n6. Checkpoint/restore (CRIU), docker pause, and memory-swap/zram tricks for keeping many idle containers cheap.\n7. Hosted sandbox services relevant to AI agents: E2B, Modal, Fly.io Machines (scale-to-zero, suspend/resume), Cloudflare Sandboxes/Containers, Daytona, Northflank — pricing model shape and cold-start characteristics.\n\nKey question to answer: for a fleet of per-user AI agents that are IDLE 95%+ of the time on one VM, what are the best options to make idle agents cost ~0 RAM/CPU while resuming fast, and what isolation level does each option give?\n\nBe honest about uncertainty — if you can't verify a number, say so. Return a structured report in English with sources (URLs) per claim. Your final message IS the deliverable — return the full report text.","agentId":"a619f62775c693bf5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Lightweight Isolation for Many Mostly-Idle AI Agents on One Linux VM — State of the Art, 2025–2026\n\n**TL;DR for the key question:** If your agents are idle 95%+ of the time on one VM, the two winning patterns are (a) **hardened containers + cgroup-v2 freeze + swap/zswap reclaim** (near-zero CPU, RAM pushed to compressed swap, ~instant resume, but shared-kernel isolation), and (b) **microVM or sandbox snapshot/restore to disk** (true zero RAM/CPU when idle, sub-second resume, VM-grade isolation — but on most clouds this requires nested virtualization or bare metal, which is the single biggest practical constraint). gVisor's own checkpoint/restore is an underrated middle path: stronger-than-container isolation, works on any VM without KVM, and idle state can be written to disk.\n\n---\n\n## 1. gVisor (runsc)\n\n**Model.** gVisor interposes a userspace \"application kernel\" (Sentry, written in Go) between the workload and the host. Every syscall is intercepted — via the default **systrap** platform (seccomp + signals) or KVM — and re-implemented in userspace, so most syscalls never reach the host kernel. The host attack surface is reduced to the small set of syscalls Sentry itself makes (further confined by seccomp) ([gvisor.dev performance guide](https://gvisor.dev/docs/architecture_guide/performance/), [Northflank: What is gVisor](https://northflank.com/blog/what-is-gvisor)).\n\n**Overhead.**\n- Memory: commonly cited at **~15–35 MB per sandbox** for the Sentry (third-party comparisons say ~30 MB/pod; I could not find an official per-sandbox number from Google, so treat this as approximate) ([onidel comparison](https://onidel.com/blog/gvisor-kata-firecracker-2025), [Northflank comparison](https://northflank.com/blog/kata-containers-vs-gvisor)).\n- CPU/syscall: raw syscall microbenchmarks are ~2–3x slower than native; realistic workloads see **~0–30% overhead**, dominated by syscall frequency. Google reports the majority of their internal workloads see <3% ([gvisor.dev](https://gvisor.dev/docs/architecture_guide/performance/), [systemshardening.com](https://www.systemshardening.com/articles/linux/linux-container-runtime-alternatives/)). I/O-heavy workloads (compiles, npm installs — i.e., exactly what coding agents do) sit at the bad end of that range.\n- Startup: ~50–100 ms typical, essentially container-like ([onidel](https://onidel.com/blog/gvisor-kata-firecracker-2025)).\n\n**Docker integration.** Trivially good: install `runsc`, add it as a runtime in `/etc/docker/daemon.json`, then `docker run --runtime=runsc ...`. This is the lowest-friction \"real\" isolation upgrade that exists ([gvisor.dev production guide](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/production/)).\n\n**Limitations.** Of 351 amd64 syscalls, ~277 are fully/partially implemented, **74 unsupported** ([gVisor compatibility table](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/compatibility/linux/amd64/)). Things that occasionally break: exotic ptrace users, some io_uring, certain /proc and netlink features, FUSE edge cases, some eBPF. For a typical AI-agent workload (shell, git, python, node, network calls) compatibility is generally fine — Modal runs its entire sandbox product on gVisor at 20k+ concurrent containers ([Amplify: Behind the scenes of Modal sandboxes](https://www.amplifypartners.com/blog-posts/behind-the-scenes-of-modal-sandboxes)).\n\n**Bonus for your use case:** gVisor has **native checkpoint/restore** (`runsc checkpoint --image-path ... / runsc restore`), including async lazy page loading, and works without KVM — i.e., on any cheap cloud VM. Caveats: it's wired to raw runsc commands, not cleanly exposed through Docker (`--checkpoint-dir` not supported by Docker), and CPU-feature matching matters across hosts ([gVisor checkpoint docs](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/checkpoint_restore/), [containerd issue #12280](https://github.com/containerd/containerd/issues/12280)).\n\n**Maturity:** very high (Google-internal use, GKE Sandbox, App Engine). Operational complexity: low.\n\n## 2. Firecracker microVMs\n\n**Numbers.**\n- VMM overhead: **< 5 MiB per microVM** (official claim — note this is the *VMM's* overhead; the guest still needs its own kernel + userspace RAM, realistically 50–128+ MB per agent guest) ([firecracker-microvm.github.io](https://firecracker-microvm.github.io/), [NSDI'20 paper](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/nsdi20-paper-agache.pdf)).\n- Boot: **~125 ms** to guest userspace code officially; snapshot-restore gets you to **~25–200 ms** (one practitioner writeup: 28 ms; AWS Lambda SnapStart is built on this) ([firecracker repo](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker), [dev.to 28ms sandboxes](https://dev.to/adwitiya/how-i-built-sandboxes-that-boot-in-28ms-using-firecracker-snapshots-i0k)).\n- Density: designed for thousands of microVMs per host (Lambda-scale) ([Marc Brooker, \"Seven Years of Firecracker\"](https://brooker.co.za/blog/2025/09/18/firecracker.html)).\n\n**Security model.** Hardware virtualization (KVM) — separate guest kernel, minimal device model (virtio-net, virtio-block, few others), VMM written in Rust, typically wrapped in a jailer (namespaces + seccomp around the VMM itself). This is the gold standard for multi-tenant untrusted code (AWS Lambda/Fargate, E2B, Fly.io).\n\n**The critical constraint — KVM availability.** Firecracker requires `/dev/kvm`. On a typical cloud VM this means **nested virtualization**, and the support matrix is the deciding factor:\n- **AWS EC2:** historically no nested virt on regular instances — you needed `*.metal` (~expensive, large). In **early 2026 AWS announced limited nested-virtualization availability on C8i/M8i/R8i** — new and not yet broadly available; verify region/instance support before depending on it ([InfoQ, Mar 2026](https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/03/aws-ec2-nested-virtualization/), [firecracker dev-machine-setup](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/dev-machine-setup.md)).\n- **GCP:** nested virt supported on Intel VMs (enable a flag); works, with a documented **~10%+ perf penalty** for nested guests ([alexellis.io](https://blog.alexellis.io/how-to-run-firecracker-without-kvm-on-regular-cloud-vms/)).\n- **Azure:** supported on many v3+ series. **DigitalOcean, Hetzner, OVH, Vultr (bare-metal/некоторые plans):** generally yes — dedicated/bare-metal providers are the usual home for self-hosted Firecracker fleets. E2B's self-host docs historically targeted GCP for exactly this reason.\n- So: **on many \"typical\" cloud VMs (especially AWS non-metal), Firecracker simply won't run.** This is the #1 reason teams fall back to gVisor or hardened containers.\n\n**Snapshots.** Full VM snapshot/restore (memory + device state) is a first-class feature; diff snapshots and UFFD-based lazy page loading exist. Security caveat: **restoring the same snapshot more than once duplicates entropy, UUIDs, and cryptographic state**; Firecracker injects a VM Generation ID so Linux ≥5.18 reseeds its PRNG on resume, but application-level state (cached tokens, seeds) is your problem ([snapshot-support.md](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/snapshotting/snapshot-support.md), [random-for-clones.md](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/snapshotting/random-for-clones.md)).\n\n**Tooling.** This is Firecracker's weak spot — it's a VMM, not a platform. Options: **firecracker-containerd** (AWS project; maintained but niche, notable ops complexity — devmapper snapshotter etc.), **Kata Containers with the FC hypervisor** (works but devmapper requirement is a recurring pain point — see [kata issue #12558](https://github.com/kata-containers/kata-containers/issues/12558)), **flintlock/Liquid Metal** (community-owned since Weaveworks' collapse; alive but low-momentum) ([flintlock repo](https://github.com/liquidmetal-dev/flintlock)), or rolling your own against the REST API (what E2B, Fly, and most sandbox startups did). Expect to own guest kernel builds, rootfs images, and CNI networking yourself ([some-natalie: \"Please stop saying just use Firecracker\"](https://some-natalie.dev/blog/stop-saying-just-use-firecracker/)).\n\n## 3. Kata Containers\n\nKata runs each container (or pod) in a lightweight VM with a standard OCI/CRI interface — the \"managed\" way to get VM isolation. Overhead depends on hypervisor backend: **QEMU ~130–200 MB/pod, Cloud Hypervisor ~130 MB, Firecracker backend ~50 MB** (third-party benchmark figures; Kata's own default pod overhead in K8s is typically declared as ~130–160 MB RuntimeClass overhead) ([Northflank comparison](https://northflank.com/blog/kata-containers-vs-firecracker-vs-gvisor), [container-runtime-benchmarks](https://github.com/bikramkgupta/container-runtime-benchmarks)). Startup ~150–300 ms+; near-native CPU/IO once running (no syscall tax — syscalls hit the *guest* kernel natively). Requires KVM, same nested-virt constraint as Firecracker. Best when you're already on Kubernetes/containerd and want RuntimeClass-level choice; heavier and more moving parts than either gVisor or raw FC. Actively maintained (OpenInfra Foundation; used by AWS for K8s isolation guidance) ([AWS blog](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/containers/enhancing-kubernetes-workload-isolation-and-security-using-kata-containers/)).\n\n## 4. nsjail / bubblewrap / raw namespaces + seccomp + cgroups v2\n\n**What you get.** All of these compose the same kernel primitives: user/PID/mount/net/IPC namespaces, seccomp-BPF syscall filters, rlimits, cgroups. nsjail (Google) adds the Kafel BPF language and is battle-tested for CTFs and code-judging platforms; bubblewrap (Flatpak's engine) is setuid-less and ubiquitous. Seccomp is the real security layer: a process that can only call a small syscall whitelist can't reach most kernel bug surfaces ([google/nsjail](https://github.com/google/nsjail), [morphllm nsjail writeup](https://www.morphllm.com/nsjail-sandbox), [Windmill's isolation docs](https://www.windmill.dev/docs/advanced/security_isolation)).\n\n**Honest assessment.** You still share the host kernel. Isolation quality is proportional to how tight your seccomp profile is — and AI agents that run arbitrary shell commands need a *broad* syscall surface (fork/exec, sockets, mount-ish operations for package managers), which forces you to leave most of the kernel exposed. Known risk classes: kernel LPE bugs reachable through allowed syscalls (Dirty Pipe-class), **unprivileged user namespaces as an attack-surface multiplier** (Qualys demonstrated bypasses of Ubuntu's userns restrictions in 2025; multiple 2025–2026 CVEs leaned on userns) ([search summary; see also container-security.site breakout list](https://www.container-security.site/attackers/container_breakout_vulnerabilities.html)).\n\n**When it's \"good enough\":** semi-trusted workloads (your own agents doing dumb things, not adversarial tenants), defense-in-depth inside a VM you already own, or short-lived evaluation of code where you can afford a tight syscall whitelist. Not good enough as the *sole* boundary between mutually distrusting customers. Overhead is effectively **zero** — this is the cheapest option per instance by far.\n\n## 5. Hardened Docker as the middle path\n\nThe standard hardening stack for agent workloads: **userns-remap or rootless mode** (container root ≠ host root), `--cap-drop=ALL`, `--security-opt no-new-privileges`, default-or-tighter **seccomp** profile (Docker's default blocks ~40+ dangerous syscalls), AppArmor, `--read-only` rootfs + tmpfs for scratch, `--pids-limit`, cgroup CPU/memory quotas, no docker.sock mount, egress firewalling ([virtua.cloud hardening guide](https://www.virtua.cloud/learn/en/tutorials/docker-security-hardening-rootless-seccomp), [Ken Muse on rootless trade-offs](https://www.kenmuse.com/blog/rootless-docker-and-its-hidden-security-trade-offs/), [dev.to: sandboxing AI coding agents](https://dev.to/alanwest/how-to-sandbox-ai-coding-agents-without-crippling-them-116c)).\n\n**How far it gets you:** quite far against *accidents and opportunistic malware* — an agent that `rm -rf`s, forks-bombs, or curl-pipes something nasty is well contained. What it does not stop: a determined attacker with a kernel 0-day (shared kernel = one bug owns every container on the host), and runtime bugs like **runc CVE-2024-21626 \"Leaky Vessels\"** (container escape via fd leak, patched in runc 1.1.12) recur every couple of years ([Snyk Leaky Vessels](https://labs.snyk.io/resources/leaky-vessels-docker-runc-container-breakout-vulnerabilities/), [Wiz](https://www.wiz.io/blog/leaky-vessels-container-escape-vulnerabilities)). Notably, **Anthropic's and OpenAI's own coding-agent sandboxes are namespace/seccomp-based OS sandboxes, not VMs** — the industry treats this level as acceptable for agents acting on behalf of the same user whose machine it is, but hosted multi-tenant products (Modal, E2B, Fly) all chose gVisor or microVMs instead ([Medium: How Claude Code and Codex sandbox untrusted code](https://medium.com/@Koukyosyumei/how-claude-code-and-codex-sandbox-untrusted-code-ba39b493046a)). That split is the clearest signal of where the line is.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** hardened Docker is the right boundary *within one trust domain* (all agents belong to you/one org). Between paying tenants or truly adversarial code, add gVisor or a VM.\n\n## 6. Making idle agents cost ~0: pause, CRIU, swap tricks, snapshots\n\nThis is the crux for a 95%-idle fleet. Options, cheapest-to-resume first:\n\n| Technique | Idle CPU | Idle RAM | Resume | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `docker pause` (cgroup freezer / `cgroup.freeze`) | 0 | **full RAM retained** | ~instant (<10 ms) | Kernel freezes the process tree; memory stays resident ([oneuptime pause guide](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-08-how-to-use-docker-pause-for-container-freeze/view)) |\n| pause + **swap/zswap/zram + `memory.reclaim`** | 0 | ~0 resident (pages compressed in RAM via zram, or pushed to disk swap/zswap) | ms–seconds (page-fault-driven, gradual) | cgroup v2 `memory.reclaim` lets you *proactively* force a specific idle container's pages out (`echo 1G > memory.reclaim`); zswap now has per-cgroup accounting and writeback control ([kernel cgroup-v2 docs](https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html), [LWN: zswap cgroup accounting](https://lwn.net/Articles/892839/)) |\n| **CRIU checkpoint** (`docker checkpoint`, Podman, or containerd) | 0 | **0** (state on disk) | ~100 ms–seconds, depends on image size | True zero-footprint. Fragile spots: TCP connection restore, GPU state, some fd types; `docker checkpoint` is still marked experimental; Podman support is better. Checkpoint images can contain secrets — encrypt at rest ([criu.org](https://www.criu.org/Main_Page), [docker checkpoint docs](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/checkpoint/), [DevZero CRIU writeup](https://www.devzero.io/blog/checkpoint-restore-with-criu)) |\n| **gVisor checkpoint/restore** | 0 | 0 | sub-second, async lazy page load supported | Same idea as CRIU but the Sentry checkpoints itself — much more robust than CRIU-on-arbitrary-processes because the \"kernel\" state is fully owned in userspace. Needs raw runsc orchestration ([gVisor C/R docs](https://gvisor.dev/docs/user_guide/checkpoint_restore/)) |\n| **Firecracker/Kata VM snapshot to disk** | 0 | 0 | ~25–500 ms (UFFD lazy loading makes it fast) | Strongest isolation + zero idle cost; needs KVM; entropy-duplication caveat on repeated restores ([FC snapshot docs](https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/snapshotting/snapshot-support.md)) |\n\nPractical composite that works well for a single-VM fleet of container agents: run each agent hardened + memory-capped; on idle, freeze the cgroup and `memory.reclaim` its pages into **zram** (compressed RAM, typically 2–3:1 for agent heaps) or disk swap with zswap in front; on an inbound message, unfreeze — the process faults pages back in transparently. Idle cost drops to roughly (RSS ÷ compression ratio) with zram, or near-zero RAM with disk swap, and resume is effectively instant for the first response with a short warm-up tail. This requires no snapshot tooling and no process-restore fragility. (Assessment mine; the primitives are documented in the kernel cgroup-v2 docs above. I did not find a published end-to-end benchmark of exactly this pattern — treat the compression ratio and warm-up figures as typical rather than verified.)\n\n## 7. Hosted sandbox services (for calibration or offloading)\n\n| Service | Isolation | Cold start / resume | Idle cost model | Sources |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **E2B** | Firecracker microVM, dedicated kernel | ~150 ms create (reported 78 ms p50 as of early 2026); pause ≈ 4 s/GiB, resume ≈ 1 s | Per-second while running (~$0.05/vCPU-hr + $0.016/GiB-hr) + monthly plan ($0 hobby/$150 Pro); paused sandboxes bill storage only | [e2b.dev/pricing](https://e2b.dev/pricing), [persistence docs](https://e2b.dev/docs/sandbox/persistence), [Beam pricing analysis](https://www.beam.cloud/blog/e2b-pricing-explained) |\n| **Modal Sandboxes** | gVisor | sub-second; memory snapshots give 3–10x faster cold boots (feature still maturing) | Pure usage-based; sandboxes at a premium CPU rate (~$0.0000394/core-s ≈ $0.14/core-hr, mem ~$0.024/GiB-hr); auto-shutdown on idle | [Modal memory snapshots](https://modal.com/docs/guide/memory-snapshots), [sandboxes docs](https://modal.com/docs/guide/sandboxes), [Blaxel pricing guide](https://blaxel.ai/blog/modal-pricing-alternatives-guide) |\n| **Fly.io Machines** | Firecracker, one VM per machine | cold start ~2 s+; **suspend/resume: a few hundred ms** | Stopped/suspended = rootfs storage only, **$0.15/GB-month**, zero CPU/RAM billing. Suspend limits: ≤2 GB RAM, no GPU/swap | [fly.io suspend-resume](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/), [pricing](https://fly.io/docs/about/pricing/) |\n| **Cloudflare Sandboxes/Containers** | container on Cloudflare's edge (Workers-adjacent) | \"milliseconds\" claimed; sleeps after idle (default 10 min) and **loses state on wake** (fresh container) unless using newer persistence features | Active-usage billing (CPU $0.00002/vCPU-s since Nov 2025); pay only while awake | [CF Sandbox pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/platform/pricing/), [lifecycle docs](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/concepts/sandboxes/), [InfoQ GA note](https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/04/cloudflare-sandboxes-ga/) |\n| **Daytona** | container-based (OCI images), not microVM | ~90 ms, some configs ~27 ms claimed | usage-based; enterprise pricing less transparent | [Superagent benchmark](https://www.superagent.sh/blog/ai-code-sandbox-benchmark-2026), [Medium sub-90ms piece](https://medium.com/@kacperwlodarczyk/sub-90ms-cloud-code-execution-how-daytona-replaced-docker-in-our-ai-agent-stack-b6f343e4e547) — vendor-adjacent numbers, treat with care |\n| **Northflank** | Kata + Cloud Hypervisor/Firecracker microVMs (also gVisor) | ~1–2 s to full readiness | per-second: ~$0.0167/vCPU-hr, ~$0.0083/GB-hr; BYOC option | [northflank.com/product/sandboxes](https://northflank.com/product/sandboxes), [their pricing blog](https://northflank.com/blog/aws-lambda-microvms-vs-northflank-pricing) — vendor numbers |\n\nPattern to note: **every hosted provider converged on the same shape you're trying to build** — snapshot/suspend idle sandboxes to storage (billed at ~$0.15/GB-mo-ish), resume in 0.1–1 s, bill CPU/RAM per-second only while awake.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line: recommendations by scenario\n\n**Fleet of per-user agents, one VM, idle 95%+:**\n\n1. **Same trust domain (agents are yours/one org's), any cloud VM:** hardened Docker/Podman (userns, cap-drop, seccomp, pids-limit, memory caps) + **freeze + zram/zswap + `memory.reclaim`** for idle agents. Idle ≈ 0 CPU, RAM compressed 2–3x or paged to disk; resume effectively instant. Isolation: container-grade (kernel-shared) — accept this only within one trust domain. Lowest complexity of all options.\n2. **Untrusted/multi-tenant, but no KVM available (typical AWS non-metal VM):** **gVisor (`--runtime=runsc`)** on top of option 1's resource tricks, or gVisor checkpoint-to-disk for long-idle agents. ~30 MB/sandbox overhead, 0–30% syscall tax, dramatically smaller kernel attack surface, no hardware requirements. This is the best isolation-per-ops-dollar on commodity VMs.\n3. **Untrusted/multi-tenant and you have KVM** (bare metal, Hetzner/OVH, GCP/Azure nested, new AWS C8i/M8i/R8i nested): **Firecracker with snapshot/restore per agent** — true zero idle footprint, ~25–500 ms resume, VM-grade isolation. Budget real engineering time for images, networking, snapshot hygiene (entropy!), and orchestration; or use Kata to soften the ops burden at ~50–130 MB/pod overhead.\n4. **Don't want to run it at all:** Fly Machines (suspend/resume, storage-only idle billing) or E2B (pause/resume) match the requirement almost exactly.\n\n**Confidence notes / unverified items:** per-instance memory figures for gVisor (~30 MB) and Kata (~50–200 MB) come from third-party benchmarks, not official docs, and vary with config; Daytona's 27–90 ms and Northflank's pricing are vendor or vendor-adjacent claims; the 2026 AWS nested-virt launch is very recent and its practical availability/limits for Firecracker workloads is not yet well documented; the freeze+zram composite pattern's numbers are engineering estimates from the underlying primitives, not a published benchmark."}],"resolvedModel":"claude-fable-5","totalDurationMs":333350,"totalTokens":61170,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_i