{"type":"ai-title","aiTitle":"בדיקת פונקציונליות של מודל חדש","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da"}
{"parentUuid":"e357e07d-25f0-4c8d-8a65-e864595aa66b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a96b1813-8540-42d9-9d31-897bfe659ff9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012tCh6dx9bouPezaKL9tXh5","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Multi-Tenant Agent Hosting: 2025–2026 Best Practices Research Report\n\nScope: a free pilot where each signup provisions a persistent, personal AI agent (currently: one always-on Docker container per user on a single VM), reached via a dedicated Telegram bot. Findings below are organized per your six questions, with sources per claim and explicit uncertainty notes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scale-to-zero patterns for per-user workloads\n\n**The consensus 2025–2026 pattern:** don't keep per-user processes running; keep per-user *state* durable and cheap, and attach compute only when a message arrives. Every major platform has converged on some form of this.\n\n### Activation-on-message (queue in front, container started on demand)\n- The self-hosted version of this is exactly what **Sablier** implements: a reverse-proxy middleware (Traefik/Nginx/Caddy) that starts a Docker container on the first incoming request and stops it after inactivity. It also has a \"scale mode\" that throttles CPU/memory instead of stopping, avoiding cold starts entirely. Works with plain Docker, Swarm, Podman, K8s, Proxmox LXC. ([github.com/sablierapp/sablier](https://github.com/sablierapp/sablier), [sablierapp.dev](https://sablierapp.dev/), [production-ready.de writeup](https://www.production-ready.de/2023/08/20/docker-scale-to-zero-with-traefik-sablier-en.html))\n- On Kubernetes, **KEDA** scales deployments to zero and wakes them on queue depth or HTTP events (HTTP add-on still beta). ([Google Cloud blog](https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/scale-to-zero-on-gke-with-keda/), [GKE tutorial](https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/scale-to-zero-using-keda))\n- For your Telegram shape specifically, the queue-in-front pattern is natural: a single always-on lightweight gateway receives all webhook updates, enqueues them per-user, and a supervisor `docker start`s (or unpauses) the user's container, delivers the queued messages, and stops it after N minutes idle. Sablier is HTTP-oriented, so for message-driven wake you'd likely write a small supervisor yourself (this is a modest amount of code: Docker API `start`/`stop` + a per-user queue with a debounce window).\n\n### Fly.io Machines: suspend/resume — fast, and state is preserved\n- Suspend saves the Machine's **full memory state** to persistent storage (no volume needed). Resume is \"**a few hundred ms**\" vs ~2+ seconds for a cold start of a typical app. ([fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/))\n- Hard limits that matter for you: suspendable Machines must have **≤ 2 GB RAM**, no swap, no GPU. Snapshots are discarded on redeploy (new code ⇒ cold boot, rootfs preserved). Network connections go stale across resume — your agent runtime must reconnect; clock lags a few seconds until NTP resync. ([same doc](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/))\n- Fly also has plain autostop/autostart (stop on idle, start on request via their proxy) and \"Power Pause\". ([fly.io/docs/launch/autostop-autostart](https://fly.io/docs/launch/autostop-autostart/), [Power Pause blog](https://fly.io/blog/fly-now-with-power-pause/))\n- Economics: **stopped/suspended Machines cost only rootfs storage — $0.15/GB/month**. So 1,000 idle per-user agents with 1 GB rootfs ≈ $150/mo idle cost plus compute only while awake. This is arguably the closest managed analog to your current architecture (one persistent container per user) with scale-to-zero bolted on. ([fly.io/docs/about/pricing](https://fly.io/docs/about/pricing/), [community announcement of rootfs charges](https://community.fly.io/t/we-are-going-to-start-collecting-charges-for-stopped-machines-rootfs-starting-april-25th/17825))\n\n### Cloudflare Durable Objects / Agents SDK\n- A Cloudflare Agent literally *is* a Durable Object: one addressable, single-threaded actor per user with its own embedded SQLite, WebSockets, and alarms/scheduler. It hibernates when idle (**zero compute cost while inactive**), and the platform wakes it on HTTP request, WebSocket message, alarm, or email. Marginal cost of \"one agent per user\" is ~zero; millions of instances are the intended usage. ([developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/), [blog.cloudflare.com/project-think](https://blog.cloudflare.com/project-think/), [agents.cloudflare.com](https://agents.cloudflare.com/), [truvisory analysis](https://truvisory.com/agents-mcp/agents-sdk-durable-objects/))\n- Constraint: DOs run JS/WASM in isolates — you can't run an arbitrary Node.js Docker image inside one. **Cloudflare Containers** (public beta since June 2025) closes that gap: each unique ID gets a container instance routed via a DO, which boots on first fetch and sleeps after a configurable `sleepAfter` timeout; billing stops while asleep (memory $0.0000025/GiB-s; requires $5/mo Workers Paid). Note: sleeping a Cloudflare Container loses memory state (it's stop/start, not suspend) — persistent state must live in the DO's SQLite or external storage. ([blog.cloudflare.com containers beta](https://blog.cloudflare.com/containers-are-available-in-public-beta-for-simple-global-and-programmable/), [containers pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/pricing/), [devclass coverage](https://devclass.com/2025/07/01/cloudflare-container-platform-in-public-preview-with-scale-to-zero-pricing-some-initial-limitations/))\n\n### Modal\n- Modal Sandboxes support **memory snapshots**: full state (filesystem + entire process tree memory) checkpointed and restored as an exact clone. Restores are ~2.5x faster than cold container start; init-heavy workloads see 3–10x. Introduced Jan 2025. Caveats: snapshots **expire after 7 days**, and restore requires the same instance type, which can cause scheduling delays. ([modal.com/docs/guide/memory-snapshots](https://modal.com/docs/guide/memory-snapshots), [sandbox snapshots](https://modal.com/docs/guide/sandbox-snapshots), [blog](https://modal.com/blog/mem-snapshots)) The 7-day expiry makes \"resume a user's agent after 3 idle weeks\" awkward unless you treat snapshots as a warm-start optimization over durable state, not the source of truth.\n\n### AWS Fargate / Lambda for this shape\n- **Fargate does not scale to zero** — a bot-per-user fleet of Fargate tasks reproduces your exact cost problem ($48–95/month-region per always-on minimum task). **Lambda scales to zero and bills per invocation**, but it's stateless with a 15-minute max execution — fine for \"wake, load state, respond, exit\" agents, poor for long agentic runs or in-memory continuity. Break-even analyses put Lambda ahead for spiky/infrequent traffic (your idle-most-of-the-time profile) and Fargate ahead for sustained load. ([AWS decision guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/decision-guides/latest/fargate-or-lambda/fargate-or-lambda.html), [CloudZero](https://www.cloudzero.com/blog/fargate-vs-lambda/), [LeanOps break-even analysis](https://leanopstech.com/blog/aws-lambda-vs-fargate-cost-breakeven-2026/)) AWS's own answer for persistent agents is Bedrock AgentCore-style managed runtimes rather than raw Fargate ([Medium overview](https://medium.com/@loic.labeye/lambda-fargate-agentcore-which-severless-option-to-choose-for-your-agentic-pattern-aaf68cb99700)) — uncertainty flag: I did not deep-verify AgentCore specifics in this pass.\n\n**Bottom line for #1:** cheapest-to-adopt for your current design is (a) self-hosted wake-on-message (Sablier-style supervisor + queue) on your existing VM, or (b) lift the per-user container to Fly Machines with autostop/suspend (~$0.15/GB/mo idle, sub-second-ish resume). The deeper rearchitecture is the actor model (next section).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The actor model: state-as-data + shared worker pool\n\nThis is the pattern with the strongest 2025–2026 momentum, and it directly attacks your bottleneck: **the agent is a row/file, not a process.**\n\n- **Letta** (ex-MemGPT) is the canonical \"agents as data\" precedent: a single stateful server owns all agent state in PostgreSQL — memory blocks, message history, tool state. Agents don't run as processes; the server hydrates an agent's context on each interaction, runs the LLM loop, persists, and returns. Thousands of \"agents\" are just DB rows; compute is a shared pool. ([docs.letta.com/core-concepts](https://docs.letta.com/core-concepts/), [stateful agents overview](https://docs.letta.com/guides/agents/overview/), [architecture writeup](https://sudoall.com/letta-stateful-agents-nodejs/))\n- **Cloudflare Durable Objects** — virtual-actor semantics: single-writer per actor (no concurrent-write corruption), state survives restarts/deploys, zero idle cost, wake-on-event. ([developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/))\n- **Rivet Actors** — explicitly markets \"one actor per agent/session/user\": long-lived lightweight processes, in-memory state with automatic persistence, actors sleep on inactivity and wake on request/message/alarm, self-hostable or managed; the docs explicitly cite **Microsoft Orleans' virtual actor pattern** as the lineage. ([rivet.dev/actors](https://rivet.dev/actors/), [github.com/rivet-dev/rivet](https://github.com/rivet-dev/rivet), [rivet.dev/agent](https://www.rivet.dev/agent/))\n- Orleans itself established the core guarantees you'd want: actors \"always exist\" logically, are activated on demand, deactivated when idle, and the runtime handles placement — the caller never knows whether the actor was resident.\n\n**Known pitfalls of the shared-pool/state-as-data approach** (from these sources plus multi-tenant OpenClaw write-ups):\n1. **Tenant isolation becomes your job.** In a shared runtime, every file write, tool call, and cron job must be tenant-scoped; one plugin writing to a shared path is a cross-tenant data leak. Practical mitigation seen in the wild: root all FS ops at `/workspace/<tenant_id>/` with path-traversal guards. ([KinthAI on OpenClaw multi-tenancy](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale), [dev.to multi-tenant agent platform](https://dev.to/agentbot/how-we-built-a-multi-tenant-ai-agent-platform-and-open-sourced-it-521g))\n2. **Single-writer discipline**: you must guarantee one activation per user at a time (two workers hydrating the same user = state corruption / duplicate replies). DOs/Orleans give you this for free; a DIY pool needs a per-user lock or queue.\n3. **Hydration cost**: if the agent's \"state\" includes a big filesystem (your NanoClaw-like agents have workspaces, installed packages, etc.), loading state on demand is much heavier than loading DB rows. This is the real fork in the road: *agents whose state is conversation + memory files* fit the actor model beautifully; *agents that accumulate arbitrary filesystem/container state* fit suspend/resume (Fly, Modal snapshots) better.\n4. **Noisy neighbors** in the shared pool: per-tenant CPU/memory/token quotas are required, not optional. ([KinthAI](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale))\n\nThe KinthAI analysis of exactly your architecture (\"one VM/container per OpenClaw user\") claims it breaks at **~30–50 users** operationally, costs $500–1,500/mo at 100 users at $5–15/VM, produces a fleet of stale unpatched installs, and estimates ~2 engineer-months to build real multi-tenancy instead. (Uncertainty: this is one vendor's blog with an obvious interest in that conclusion; the qualitative failure modes match other sources, but treat the numbers as illustrative.) ([blog.kinthai.ai](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale))\n\n---\n\n## 3. Telegram multi-tenant bot architecture\n\n### Bot-per-user vs one bot with routing\n- **Hard constraint on bot-per-user:** BotFather allows **20 bots per Telegram account (40 with Premium)**. Programmatic mass-creation of bots is not a supported API operation. A bot-per-user product therefore requires either users creating their own bots and pasting tokens into your signup flow (real friction, but gives each user a truly dedicated bot identity), or it simply doesn't scale on your own accounts. ([core.telegram.org/bots/faq](https://core.telegram.org/bots/faq), [tginfo limits tracker](https://github.com/tginfo/Telegram-Limits/issues/288))\n- The common scalable design is **one bot (or a small number), with per-chat routing** to the user's agent: `telegram:dm:<user_id> → agent X`, with tenant context carried via something like AsyncLocalStorage in a Node dispatcher. Multi-bot gateways (many tokens → one dispatcher, identified per-token) are the standard middle ground and are exactly what BYO-token supports. ([mudrava.com bot framework writeup](https://mudrava.com/en/now-every-new-bot-takes-three-minutes-instead-of-a-week/), [hermes-agent multi-tenant routing issues](https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/issues/9514), [BOTServer architectures](https://github.com/solyarisoftware/BOTServer/blob/master/wiki/architectures.md), [dev.to design patterns](https://dev.to/madhead/two-design-patterns-for-telegram-bots-59f5))\n- If you keep BYO-token bot-per-user: **webhooks let one server host unlimited bot tokens** (route by token in the webhook path). Long-polling per bot means one persistent `getUpdates` loop per token — and Telegram returns **409 Conflict** on concurrent getUpdates for the same token, so polling can't be horizontally scaled per bot. ([grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types](https://grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types), [gramio.dev/updates/webhook](https://gramio.dev/updates/webhook), [BOTServer](https://github.com/solyarisoftware/BOTServer/blob/master/wiki/architectures.md))\n\n### Webhook vs long-polling at scale\n- Consensus: polling is fine for dev and single low-traffic bots; **webhooks win at scale** (no wasted requests, lower latency, one endpoint for many bots, works with scale-to-zero since Telegram pushes to you — critically, *polling requires an always-on process, which defeats your scale-to-zero goal*). ([grammy.dev](https://grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types), [gramio.dev](https://gramio.dev/updates/webhook), [hostman tutorial](https://hostman.com/tutorials/difference-between-polling-and-webhook-in-telegram-bots/))\n\n### Idempotency, dedupe, debouncing\n- Telegram **retries webhook delivery** if your response is missing or ≥500, and ordering is not guaranteed under retries. Best practice: **ACK the webhook immediately (200) and process async**, dedupe by `update_id` (e.g., Redis SETNX with ~1h TTL), and make handlers idempotent. ([grammy.dev](https://grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types), [gramio.dev](https://gramio.dev/updates/webhook), [core.telegram.org/bots/faq](https://core.telegram.org/bots/faq))\n- For agent workloads specifically, **debounce**: users send bursts (\"wait\", \"actually also do X\"); queue updates per chat and flush to the agent after a short quiet window (1–3 s) or when the agent becomes free, delivering the batch as one turn. This also gives you the wake-on-message buffer for free: the queue *is* the wake signal. (This paragraph is synthesis from the routing/queueing sources above plus standard practice — no single canonical citation.)\n- Outbound rate limits (from Telegram's Bots FAQ): ~1 msg/sec per chat, ~30 msg/sec overall per bot — a shared single bot needs an outbound rate-limiter/queue; bot-per-user mostly sidesteps this. ([core.telegram.org/bots/faq](https://core.telegram.org/bots/faq))\n\n---\n\n## 4. Capacity math: idle Node.js agent containers per VM\n\n- Container runtime overhead is ~10 MB plus your app's RSS; a general density study puts ~220 MB-average containers at **400–500 practical per 128 GB host** (theoretical 560+). ([OneUptime containers-vs-VMs density deep dive](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-01-16-containers-vs-vms-density-efficiency-comparison/view))\n- Scaling that to your numbers (~150–400 MB RSS per Node agent, no dedup, no swap, leaving ~15–20% headroom for host + page cache):\n  - **8 GB VM:** ~15–40 idle agents\n  - **16 GB:** ~35–85\n  - **32 GB:** ~70–170\n  - **64 GB:** ~140–350\n  These are arithmetic estimates from the cited per-container figures, not benchmarks — CPU is rarely the binding constraint for *idle* agents; memory is.\n- **Density levers, in rough order of payoff:**\n  1. **Lazy start / stop-on-idle** (Sablier-style, section 1): the single biggest lever — idle agents cost ~0 RAM; capacity becomes a function of *concurrent active* users (often 1–5% of signups). ([sablierapp.dev](https://sablierapp.dev/))\n  2. **zram swap + relaxed overcommit** (`vm.overcommit_memory=1`): compressed in-RAM swap lets you overcommit with \"a negligible hit\" for rarely-touched pages — idle Node heaps compress well; lz4/zstd tradeoff documented. Commonly cited effective gains of ~1.5–2x on idle-heavy fleets, though exact ratios depend on workload (uncertainty: no rigorous Node-specific benchmark found). ([Debian zramswap manpage](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/zram-tools/zramswap.1.en.html), [zram+overcommit notes](https://copyninja.in/blog/zram_memory_overcommit.html), [systemd-zram-generator guide](https://dev.to/lyraalishaikh/stop-hitting-swap-too-late-practical-zram-on-linux-with-systemd-zram-generator-4m4j))\n  3. **Per-container memory limits + `--max-old-space-size`**: Node 20+ is cgroup-aware for max heap, but you should still set the V8 heap below the cgroup limit to get GC pressure instead of OOM-kills. ([Red Hat: Node.js 20+ memory in containers](https://developers.redhat.com/articles/2025/10/10/nodejs-20-memory-management-containers), [IBM article](https://developer.ibm.com/articles/nodejs-memory-management-in-container-environments/), [Node best practices repo](https://github.com/goldbergyoni/nodebestpractices/blob/master/sections/docker/memory-limit.md))\n  4. **KSM (kernel same-page merging)**: dedupes identical pages across identical runtimes; 5–10% CPU overhead for scanning. Two caveats: KSM only merges pages marked `MADV_MERGEABLE` (kernels ≥6.4 allow process-wide opt-in via `prctl(PR_SET_MEMORY_MERGE)`; wiring this into containers takes effort), and cross-tenant page dedup is a documented **side-channel risk** — reasonable within one trust domain, questionable across untrusting tenants. ([OneUptime KSM guide](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-03-04-configure-kernel-samepage-merging-ksm-for-memory-deduplication/view), [Proxmox KSM wiki noting security tradeoffs](https://pve.proxmox.com/wiki/Kernel_Samepage_Merging_(KSM)), [Wikipedia KSM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_same-page_merging))\n- Honest caveat: I found no published benchmark of specifically \"N idle Node.js agent containers per VM size\"; the figures above combine the OneUptime density study with your stated RSS range.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Provisioning-pipeline hardening for a public free signup\n\nFree compute + public signup is one of the most-abused combinations on the internet, and it's well documented:\n\n- **Documented abuse wave:** crypto-mining gangs systematically farmed free tiers of GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, TravisCI, CircleCI, LayerCI, Render, CodeShip, Sourcehut, Okteto, Azure — cycling new accounts as each free allotment ran out. **TravisCI and Sourcehut killed their free tiers over it**; LayerCI documented the arms race in \"Crypto miners are killing free CI.\" ([The Record](https://therecord.media/crypto-mining-gangs-are-running-amok-on-free-cloud-computing-platforms), [webapp.io/blog/crypto-miners-are-killing-free-ci](https://webapp.io/blog/crypto-miners-are-killing-free-ci), [The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/how-devops-sites-are-battling-cryptocurrency-miners/))\n- **GitLab's countermeasures** (a good template): CI-minute quotas applied to free namespaces, and **credit-card verification for free compute** (card validated, not charged) — the single measure they credit with cutting abuse most. ([about.gitlab.com/blog/prevent-crypto-mining-abuse](https://about.gitlab.com/blog/prevent-crypto-mining-abuse/), [The Register](https://www.theregister.com/2021/05/19/gitlab_crypto/))\n- Your agents are *general-purpose compute with network egress on your API bill*, so assume: miners, spam/phishing senders, proxies-for-hire, and LLM-credit farming (people extracting your Anthropic/OpenAI spend for free inference). The LLM-token-farming variant is newer; documented cases are thinner than crypto-mining — flagging that as inference, though the incentive structure is identical.\n\n**Layered checklist synthesized from the guidance:**\n1. **Signup friction ladder:** email verification (double opt-in) → block disposable-email domains → CAPTCHA (reCAPTCHA v3 / Turnstile invisible scoring) → phone or OAuth verification for suspicious cohorts → card-on-file only if abuse persists. ([UserCheck free-trial-abuse guide](https://www.usercheck.com/guides/how-to-prevent-free-trial-abuse), [payproglobal](https://payproglobal.com/how-to/prevent-free-trial-abuse/), [suped.com](https://www.suped.com/email-deliverability/answers/how-can-i-prevent-nefarious-email-signups-using-rate-limiting-recaptcha-and-double-opt-in))\n2. **Rate limits:** per-IP and per-ASN signup caps; flag multiple signups from one IP range; VPN/proxy detection scoring. ([sensfrx](https://blog.sensfrx.ai/free-trial-abuse/), [UserCheck](https://www.usercheck.com/guides/how-to-prevent-free-trial-abuse))\n3. **Runtime quotas per agent (this is where a Telegram-gated product is lucky):** a Telegram account is itself a phone-verified identity — bind one agent per Telegram user ID and much of the fake-signup surface disappears. Then cap per-user: LLM tokens/day, container CPU shares and memory, disk, and **egress** (crypto pools and spam need network; a default-deny or throttled egress policy is the highest-value single control — GitLab/LayerCI both note miners need sustained CPU + specific egress). ([webapp.io](https://webapp.io/blog/crypto-miners-are-killing-free-ci), [about.gitlab.com](https://about.gitlab.com/blog/prevent-crypto-mining-abuse/))\n4. **Detection + kill switches:** sustained-high-CPU alerts on \"idle\" agents (miner signature), per-agent instant suspend (`docker pause`/`stop` is your kill switch), global provisioning freeze flag, and a review queue for anomalous accounts. CircleCI describes running a dedicated abuse-detection function for exactly this. ([The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/how-devops-sites-are-battling-cryptocurrency-miners/))\n5. **Provision lazily:** don't build the container at signup — create the DB record, and materialize the container on first real message. This makes mass-signup attacks cost you ~nothing and doubles as your scale-to-zero onramp. (Synthesis; also the KinthAI onboarding-friction point argues signup-time heavy provisioning hurts conversion anyway — [blog.kinthai.ai](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale).)\n\n---\n\n## 6. The \"your own isolated server\" promise: honesty vs architecture\n\n**Current state of your product vs. the promise:** a Docker container on a shared VM is *not* \"a server that belongs only to you\" under any technically honest reading — all containers share one kernel, and the 2025–2026 sandboxing literature is blunt that namespace isolation is the *minimum viable* tier: one kernel exploit or mis-mounted socket exposes every tenant. ([emirb.github.io \"Your Container Is Not a Sandbox\"](https://emirb.github.io/blog/microvm-2026/), [Northflank isolation guide](https://northflank.com/blog/how-to-sandbox-ai-agents), [manveerc sandboxing guide](https://manveerc.substack.com/p/ai-agent-sandboxing-guide))\n\n**How vendors in this exact market phrase isolation — the observable norm is to name the mechanism, not use \"belongs to you\" language:**\n- **E2B:** \"each sandbox runs in a Firecracker microVM with a dedicated kernel\" — hardware-level isolation, named technology. ([Northflank comparison](https://northflank.com/blog/daytona-vs-e2b-ai-code-execution-sandboxes), [ZenML comparison](https://www.zenml.io/blog/e2b-vs-daytona))\n- **Daytona:** honestly positioned as Docker-with-shared-kernel by default, with harder options (Kata/Cloud Hypervisor, Sysbox) for stronger isolation — i.e., it discloses the tier. ([ZenML](https://www.zenml.io/blog/e2b-vs-daytona), [Spheron guide](https://www.spheron.network/blog/ai-agent-code-execution-sandbox-e2b-daytona-firecracker/))\n- **KiloClaw** (managed OpenClaw hosting — your product's closest comparable): \"each customer runs inside a dedicated Firecracker micro-VM… no shared kernel, filesystem, or process namespace between tenants.\" ([kilo.ai](https://kilo.ai/openclaw/managed-alternatives))\n- Commentary across the space explicitly warns to \"separate the marketing from the underlying isolation technology.\" ([shayon.dev \"Let's discuss sandbox isolation\"](https://www.shayon.dev/post/2026/52/lets-discuss-sandbox-isolation/), [Northflank \"What is a sandbox?\"](https://northflank.com/blog/what-is-a-sandbox-and-isolation-technologies))\n\n**Ways to honestly satisfy (or fix) the promise, cheapest first:**\n1. **Rewrite the promise** to match reality: \"your own isolated agent environment — dedicated container, private filesystem and memory, resource-isolated\" is defensible for Docker-per-user; \"your own server\" is not. Cheapest fix, zero infra work.\n2. **Dedicated microVM per user** (Firecracker/Kata/Fly Machines — Fly Machines *are* per-tenant microVMs): lets you truthfully say \"dedicated virtual machine with its own kernel,\" which is how E2B/KiloClaw phrase it. Combined with suspend/stop-on-idle this doesn't have to cost much more than containers. ([fly.io](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/), [emirb](https://emirb.github.io/blog/microvm-2026/))\n3. **\"Belongs only to you\" taken literally** implies dedicated hardware/VM — almost certainly not what a free pilot should promise. Note that if you go the actor-model/shared-pool route from §2, the promise becomes *less* true, not more — you'd need to reword toward \"private, isolated agent with its own memory and workspace.\"\n4. Legal-risk note, honestly flagged: I found **no** FTC enforcement action specifically about \"dedicated server\" isolation claims in agent/sandbox hosting; the marketing-vs-reality risk here is currently reputational/contractual (and generic deceptive-practices exposure) rather than a documented enforcement pattern. ([search corroboration: Northflank/community sources above; absence of FTC cases per search results])\n\n---\n\n## Synthesis: what the evidence points to for your shape\n\nTwo coherent end-states, both well-precedented in 2025–2026:\n\n- **Path A — keep container-per-user, make it lazy** (smallest delta): webhook gateway (always-on, tiny) → per-user message queue with `update_id` dedupe + debounce → supervisor starts the user's container on message, stops after idle; zram + memory limits on the VM; provision on first message, not at signup; Telegram-identity-anchored quotas + egress limits + CPU-anomaly kill switch. Later, swap the backend for Fly Machines (suspend/resume, $0.15/GB/mo idle) to also make the \"dedicated VM\" claim honest.\n- **Path B — actor model** (bigger rewrite, best economics at scale): agent state as DB rows/files (Letta-style) served by a small shared worker pool, or built directly on Durable Objects/Rivet-style actors — zero idle cost, but tenant isolation becomes application-level and the \"your own server\" promise must be rewritten.\n\nThe main genuine uncertainty across sources: real-world resume-latency numbers under load (Fly says \"few hundred ms\" but no independent benchmarks found), Node-specific zram density gains (no rigorous benchmark), and LLM-credit-farming abuse (incentive-obvious, thinly documented compared to crypto-mining)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: adb1ccdc79892a5da (use SendMessage with to: 'adb1ccdc79892a5da', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 61490\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 324390</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"f3430fef-e8a2-432b-b7c2-d692c1dd5694","timestamp":"2026-07-02T14:46:27.568Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Context: a startup hosts a free pilot where each signup instantly provisions a PERSISTENT personal AI agent (container on a single VM), which the user chats with over a dedicated Telegram bot. Agents are idle most of the time. Bottleneck: every signup = a full always-on Docker container. Research CURRENT (2025-2026) best practices for multi-tenant \"agent hosting\" / serverless-style architectures:\n\n1. Scale-to-zero patterns for per-user workloads: activation-on-message (message queue in front, container started on demand), Fly.io Machines suspend/resume (how fast? state preserved?), Cloudflare Durable Objects as per-user actor model, Modal sandboxes with memory snapshots, AWS Fargate/Lambda tradeoffs for this shape.\n2. The \"actor model\" approach: per-user state as data (files/db rows) + a small shared pool of worker runtimes that load a user's state on demand — precedents and pitfalls (who does this: Letta server, Durable Objects, Orleans-style virtual actors, Rivet Actors, etc.).\n3. Telegram-bot multi-tenant architectures: one bot with per-user routing vs bot-per-user; webhook vs long-polling at scale; message queuing/debouncing and idempotency (dedupe by update_id) — best practices to avoid duplicate replies.\n4. Capacity math precedents: roughly how many idle Docker containers (Node.js-based agent runtime, ~150-400MB RSS each) fit on typical VM sizes; techniques to raise density (memory limits, zram/swap, lazy start, kernel same-page merging).\n5. Provisioning-pipeline hardening for a public signup form: rate limiting, abuse prevention (free-tier crypto-mining and spam abuse of hosted agents is common — find documented cases/guidance), CAPTCHA/verification, quota per user, kill switches.\n6. Compliance/promise-keeping angle: if the product promises \"your agent runs on an isolated server that belongs only to you\", what architecture choices can still honestly satisfy that (dedicated microVM? dedicated container? marketing-vs-reality risk) — find how sandbox/agent-hosting vendors phrase isolation guarantees.\n\nBe honest about uncertainty. Structured English report, source URLs per claim. Your final message IS the deliverable.","agentId":"adb1ccdc79892a5da","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Multi-Tenant Agent Hosting: 2025–2026 Best Practices Research Report\n\nScope: a free pilot where each signup provisions a persistent, personal AI agent (currently: one always-on Docker container per user on a single VM), reached via a dedicated Telegram bot. Findings below are organized per your six questions, with sources per claim and explicit uncertainty notes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scale-to-zero patterns for per-user workloads\n\n**The consensus 2025–2026 pattern:** don't keep per-user processes running; keep per-user *state* durable and cheap, and attach compute only when a message arrives. Every major platform has converged on some form of this.\n\n### Activation-on-message (queue in front, container started on demand)\n- The self-hosted version of this is exactly what **Sablier** implements: a reverse-proxy middleware (Traefik/Nginx/Caddy) that starts a Docker container on the first incoming request and stops it after inactivity. It also has a \"scale mode\" that throttles CPU/memory instead of stopping, avoiding cold starts entirely. Works with plain Docker, Swarm, Podman, K8s, Proxmox LXC. ([github.com/sablierapp/sablier](https://github.com/sablierapp/sablier), [sablierapp.dev](https://sablierapp.dev/), [production-ready.de writeup](https://www.production-ready.de/2023/08/20/docker-scale-to-zero-with-traefik-sablier-en.html))\n- On Kubernetes, **KEDA** scales deployments to zero and wakes them on queue depth or HTTP events (HTTP add-on still beta). ([Google Cloud blog](https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/scale-to-zero-on-gke-with-keda/), [GKE tutorial](https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/scale-to-zero-using-keda))\n- For your Telegram shape specifically, the queue-in-front pattern is natural: a single always-on lightweight gateway receives all webhook updates, enqueues them per-user, and a supervisor `docker start`s (or unpauses) the user's container, delivers the queued messages, and stops it after N minutes idle. Sablier is HTTP-oriented, so for message-driven wake you'd likely write a small supervisor yourself (this is a modest amount of code: Docker API `start`/`stop` + a per-user queue with a debounce window).\n\n### Fly.io Machines: suspend/resume — fast, and state is preserved\n- Suspend saves the Machine's **full memory state** to persistent storage (no volume needed). Resume is \"**a few hundred ms**\" vs ~2+ seconds for a cold start of a typical app. ([fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/))\n- Hard limits that matter for you: suspendable Machines must have **≤ 2 GB RAM**, no swap, no GPU. Snapshots are discarded on redeploy (new code ⇒ cold boot, rootfs preserved). Network connections go stale across resume — your agent runtime must reconnect; clock lags a few seconds until NTP resync. ([same doc](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/))\n- Fly also has plain autostop/autostart (stop on idle, start on request via their proxy) and \"Power Pause\". ([fly.io/docs/launch/autostop-autostart](https://fly.io/docs/launch/autostop-autostart/), [Power Pause blog](https://fly.io/blog/fly-now-with-power-pause/))\n- Economics: **stopped/suspended Machines cost only rootfs storage — $0.15/GB/month**. So 1,000 idle per-user agents with 1 GB rootfs ≈ $150/mo idle cost plus compute only while awake. This is arguably the closest managed analog to your current architecture (one persistent container per user) with scale-to-zero bolted on. ([fly.io/docs/about/pricing](https://fly.io/docs/about/pricing/), [community announcement of rootfs charges](https://community.fly.io/t/we-are-going-to-start-collecting-charges-for-stopped-machines-rootfs-starting-april-25th/17825))\n\n### Cloudflare Durable Objects / Agents SDK\n- A Cloudflare Agent literally *is* a Durable Object: one addressable, single-threaded actor per user with its own embedded SQLite, WebSockets, and alarms/scheduler. It hibernates when idle (**zero compute cost while inactive**), and the platform wakes it on HTTP request, WebSocket message, alarm, or email. Marginal cost of \"one agent per user\" is ~zero; millions of instances are the intended usage. ([developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/), [blog.cloudflare.com/project-think](https://blog.cloudflare.com/project-think/), [agents.cloudflare.com](https://agents.cloudflare.com/), [truvisory analysis](https://truvisory.com/agents-mcp/agents-sdk-durable-objects/))\n- Constraint: DOs run JS/WASM in isolates — you can't run an arbitrary Node.js Docker image inside one. **Cloudflare Containers** (public beta since June 2025) closes that gap: each unique ID gets a container instance routed via a DO, which boots on first fetch and sleeps after a configurable `sleepAfter` timeout; billing stops while asleep (memory $0.0000025/GiB-s; requires $5/mo Workers Paid). Note: sleeping a Cloudflare Container loses memory state (it's stop/start, not suspend) — persistent state must live in the DO's SQLite or external storage. ([blog.cloudflare.com containers beta](https://blog.cloudflare.com/containers-are-available-in-public-beta-for-simple-global-and-programmable/), [containers pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/pricing/), [devclass coverage](https://devclass.com/2025/07/01/cloudflare-container-platform-in-public-preview-with-scale-to-zero-pricing-some-initial-limitations/))\n\n### Modal\n- Modal Sandboxes support **memory snapshots**: full state (filesystem + entire process tree memory) checkpointed and restored as an exact clone. Restores are ~2.5x faster than cold container start; init-heavy workloads see 3–10x. Introduced Jan 2025. Caveats: snapshots **expire after 7 days**, and restore requires the same instance type, which can cause scheduling delays. ([modal.com/docs/guide/memory-snapshots](https://modal.com/docs/guide/memory-snapshots), [sandbox snapshots](https://modal.com/docs/guide/sandbox-snapshots), [blog](https://modal.com/blog/mem-snapshots)) The 7-day expiry makes \"resume a user's agent after 3 idle weeks\" awkward unless you treat snapshots as a warm-start optimization over durable state, not the source of truth.\n\n### AWS Fargate / Lambda for this shape\n- **Fargate does not scale to zero** — a bot-per-user fleet of Fargate tasks reproduces your exact cost problem ($48–95/month-region per always-on minimum task). **Lambda scales to zero and bills per invocation**, but it's stateless with a 15-minute max execution — fine for \"wake, load state, respond, exit\" agents, poor for long agentic runs or in-memory continuity. Break-even analyses put Lambda ahead for spiky/infrequent traffic (your idle-most-of-the-time profile) and Fargate ahead for sustained load. ([AWS decision guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/decision-guides/latest/fargate-or-lambda/fargate-or-lambda.html), [CloudZero](https://www.cloudzero.com/blog/fargate-vs-lambda/), [LeanOps break-even analysis](https://leanopstech.com/blog/aws-lambda-vs-fargate-cost-breakeven-2026/)) AWS's own answer for persistent agents is Bedrock AgentCore-style managed runtimes rather than raw Fargate ([Medium overview](https://medium.com/@loic.labeye/lambda-fargate-agentcore-which-severless-option-to-choose-for-your-agentic-pattern-aaf68cb99700)) — uncertainty flag: I did not deep-verify AgentCore specifics in this pass.\n\n**Bottom line for #1:** cheapest-to-adopt for your current design is (a) self-hosted wake-on-message (Sablier-style supervisor + queue) on your existing VM, or (b) lift the per-user container to Fly Machines with autostop/suspend (~$0.15/GB/mo idle, sub-second-ish resume). The deeper rearchitecture is the actor model (next section).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The actor model: state-as-data + shared worker pool\n\nThis is the pattern with the strongest 2025–2026 momentum, and it directly attacks your bottleneck: **the agent is a row/file, not a process.**\n\n- **Letta** (ex-MemGPT) is the canonical \"agents as data\" precedent: a single stateful server owns all agent state in PostgreSQL — memory blocks, message history, tool state. Agents don't run as processes; the server hydrates an agent's context on each interaction, runs the LLM loop, persists, and returns. Thousands of \"agents\" are just DB rows; compute is a shared pool. ([docs.letta.com/core-concepts](https://docs.letta.com/core-concepts/), [stateful agents overview](https://docs.letta.com/guides/agents/overview/), [architecture writeup](https://sudoall.com/letta-stateful-agents-nodejs/))\n- **Cloudflare Durable Objects** — virtual-actor semantics: single-writer per actor (no concurrent-write corruption), state survives restarts/deploys, zero idle cost, wake-on-event. ([developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/))\n- **Rivet Actors** — explicitly markets \"one actor per agent/session/user\": long-lived lightweight processes, in-memory state with automatic persistence, actors sleep on inactivity and wake on request/message/alarm, self-hostable or managed; the docs explicitly cite **Microsoft Orleans' virtual actor pattern** as the lineage. ([rivet.dev/actors](https://rivet.dev/actors/), [github.com/rivet-dev/rivet](https://github.com/rivet-dev/rivet), [rivet.dev/agent](https://www.rivet.dev/agent/))\n- Orleans itself established the core guarantees you'd want: actors \"always exist\" logically, are activated on demand, deactivated when idle, and the runtime handles placement — the caller never knows whether the actor was resident.\n\n**Known pitfalls of the shared-pool/state-as-data approach** (from these sources plus multi-tenant OpenClaw write-ups):\n1. **Tenant isolation becomes your job.** In a shared runtime, every file write, tool call, and cron job must be tenant-scoped; one plugin writing to a shared path is a cross-tenant data leak. Practical mitigation seen in the wild: root all FS ops at `/workspace/<tenant_id>/` with path-traversal guards. ([KinthAI on OpenClaw multi-tenancy](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale), [dev.to multi-tenant agent platform](https://dev.to/agentbot/how-we-built-a-multi-tenant-ai-agent-platform-and-open-sourced-it-521g))\n2. **Single-writer discipline**: you must guarantee one activation per user at a time (two workers hydrating the same user = state corruption / duplicate replies). DOs/Orleans give you this for free; a DIY pool needs a per-user lock or queue.\n3. **Hydration cost**: if the agent's \"state\" includes a big filesystem (your NanoClaw-like agents have workspaces, installed packages, etc.), loading state on demand is much heavier than loading DB rows. This is the real fork in the road: *agents whose state is conversation + memory files* fit the actor model beautifully; *agents that accumulate arbitrary filesystem/container state* fit suspend/resume (Fly, Modal snapshots) better.\n4. **Noisy neighbors** in the shared pool: per-tenant CPU/memory/token quotas are required, not optional. ([KinthAI](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale))\n\nThe KinthAI analysis of exactly your architecture (\"one VM/container per OpenClaw user\") claims it breaks at **~30–50 users** operationally, costs $500–1,500/mo at 100 users at $5–15/VM, produces a fleet of stale unpatched installs, and estimates ~2 engineer-months to build real multi-tenancy instead. (Uncertainty: this is one vendor's blog with an obvious interest in that conclusion; the qualitative failure modes match other sources, but treat the numbers as illustrative.) ([blog.kinthai.ai](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale))\n\n---\n\n## 3. Telegram multi-tenant bot architecture\n\n### Bot-per-user vs one bot with routing\n- **Hard constraint on bot-per-user:** BotFather allows **20 bots per Telegram account (40 with Premium)**. Programmatic mass-creation of bots is not a supported API operation. A bot-per-user product therefore requires either users creating their own bots and pasting tokens into your signup flow (real friction, but gives each user a truly dedicated bot identity), or it simply doesn't scale on your own accounts. ([core.telegram.org/bots/faq](https://core.telegram.org/bots/faq), [tginfo limits tracker](https://github.com/tginfo/Telegram-Limits/issues/288))\n- The common scalable design is **one bot (or a small number), with per-chat routing** to the user's agent: `telegram:dm:<user_id> → agent X`, with tenant context carried via something like AsyncLocalStorage in a Node dispatcher. Multi-bot gateways (many tokens → one dispatcher, identified per-token) are the standard middle ground and are exactly what BYO-token supports. ([mudrava.com bot framework writeup](https://mudrava.com/en/now-every-new-bot-takes-three-minutes-instead-of-a-week/), [hermes-agent multi-tenant routing issues](https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/issues/9514), [BOTServer architectures](https://github.com/solyarisoftware/BOTServer/blob/master/wiki/architectures.md), [dev.to design patterns](https://dev.to/madhead/two-design-patterns-for-telegram-bots-59f5))\n- If you keep BYO-token bot-per-user: **webhooks let one server host unlimited bot tokens** (route by token in the webhook path). Long-polling per bot means one persistent `getUpdates` loop per token — and Telegram returns **409 Conflict** on concurrent getUpdates for the same token, so polling can't be horizontally scaled per bot. ([grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types](https://grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types), [gramio.dev/updates/webhook](https://gramio.dev/updates/webhook), [BOTServer](https://github.com/solyarisoftware/BOTServer/blob/master/wiki/architectures.md))\n\n### Webhook vs long-polling at scale\n- Consensus: polling is fine for dev and single low-traffic bots; **webhooks win at scale** (no wasted requests, lower latency, one endpoint for many bots, works with scale-to-zero since Telegram pushes to you — critically, *polling requires an always-on process, which defeats your scale-to-zero goal*). ([grammy.dev](https://grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types), [gramio.dev](https://gramio.dev/updates/webhook), [hostman tutorial](https://hostman.com/tutorials/difference-between-polling-and-webhook-in-telegram-bots/))\n\n### Idempotency, dedupe, debouncing\n- Telegram **retries webhook delivery** if your response is missing or ≥500, and ordering is not guaranteed under retries. Best practice: **ACK the webhook immediately (200) and process async**, dedupe by `update_id` (e.g., Redis SETNX with ~1h TTL), and make handlers idempotent. ([grammy.dev](https://grammy.dev/guide/deployment-types), [gramio.dev](https://gramio.dev/updates/webhook), [core.telegram.org/bots/faq](https://core.telegram.org/bots/faq))\n- For agent workloads specifically, **debounce**: users send bursts (\"wait\", \"actually also do X\"); queue updates per chat and flush to the agent after a short quiet window (1–3 s) or when the agent becomes free, delivering the batch as one turn. This also gives you the wake-on-message buffer for free: the queue *is* the wake signal. (This paragraph is synthesis from the routing/queueing sources above plus standard practice — no single canonical citation.)\n- Outbound rate limits (from Telegram's Bots FAQ): ~1 msg/sec per chat, ~30 msg/sec overall per bot — a shared single bot needs an outbound rate-limiter/queue; bot-per-user mostly sidesteps this. ([core.telegram.org/bots/faq](https://core.telegram.org/bots/faq))\n\n---\n\n## 4. Capacity math: idle Node.js agent containers per VM\n\n- Container runtime overhead is ~10 MB plus your app's RSS; a general density study puts ~220 MB-average containers at **400–500 practical per 128 GB host** (theoretical 560+). ([OneUptime containers-vs-VMs density deep dive](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-01-16-containers-vs-vms-density-efficiency-comparison/view))\n- Scaling that to your numbers (~150–400 MB RSS per Node agent, no dedup, no swap, leaving ~15–20% headroom for host + page cache):\n  - **8 GB VM:** ~15–40 idle agents\n  - **16 GB:** ~35–85\n  - **32 GB:** ~70–170\n  - **64 GB:** ~140–350\n  These are arithmetic estimates from the cited per-container figures, not benchmarks — CPU is rarely the binding constraint for *idle* agents; memory is.\n- **Density levers, in rough order of payoff:**\n  1. **Lazy start / stop-on-idle** (Sablier-style, section 1): the single biggest lever — idle agents cost ~0 RAM; capacity becomes a function of *concurrent active* users (often 1–5% of signups). ([sablierapp.dev](https://sablierapp.dev/))\n  2. **zram swap + relaxed overcommit** (`vm.overcommit_memory=1`): compressed in-RAM swap lets you overcommit with \"a negligible hit\" for rarely-touched pages — idle Node heaps compress well; lz4/zstd tradeoff documented. Commonly cited effective gains of ~1.5–2x on idle-heavy fleets, though exact ratios depend on workload (uncertainty: no rigorous Node-specific benchmark found). ([Debian zramswap manpage](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/zram-tools/zramswap.1.en.html), [zram+overcommit notes](https://copyninja.in/blog/zram_memory_overcommit.html), [systemd-zram-generator guide](https://dev.to/lyraalishaikh/stop-hitting-swap-too-late-practical-zram-on-linux-with-systemd-zram-generator-4m4j))\n  3. **Per-container memory limits + `--max-old-space-size`**: Node 20+ is cgroup-aware for max heap, but you should still set the V8 heap below the cgroup limit to get GC pressure instead of OOM-kills. ([Red Hat: Node.js 20+ memory in containers](https://developers.redhat.com/articles/2025/10/10/nodejs-20-memory-management-containers), [IBM article](https://developer.ibm.com/articles/nodejs-memory-management-in-container-environments/), [Node best practices repo](https://github.com/goldbergyoni/nodebestpractices/blob/master/sections/docker/memory-limit.md))\n  4. **KSM (kernel same-page merging)**: dedupes identical pages across identical runtimes; 5–10% CPU overhead for scanning. Two caveats: KSM only merges pages marked `MADV_MERGEABLE` (kernels ≥6.4 allow process-wide opt-in via `prctl(PR_SET_MEMORY_MERGE)`; wiring this into containers takes effort), and cross-tenant page dedup is a documented **side-channel risk** — reasonable within one trust domain, questionable across untrusting tenants. ([OneUptime KSM guide](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-03-04-configure-kernel-samepage-merging-ksm-for-memory-deduplication/view), [Proxmox KSM wiki noting security tradeoffs](https://pve.proxmox.com/wiki/Kernel_Samepage_Merging_(KSM)), [Wikipedia KSM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_same-page_merging))\n- Honest caveat: I found no published benchmark of specifically \"N idle Node.js agent containers per VM size\"; the figures above combine the OneUptime density study with your stated RSS range.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Provisioning-pipeline hardening for a public free signup\n\nFree compute + public signup is one of the most-abused combinations on the internet, and it's well documented:\n\n- **Documented abuse wave:** crypto-mining gangs systematically farmed free tiers of GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, TravisCI, CircleCI, LayerCI, Render, CodeShip, Sourcehut, Okteto, Azure — cycling new accounts as each free allotment ran out. **TravisCI and Sourcehut killed their free tiers over it**; LayerCI documented the arms race in \"Crypto miners are killing free CI.\" ([The Record](https://therecord.media/crypto-mining-gangs-are-running-amok-on-free-cloud-computing-platforms), [webapp.io/blog/crypto-miners-are-killing-free-ci](https://webapp.io/blog/crypto-miners-are-killing-free-ci), [The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/how-devops-sites-are-battling-cryptocurrency-miners/))\n- **GitLab's countermeasures** (a good template): CI-minute quotas applied to free namespaces, and **credit-card verification for free compute** (card validated, not charged) — the single measure they credit with cutting abuse most. ([about.gitlab.com/blog/prevent-crypto-mining-abuse](https://about.gitlab.com/blog/prevent-crypto-mining-abuse/), [The Register](https://www.theregister.com/2021/05/19/gitlab_crypto/))\n- Your agents are *general-purpose compute with network egress on your API bill*, so assume: miners, spam/phishing senders, proxies-for-hire, and LLM-credit farming (people extracting your Anthropic/OpenAI spend for free inference). The LLM-token-farming variant is newer; documented cases are thinner than crypto-mining — flagging that as inference, though the incentive structure is identical.\n\n**Layered checklist synthesized from the guidance:**\n1. **Signup friction ladder:** email verification (double opt-in) → block disposable-email domains → CAPTCHA (reCAPTCHA v3 / Turnstile invisible scoring) → phone or OAuth verification for suspicious cohorts → card-on-file only if abuse persists. ([UserCheck free-trial-abuse guide](https://www.usercheck.com/guides/how-to-prevent-free-trial-abuse), [payproglobal](https://payproglobal.com/how-to/prevent-free-trial-abuse/), [suped.com](https://www.suped.com/email-deliverability/answers/how-can-i-prevent-nefarious-email-signups-using-rate-limiting-recaptcha-and-double-opt-in))\n2. **Rate limits:** per-IP and per-ASN signup caps; flag multiple signups from one IP range; VPN/proxy detection scoring. ([sensfrx](https://blog.sensfrx.ai/free-trial-abuse/), [UserCheck](https://www.usercheck.com/guides/how-to-prevent-free-trial-abuse))\n3. **Runtime quotas per agent (this is where a Telegram-gated product is lucky):** a Telegram account is itself a phone-verified identity — bind one agent per Telegram user ID and much of the fake-signup surface disappears. Then cap per-user: LLM tokens/day, container CPU shares and memory, disk, and **egress** (crypto pools and spam need network; a default-deny or throttled egress policy is the highest-value single control — GitLab/LayerCI both note miners need sustained CPU + specific egress). ([webapp.io](https://webapp.io/blog/crypto-miners-are-killing-free-ci), [about.gitlab.com](https://about.gitlab.com/blog/prevent-crypto-mining-abuse/))\n4. **Detection + kill switches:** sustained-high-CPU alerts on \"idle\" agents (miner signature), per-agent instant suspend (`docker pause`/`stop` is your kill switch), global provisioning freeze flag, and a review queue for anomalous accounts. CircleCI describes running a dedicated abuse-detection function for exactly this. ([The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/how-devops-sites-are-battling-cryptocurrency-miners/))\n5. **Provision lazily:** don't build the container at signup — create the DB record, and materialize the container on first real message. This makes mass-signup attacks cost you ~nothing and doubles as your scale-to-zero onramp. (Synthesis; also the KinthAI onboarding-friction point argues signup-time heavy provisioning hurts conversion anyway — [blog.kinthai.ai](https://blog.kinthai.ai/openclaw-multi-tenancy-why-vm-per-user-doesnt-scale).)\n\n---\n\n## 6. The \"your own isolated server\" promise: honesty vs architecture\n\n**Current state of your product vs. the promise:** a Docker container on a shared VM is *not* \"a server that belongs only to you\" under any technically honest reading — all containers share one kernel, and the 2025–2026 sandboxing literature is blunt that namespace isolation is the *minimum viable* tier: one kernel exploit or mis-mounted socket exposes every tenant. ([emirb.github.io \"Your Container Is Not a Sandbox\"](https://emirb.github.io/blog/microvm-2026/), [Northflank isolation guide](https://northflank.com/blog/how-to-sandbox-ai-agents), [manveerc sandboxing guide](https://manveerc.substack.com/p/ai-agent-sandboxing-guide))\n\n**How vendors in this exact market phrase isolation — the observable norm is to name the mechanism, not use \"belongs to you\" language:**\n- **E2B:** \"each sandbox runs in a Firecracker microVM with a dedicated kernel\" — hardware-level isolation, named technology. ([Northflank comparison](https://northflank.com/blog/daytona-vs-e2b-ai-code-execution-sandboxes), [ZenML comparison](https://www.zenml.io/blog/e2b-vs-daytona))\n- **Daytona:** honestly positioned as Docker-with-shared-kernel by default, with harder options (Kata/Cloud Hypervisor, Sysbox) for stronger isolation — i.e., it discloses the tier. ([ZenML](https://www.zenml.io/blog/e2b-vs-daytona), [Spheron guide](https://www.spheron.network/blog/ai-agent-code-execution-sandbox-e2b-daytona-firecracker/))\n- **KiloClaw** (managed OpenClaw hosting — your product's closest comparable): \"each customer runs inside a dedicated Firecracker micro-VM… no shared kernel, filesystem, or process namespace between tenants.\" ([kilo.ai](https://kilo.ai/openclaw/managed-alternatives))\n- Commentary across the space explicitly warns to \"separate the marketing from the underlying isolation technology.\" ([shayon.dev \"Let's discuss sandbox isolation\"](https://www.shayon.dev/post/2026/52/lets-discuss-sandbox-isolation/), [Northflank \"What is a sandbox?\"](https://northflank.com/blog/what-is-a-sandbox-and-isolation-technologies))\n\n**Ways to honestly satisfy (or fix) the promise, cheapest first:**\n1. **Rewrite the promise** to match reality: \"your own isolated agent environment — dedicated container, private filesystem and memory, resource-isolated\" is defensible for Docker-per-user; \"your own server\" is not. Cheapest fix, zero infra work.\n2. **Dedicated microVM per user** (Firecracker/Kata/Fly Machines — Fly Machines *are* per-tenant microVMs): lets you truthfully say \"dedicated virtual machine with its own kernel,\" which is how E2B/KiloClaw phrase it. Combined with suspend/stop-on-idle this doesn't have to cost much more than containers. ([fly.io](https://fly.io/docs/reference/suspend-resume/), [emirb](https://emirb.github.io/blog/microvm-2026/))\n3. **\"Belongs only to you\" taken literally** implies dedicated hardware/VM — almost certainly not what a free pilot should promise. Note that if you go the actor-model/shared-pool route from §2, the promise becomes *less* true, not more — you'd need to reword toward \"private, isolated agent with its own memory and workspace.\"\n4. Legal-risk note, honestly flagged: I found **no** FTC enforcement action specifically about \"dedicated server\" isolation claims in agent/sandbox hosting; the marketing-vs-reality risk here is currently reputational/contractual (and generic deceptive-practices exposure) rather than a documented enforcement pattern. ([search corroboration: Northflank/community sources above; absence of FTC cases per search results])\n\n---\n\n## Synthesis: what the evidence points to for your shape\n\nTwo coherent end-states, both well-precedented in 2025–2026:\n\n- **Path A — keep container-per-user, make it lazy** (smallest delta): webhook gateway (always-on, tiny) → per-user message queue with `update_id` dedupe + debounce → supervisor starts the user's container on message, stops after idle; zram + memory limits on the VM; provision on first message, not at signup; Telegram-identity-anchored quotas + egress limits + CPU-anomaly kill switch. Later, swap the backend for Fly Machines (suspend/resume, $0.15/GB/mo idle) to also make the \"dedicated VM\" claim honest.\n- **Path B — actor model** (bigger rewrite, best economics at scale): agent state as DB rows/files (Letta-style) served by a small shared worker pool, or built directly on Durable Objects/Rivet-style actors — zero idle cost, but tenant isolation becomes application-level and the \"your own server\" promise must be rewritten.\n\nThe main genuine uncertainty across sources: real-world resume-latency numbers under load (Fly says \"few hundred ms\" but no independent benchmarks found), Node-specific zram density gains (no rigorous benchmark), and LLM-credit-farming abuse (incentive-obvious, thinly documented compared to crypto-mining)."}],"resolvedModel":"claude-fable-5","totalDurationMs":324390,"totalTokens":61490,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1523,"cache_read_input_tokens":48866,"output_tokens":11099,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1523},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":11099,"cache_read_input_tokens":48866,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1523,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":22}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e357e07d-25f0-4c8d-8a65-e864595aa66b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
