cture: which of these can act as a credential-brokering proxy where the AGENT NEVER HOLDS TOKENS (their current OneCLI principle)? Which require tokens in the agent's environment? Per-user (end-user auth) vs per-workspace auth support — critical: each of their users must connect THEIR OWN Gmail/CRM.\n8. Pricing reality check: for a pilot with ~100-1000 users each doing tens of actions/day, rough monthly cost per option (mark estimates clearly).\n\nKey question: should they fix/keep their homegrown OneCLI vault, or adopt one of these as the integration layer under it — and which shortlist makes sense for per-end-user auth with Telegram-based non-technical users (auth flow must be a simple link click)?\n\nIRON RULES: name things precisely, link sources, mark estimates/uncertainty, don't fabricate. Structured English report. Your final message IS the deliverable."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2116,"cache_read_input_tokens":84188,"output_tokens":3412,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2116,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3412,"cache_read_input_tokens":84188,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2116,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2116},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CcdSW1M9ct6ahHynCSS3P","attributionMcpServer":"nanoclaw","attributionMcpTool":"send_message","type":"assistant","uuid":"aa9ca72a-9d08-4bd6-bced-527da6681c65","timestamp":"2026-07-02T15:08:28.508Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"aa9ca72a-9d08-4bd6-bced-527da6681c65","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_01RGNYYZ9UmAM42RJLXyWyhP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019HxPwgAkd2tJUwqsUP5i5z","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research digital-employee and calibration products","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Context: a startup (NanoCo) builds ONE personal AI agent per employee (Claude Agent SDK stack, Telegram channel). Vision: the agent encodes the methodology of the user's ROLE (multi-step workflows at \"core moments\", e.g. post-sales-call chain), calibrated to the individual via a self-profiling interview at onboarding; organizational guardrails expand as trust grows; \"managing your agent is how the employee grows\". Research what exists (2025-2026) in adjacent product space:\n\n1. **\"Digital employee / AI coworker\" products**: 11x.ai, Artisan (Ava), Maisa AI, Piper (Qualified), Beam.ai, Ema (ema.co), Lyzr, Cykel, MultiOn/please, Devin (as \"AI engineer\" framing). For each (briefly): what role(s), how the \"employee\" is configured/onboarded, pricing model, and — most important — evidence of whether the category works (public traction, churn stories, criticism like the 11x controversy).\n2. **Sales-specific post-call workflow products** (their first target role is sales): Momentum.io, Attention.com, Gong (call → CRM/followup automations), Salesforce Agentforce, HubSpot Breeze agents, Day.ai, Granola (meeting notes → actions), Fireflies/Fathom action items. What exactly do they automate after a call, and what does the market consider table-stakes vs differentiating?\n3. **Onboarding-by-interview / calibration patterns**: products where an AI interviews the user to build its own config/persona — e.g., personal-assistant products (Lindy onboarding, Martin, ohai.ai, yohana), AI coaching products, or \"agent interviews you then builds your workflow\" mechanisms. Also research \"solela.ai\" — a consumer product supposedly built on the same containerized-agent stack with \"privacy by architecture\" positioning; verify it exists and describe its onboarding/positioning (if not findable, say so).\n4. **\"Trust levels / expanding autonomy\" mechanisms**: any products or frameworks with graduated agent autonomy (human-in-the-loop → suggest → auto with review → full auto), e.g. approval workflows in Lindy/Zapier agents, \"confidence thresholds\", Microsoft Copilot agent governance, writeups on progressive agent autonomy in enterprises.\n5. **The employee-development angle**: anything that frames \"working with/managing your AI agent\" as a skill-building or career-development experience (L&D products with agents, \"AI upskilling through use\", prompts-to-skills gamification). Is anyone doing this explicitly, or is it whitespace?\n\nKey questions: (a) what onboarding/calibration UX actually retains non-technical users; (b) what the sales-role automation market treats as proven value; (c) is \"role methodology packs, personally calibrated\" occupied or whitespace; (d) is graduated-trust guardrails a real differentiator.\n\nIRON RULES: name products precisely, link sources per claim, verify before asserting (especially solela.ai), mark uncertainty. Structured English report. Your final message IS the deliverable."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2116,"cache_read_input_tokens":84188,"output_tokens":3412,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2116,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3412,"cache_read_input_tokens":84188,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2116,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2116},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CcdSW1M9ct6ahHynCSS3P","attributionMcpServer":"nanoclaw","attributionMcpTool":"send_message","type":"assistant","uuid":"46362501-3ba0-4026-9d72-0d37b722415e","timestamp":"2026-07-02T15:08:50.038Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"08f58fec-7728-45e3-8d3a-afdac2f2abd3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"42d624f0-51b1-4086-a07f-c40f6806faec","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FfoC4jS8NQFNULcVJrmQQo","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Research Report: Encoding Professional Methodology into Agent Artifacts — What Exists (2025–2026)\n\nScope: what the NanoClaw-based startup can adopt or learn from, across (1) Anthropic Agent Skills, (2) workflow/playbook encoding in agent products, (3) \"teach the agent your process\" mechanisms, (4) role-based template libraries, (5) adoption evidence — then answers to the three key questions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Anthropic Agent Skills — the SKILL.md substrate\n\n### 1.1 Exact format (open spec)\n\nAnthropic published Agent Skills as an **open standard** (spec at [agentskills.io/specification](https://agentskills.io/specification), reference repo [github.com/agentskills/agentskills](https://github.com/agentskills/agentskills)). Per third-party reporting, the open standard was released Dec 18, 2025 and has been adopted by 26+ platforms including OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor, VS Code ([agensi.io overview](https://www.agensi.io/learn/agent-skills-open-standard) — adoption count is that source's claim, I could not independently verify \"26+\").\n\nA skill is a directory whose only required file is `SKILL.md`:\n\n```\nskill-name/\n├── SKILL.md          # required: YAML frontmatter + markdown instructions\n├── scripts/          # optional executable code (run, not loaded into context)\n├── references/       # optional docs loaded on demand\n└── assets/           # optional templates/data\n```\n\nFrontmatter fields (from the [spec](https://agentskills.io/specification)):\n- `name` (required, ≤64 chars, lowercase/digits/hyphens, must match directory name)\n- `description` (required, ≤1024 chars — must say *what it does AND when to use it*, with trigger keywords)\n- optional: `license`, `compatibility` (≤500 chars, env requirements), `metadata` (arbitrary k/v, e.g. author/version), `allowed-tools` (experimental, pre-approved tools)\n\n**Progressive disclosure** is the core design ([Anthropic engineering post](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills)):\n1. **Level 1 — metadata** (~100 tokens/skill): all skills' name+description injected into system prompt at startup.\n2. **Level 2 — SKILL.md body** (<5,000 tokens / <500 lines recommended): read via filesystem tool only when the model judges the skill relevant.\n3. **Level 3+ — bundled files**: references/scripts loaded or executed on demand; \"the amount of context that can be bundled into a skill is effectively unbounded\" because scripts execute without entering context.\n\n### 1.2 What makes a GOOD skill (Anthropic's own guidance)\n\nFrom the [engineering blog](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills) and the [skill authoring best-practices doc](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices) — the best-practices page is the single most relevant document for your \"methodology pack\" idea:\n\n- **Conciseness**: \"Claude is already very smart\" — only add context the model lacks; every loaded token competes with conversation history.\n- **Degrees of freedom matched to task fragility**: high freedom (heuristic text) for judgment tasks like code review; medium (parameterized pseudocode); low freedom (\"run exactly this script, do not modify\") for fragile sequences. This maps directly onto professional methodology: judgment steps vs. deterministic steps.\n- **Workflow + checklist pattern**: for complex multi-step processes, embed a copyable checklist the agent checks off as it progresses (their canonical multi-step workflow pattern — exactly the \"core moment chain\" shape).\n- **Feedback loops**: run validator → fix → repeat; \"plan-validate-execute\" with machine-verifiable intermediate artifacts (e.g. a `changes.json` validated by script before execution).\n- **Template pattern & examples pattern**: bundle output templates (strict or flexible) and input→output example pairs.\n- **Conditional workflow pattern**: decision points routing to sub-workflows, pushed into separate files if large.\n- **Description is the trigger**: written in third person, with explicit \"Use when…\" phrasing and keywords; Claude picks from potentially 100+ skills by description alone.\n- **Evaluation-driven development**: build 3+ evals *before* writing the skill; baseline without the skill; iterate. There is no built-in eval runner — you roll your own.\n- **Iterate with Claude itself**: \"Claude A\" authors/refines the skill, \"Claude B\" (fresh instance) tests it; capture successful approaches and mistakes back into the skill.\n- Anti-patterns: vague names (`helper`, `utils`), too many alternative approaches, nested reference chains >1 level deep, time-sensitive info, magic constants in scripts.\n\n### 1.3 Discovery/loading in Claude Code and the Agent SDK\n\n- **Agent SDK** ([docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/skills)): skills load from `.claude/skills/` (project) and `~/.claude/skills/` (user) only if `settingSources`/`setting_sources` includes those sources (SDK loads no filesystem settings by default), and `\"Skill\"` must be in `allowed_tools`. Plugin-bundled skills are namespaced `plugin-name:skill-name` ([plugins in the SDK](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/plugins)). Directly relevant to NanoClaw since it runs on the Agent SDK.\n- **Claude Code** ([skills doc](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills)) extends the open standard with features that matter a lot for methodology packs:\n  - **Invocation control**: `disable-model-invocation: true` (only user can trigger, e.g. `/deploy` — for side-effectful workflows where the human controls timing) and `user-invocable: false` (background knowledge only the model loads). Default: both can invoke.\n  - `when_to_use` field — extra trigger phrases appended to description (combined cap 1,536 chars in the listing).\n  - **Arguments**: `$ARGUMENTS`, `$0/$1`, named `arguments:` — a skill is parameterizable like a command (\"one sentence → whole chain runs\" is literally `/end-of-call <client>`).\n  - **Subagent execution**: `context: fork` + `agent:` runs the skill in a forked subagent context.\n  - `allowed-tools` (pre-approved permissions while active), `disallowed-tools`, `model`, `effort`, per-skill `hooks`, `paths` (activate only for matching files), dynamic context injection via `` !`command` `` blocks.\n  - **State/lifecycle limits**: invoked skill content enters the conversation once and is *not re-read*; on auto-compaction only the first 5,000 tokens of each recent skill are re-attached under a combined 25,000-token budget — older skills can drop out entirely. There is **no built-in persistent state machine**: state across a multi-step workflow must live in the conversation, in files the skill writes, or in checklists.\n  - Custom slash commands have been merged into skills (a `.claude/commands/deploy.md` and a skill are now the same mechanism).\n\n### 1.4 Marketplace situation\n\n- **Official**: [anthropics/skills GitHub repo](https://github.com/anthropics/skills) (doubles as a Claude Code plugin marketplace via `marketplace.json`; install e.g. `/plugin install document-skills@anthropic-agent-skills`); a curated [anthropics/claude-plugins-official](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-plugins-official) directory; and in the Claude apps a **unified in-product directory** (Customize > Skills > Browse skills) featuring partner-built skills from Notion, Figma, Atlassian etc. ([help center](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/14328846-browse-skills-connectors-and-plugins-in-one-directory), [skills announcement](https://claude.com/blog/skills)). I found no evidence that a standalone `skills.claude.com` site exists — the directory lives inside the product; treat \"skills.claude.com\" as unconfirmed. **No official paid marketplace exists**; Anthropic has only signaled interest in community contributions ([claudeskills.info](https://claudeskills.info/skills/) reporting).\n- **Third-party**: [awesome-claude-skills](https://github.com/travisvn/awesome-claude-skills), [claudeskills.info](https://claudeskills.info/skills/) (~140+ free skills), [skillsdirectory.com](https://www.skillsdirectory.com/), various personal marketplaces on GitHub. All small/curated.\n- **Cautionary tale — ClawHub (OpenClaw's skill marketplace)**: grew from ~2,857 to 10,700+ skills in weeks, and security audits found **341 malicious skills** (Koi Security, later 824+ as the marketplace grew), typosquatting, scanner-evasion (payloads hidden in oversized READMEs), and ranking manipulation ([The Hacker News](https://thehackernews.com/2026/02/researchers-find-341-malicious-clawhub.html), [Unit 42](https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/openclaw-ai-supply-chain-risk/), [Dark Reading](https://www.darkreading.com/cyber-risk/malicious-openclaw-skills-clawhub-threaten-ai-supply-chain), [Silverfort](https://www.silverfort.com/blog/clawhub-vulnerability-enables-attackers-to-manipulate-rankings-to-become-the-number-one-skill/)). Lesson: skills are prompt-injection + supply-chain surface; a curated, signed, first-party pack library beats an open marketplace for a trust-sensitive product.\n- **Expert assessment**: Simon Willison, [\"Claude Skills are awesome, maybe a bigger deal than MCP\"](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Oct/16/claude-skills/) (Oct 16, 2025): skills are \"markdown with a tiny bit of YAML,\" trivially shareable, and outsource the hard parts to the harness — vs. MCP's protocol weight and token cost.\n\n---\n\n## 2. How workflow/agent products package \"a multi-step professional workflow\"\n\n| Product | Unit of packaging | Trigger model | Template library |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lindy.ai** | A \"Lindy\" = agent with trigger + steps (flow-chart style), built in natural language; Lindies can call each other (\"societies of agents\") | Event triggers (new email, call ended, form, schedule); multi-trigger workflows, loops, conditions | 50+ pre-built templates organized by use case (sales, support, meetings, email) and industry ([templates docs](https://docs.lindy.ai/fundamentals/lindy-101/templates), [template categories](https://www.lindy.ai/template-categories/workflow-automation)) |\n| **Dust.tt** | An agent = plain-English **instructions** + tools + company data sources; new **\"Skills\"** feature packages instructions+knowledge+tools into reusable modules shared across all workspace agents — update once, all agents benefit ([changelog](https://docs.dust.tt/changelog/skills-share-expertise-across-all-your-agents)) | @-mention in Slack/chat, native triggers (schedule/webhook), or external orchestrators (Zapier/Make/n8n) ([blog](https://dust.tt/blog/automate-dust-agents-workflow-tools)) | Team-oriented agent examples rather than a big public template store; semantic layer over company knowledge is the differentiator ([product](https://dust.tt/home/product)) |\n| **Relevance AI** | **\"Workforce\"** = a team of agents that \"automate entire playbooks — triggered by events or signals,\" each agent owning a step and handing off context ([workforce page](https://relevanceai.com/workforce)); flagship Bosh (BDR) is a multi-agent system with delegating sub-agents ([Bosh docs](https://relevanceai.com/docs/agent/agent-examples/bosh-bdr-agent)) | Event/signal triggers or chat | Public [agent marketplace/templates](https://relevanceai.com/templates) organized **by role** (SDR, AE, RevOps, recruiter…) and by task |\n| **Zapier Agents** (successor to Zapier Central) | **\"Behavior\"** = trigger + natural-language instructions + connected apps; an agent bundles several behaviors + data sources | Any of Zapier's 7,000+ app triggers, chat, buttons, or invocation from a Zap ([build an agent](https://help.zapier.com/hc/en-us/articles/24393442652557-Build-an-agent-in-Zapier-Agents), [trigger setup](https://help.zapier.com/hc/en-us/articles/45394909914381-Set-up-your-agent-s-trigger)) | Growing template library with preset instructions+apps per business function (Lead Qualification & Scoring, Call Follow-Up Assistant, Candidate Ranking…) — editable after instantiation ([agents guide](https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-agents-guide/)) |\n| **Relay.app** | **\"Playbook\"** = trigger + step sequence with human-in-the-loop steps; an \"Agent\" is an AI teammate that *owns a set of workflows* (role = bundle of playbooks — closest existing model to your \"role pack\") ([docs](https://docs.relay.app/agents/agents), [how it works](https://www.relay.app/how-it-works)) | App triggers, schedules, manual; \"mini AI agents\" as steps inside deterministic flows ([blog](https://www.relay.app/blog/how-to-add-a-mini-ai-agent-to-your-workflow)) | Playbooks are explicitly \"repeatable, teachable processes\" — templated, shared, refined |\n| **Gumloop** | Flow (node graph); agents pick tools mid-task via MCP | Triggers via cloud platform, browser extension | Deep template library by domain: HR/hiring, sales/CRM, legal, SEO, docs ([comparison](https://www.gumloop.com/vs/n8n)) |\n| **n8n** | Workflow JSON with an AI Agent node + sub-nodes (model, memory, tools) | Any node trigger; chat requires a separate Chat Trigger node | ~10,400 community templates, ~7,100 in the AI category ([n8n workflows](https://n8n.io/workflows/), [AI category](https://n8n.io/workflows/categories/ai/)) |\n\n**Takeaway for the startup:** the market's dominant unit is *trigger + instructions + connected tools* (\"behavior\"/\"playbook\"), with role-level packaging appearing only as (a) Relay's \"agent owns a set of workflows,\" (b) Relevance's multi-agent \"workforce playbooks,\" and (c) Dust's cross-agent \"Skills\" modules. Nobody yet ships a portable, open-format *role methodology pack*; everyone's packaging is platform-locked. Anthropic's SKILL.md is the only open, portable substrate in the list.\n\n---\n\n## 3. \"Teach the agent your process\" mechanisms\n\n- **Demonstration capture (SOP tools)**: [Scribe](https://scribe.com/library/scribe-vs-tango) and [Tango](https://www.tango.ai/) record a user performing a workflow in the browser and auto-generate step-by-step guides with screenshots; Tango now ships \"Tango Agents\" on top of captured SOPs (product details thin — uncertain how agentic it really is). These produce *human-readable* SOPs, which are exactly the raw material a SKILL.md compiler could ingest.\n- **Demonstration → automation**: [Optexity](https://optexity.com/blog/the-best-production-ready-browser-automation-platforms-in-2026) generates deterministic browser automations from a screen recording (\"demonstrate once, deploy via API\"). The broader 2026 agentic-browser landscape treats \"teach mode\" (show once, agent repeats) as an established pattern ([landscape guide](https://nohacks.co/blog/agentic-browser-landscape-2026)). I did not find a mature *chat-agent* product that learns a professional's methodology purely from watching — this appears to be genuinely open ground.\n- **Interview-based teaching (Anthropic patterns)** — most directly adoptable for NanoClaw:\n  - **`/init`** bootstraps CLAUDE.md by having the agent explore the environment; an enhanced interactive multi-phase init flow exists (`CLAUDE_CODE_NEW_INIT=1`) ([best practices](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices)).\n  - **AskUserQuestion interview pattern**: Anthropic's own recommended practice is \"have Claude interview you first\" using the AskUserQuestion tool before big work — the exact mechanism for calibrating a generic role pack to an individual ([best practices](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices)).\n  - **Memory layering**: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (personal), `./CLAUDE.md` (shared), `CLAUDE.local.md` (private), plus auto-memory (MEMORY.md) written back autonomously ([memory guide](https://joseparreogarcia.substack.com/p/claude-code-memory-explained), [memory tool docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool)). Community consensus: keep CLAUDE.md ~80–120 high-signal lines; procedures belong in skills, facts in CLAUDE.md ([Claude Code skills doc](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills): \"create a skill… when a section of CLAUDE.md has grown into a procedure rather than a fact\").\n  - **Skill self-authoring**: Anthropic explicitly recommends \"ask Claude to capture its successful approaches and common mistakes into reusable context and code within a skill,\" and states plans for agents to create/edit/evaluate skills autonomously ([engineering post](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills)). So the \"teach by doing it once together, then crystallize into a skill\" loop (Claude A authors / Claude B tests) is Anthropic's own documented workflow — adopt it verbatim.\n- **Zapier Central** (predecessor of Agents) let users teach \"behaviors\" conversationally and correct the agent's drafts over time ([community post](https://community.zapier.com/p/zapier-central)); Zapier Agents kept the natural-language instructions + editable behaviors model.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role-based agent template libraries\n\n- **Relevance AI** — most explicit \"role as product\": named AI teammates (Bosh the BDR/SDR, Apla account research, Lima lifecycle marketing) plus a [template marketplace](https://relevanceai.com/templates) organized by role pages (e.g. [SDR agent templates](https://relevanceai.com/agent-templates-roles/sales-development-representative-sdr-ai-agents-1), recruiter templates). Templates instantiate into your workspace and are calibrated by connecting CRM/data sources and editing instructions.\n- **Beam.ai** — [library of production-ready agent templates](https://beam.ai/agents), each shipping with configured workflows, integration setups, and setup guidance; 1,500+ integrations ([template blog](https://beam.ai/agentic-insights/5-ready-to-use-ai-agent-templates-that-save-40-hours-per-week)).\n- **Lindy.ai** — 50+ role/use-case templates instantiated as editable Lindies ([docs](https://docs.lindy.ai/fundamentals/lindy-101/templates)).\n- **CrewAI** — role-based by construction (`role`, `goal`, `backstory` per agent, YAML-config recommended) ([agents docs](https://docs.crewai.com/en/concepts/agents)); recruiting crews (sourcing → screening → matching → evaluation agents) are a canonical community project ([ProjectPro](https://www.projectpro.io/article/crew-ai-projects-ideas-and-examples/1117)) — but it's a dev framework, not an end-user template product.\n- **MindStudio** — no-code visual builder with templates and NL-to-workflow generation ([vs CrewAI](https://www.mindstudio.ai/blog/mindstudio-vs-crewai)). **Voiceflow**: conversational-agent templates, mostly support/voice — I did not verify role-pack depth there (uncertain).\n- **Calibration mechanisms observed across all of these** (thin everywhere): (1) connect your data sources/apps (the template's variables), (2) edit the natural-language instructions after instantiation, (3) upload brand/knowledge docs, (4) chat-based refinement of drafts. Nobody I found does a structured *methodology interview* (\"walk me through what you do after a sales call, what does 'qualified' mean for you, who gets the brief\") that compiles answers into the template's parameters. Jotform/Pipefy market \"clarifying questions\" onboarding for narrow customer-onboarding agents ([Pipefy](https://www.pipefy.com/blog/customize-customer-onboarding-ai-agents/)), but that's for end-customer flows, not professional calibration.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Adoption evidence (what gets traction)\n\nHard public numbers are scarce; here's what exists:\n\n- **n8n**: ~10,400 community templates; ~7,100 in the AI category; per one tracker, **AI-agent templates ≈78% of new submissions** (30-day window, May 2026) and multi-agent templates doubled in two months to ~¼ of the AI category ([n8n workflows](https://n8n.io/workflows/), [connectsafely analysis](https://connectsafely.ai/articles/n8n-templates-workflow-automation-examples) — third-party analysis, treat percentages as approximate).\n- **Relevance AI**: $37M+ raised (Series B $24M, May 2025); **40,000 agents built in a single January**; customers incl. Canva, Databricks, KPMG, Activision ([SaaStr](https://www.saastr.com/saastr-ai-app-of-the-week-relevance-ai-the-platform-where-ops-teams-build-entire-ai-workforces-without-writing-a-line-of-code/)). Its flagship, most-marketed template is a *sales* role (Bosh BDR) — consistent with sales/SDR being the wedge.\n- **ClawHub**: ~2,800 → 10,700+ skills within weeks of launch — demonstrates huge appetite for installable agent skills, and equally the security downside ([eSecurity Planet](https://www.esecurityplanet.com/threats/hundreds-of-malicious-skills-found-in-openclaws-clawhub/)).\n- **Zapier's public template lists** skew to sales/revops (lead qualification, call follow-up) and recruiting (candidate ranking) ([guide](https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-agents-guide/)).\n- **Which roles win**: no rigorous public breakdown found. Directional evidence (what vendors lead with, template category sizes) points to **sales/SDR first, support second, recruiting third** — but I'm marking this as inference, not measured fact.\n- Gartner (via [IBM](https://www.ibm.com/think/ai-agents) roundup): 40% of enterprise apps to embed task-specific agents by end-2026, up from <5% in 2025 — context, not product-level evidence.\n- No public adoption numbers exist yet for Anthropic's skills directory or the anthropics/skills repo beyond GitHub activity (uncertain).\n\n---\n\n## Answers to the key questions\n\n**(a) Is SKILL.md the right substrate for \"role methodology packs\"? What are its limits?**\n\nYes, with a composition layer on top. Strengths: open standard, portable across 20+ harnesses, progressive disclosure keeps a fat methodology pack token-cheap, `scripts/` gives deterministic steps (CRM writes, formatting), `assets/` gives output templates, Claude Code/SDK extensions give you arguments (`/end-of-call acme`), invocation control, per-skill tool permissions, and subagent forking. Anthropic's checklist/feedback-loop/conditional-workflow patterns in the [best-practices doc](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices) are literally \"professional methodology encoding\" patterns.\n\nLimits to engineer around:\n1. **Triggering is probabilistic** — it hinges entirely on the 1,024-char description (1,536 incl. `when_to_use` in Claude Code). For \"core moments\" you should not rely on ambient triggering: use explicit invocation (one sentence → `/skill` with args), or NanoClaw-level event routing (message classifier → skill), and `disable-model-invocation` for side-effectful chains.\n2. **No native chaining/orchestration** — skills don't declare dependencies or call each other; composition happens implicitly via the model. A \"role pack\" therefore needs a top-level router skill (or CLAUDE.md section) mapping core moments → member skills, or plugin packaging to bundle them.\n3. **No state** — skill content is injected once, not re-read; compaction can truncate to 5k tokens per skill / 25k total. Multi-turn workflow state must live in workspace files (NanoClaw already has this pattern with CLAUDE.local.md) or checklists the agent writes.\n4. **No built-in eval/QA or paid distribution**; open marketplaces are a proven malware vector (ClawHub) — curate and sign your packs.\n\n**(b) State of the art in packaging a ROLE (not a task) as an installable artifact?**\n\nImmature — this is the gap. Closest existing models: Relay.app's *agent = owner of a set of playbooks*; Relevance AI's *workforce = playbook as a coordinated agent team* plus role-organized template marketplace; Dust's *Skills = shared expertise modules across agents*; Claude Code *plugins* (bundle of namespaced skills + commands + hooks + MCP config) — technically the best \"role pack\" container in the Anthropic ecosystem, since one install delivers a coherent set of skills. Nobody ships a portable \"AI Chief-of-Staff methodology, v1.2\" that installs across platforms; everything is walled-garden. A role pack = plugin{router + N core-moment skills + calibration file + scripts} on the Agent SDK would be genuinely novel packaging on an open substrate.\n\n**(c) What calibration/personalization mechanisms exist?**\n\nExisting products calibrate shallowly: connect apps, edit instructions, upload brand docs, refine via chat (Lindy/Zapier/Relevance/Beam). The strongest documented mechanisms are Anthropic's own: layered memory files (user/project/local + auto-memory), interactive `/init`, the **AskUserQuestion interview pattern**, and the **Claude-A/Claude-B skill-refinement loop** where observed failures get folded back into the skill. The winning design that no one has shipped: generic role pack + structured onboarding interview whose answers compile into a per-user calibration file (CLAUDE.local.md-style) that all the pack's skills reference, plus an observe-and-refine loop that proposes skill edits after real usage — every ingredient is documented and proven separately; the combination is open territory.\n\nSources (primary): [Anthropic engineering — Agent Skills](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills) · [Agent Skills spec](https://agentskills.io/specification) · [Skill authoring best practices](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices) · [Claude Code skills](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills) · [Agent SDK skills](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/skills) · [anthropics/skills](https://github.com/anthropics/skills) · [Skills announcement](https://claude.com/blog/skills) · [Simon Willison](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Oct/16/claude-skills/) · [Lindy templates](https://docs.lindy.ai/fundamentals/lindy-101/templates) · [Dust Skills changelog](https://docs.dust.tt/changelog/skills-share-expertise-across-all-your-agents) · [Relevance workforce](https://relevanceai.com/workforce) · [Relevance templates](https://relevanceai.com/templates) · [Zapier Agents guide](https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-agents-guide/) · [Relay.app agents](https://docs.relay.app/agents/agents) · [Beam templates](https://beam.ai/agents) · [CrewAI agents](https://docs.crewai.com/en/concepts/agents) · [SaaStr on Relevance](https://www.saastr.com/saastr-ai-app-of-the-week-relevance-ai-the-platform-where-ops-teams-build-entire-ai-workforces-without-writing-a-line-of-code/) · [n8n workflows](https://n8n.io/workflows/) · [Hacker News on ClawHub](https://thehackernews.com/2026/02/researchers-find-341-malicious-clawhub.html) · [Unit 42](https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/openclaw-ai-supply-chain-risk/) · [Claude Code best practices](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices) · [Tango](https://www.tango.ai/) · [Optexity](https://optexity.com/blog/the-best-production-ready-browser-automation-platforms-in-2026)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af409a47fc9079e7d (use SendMessage with to: 'af409a47fc9079e7d', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 89030\ntool_uses: 27\nduration_ms: 358475</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"a9a07523-47ec-41d6-9cee-b30d50858582","timestamp":"2026-07-02T15:14:11.984Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Context: a startup builds personal AI work-agents on the Anthropic Claude Agent SDK (NanoClaw stack, agents chat via Telegram). Their differentiation: encoding the METHODOLOGY of a profession — multi-step workflows a professional runs at each \"core moment\" of their job (e.g., end of a sales call → structured summary → CRM update → follow-up email → team brief → next-step reminder), packaged so the user says one sentence and the whole chain runs. Research what ALREADY EXISTS (2025-2026) that they can adopt or learn from:\n\n1. **Anthropic Agent Skills** — the SKILL.md format (agentskills.io / anthropic docs / claude skills spec): exact structure (frontmatter, progressive disclosure, scripts, references), how skills compose, how Claude Code/Agent SDK discovers and loads them, best practices for authoring multi-step workflow skills, the /skills marketplace situation (skills.claude.com? anthropics/skills GitHub repo, third-party marketplaces like ClawHub for OpenClaw, smithery, skillsmp). What makes a GOOD skill per Anthropic's engineering blog (\"Equipping agents for the real world with Agent Skills\")?\n2. **Workflow/playbook encoding in agent products**: how do Lindy.ai, Dust.tt, Relevance AI, Gumloop, n8n (AI agents), Zapier Agents/Central, Relay.app model \"a multi-step professional workflow triggered by an event or one command\"? What's their unit of packaging (template? recipe? agent team?), and do they have template libraries per role?\n3. **\"Teach the agent your process\" mechanisms**: products where users teach an agent an SOP by demonstration, interview, or document ingestion — e.g., Scribe/Tango (SOP capture), any agent products that learn from watching or from a conversational interview. Also Anthropic's own patterns (CLAUDE.md conventions, /init style bootstrapping, memory).\n4. **Role-based agent template libraries**: who ships pre-built role packs (\"AI SDR\", \"AI recruiter\", \"AI executive assistant\") as CONFIGURABLE TEMPLATES rather than closed products? e.g., Relevance AI's \"AI workforce\" templates, Lindy templates, CrewAI templates, Beam.ai, MindStudio, Voiceflow. How do they handle per-user calibration of a generic role template?\n5. Evidence on what works: any public numbers/case studies on adoption of skill marketplaces, template libraries (which roles/templates get most traction — sales? recruiting? support?).\n\nKey questions: (a) Is Anthropic's SKILL.md the right substrate for \"role methodology packs\" and what are its limits (triggering, chaining, state)? (b) What is the state of the art in packaging a ROLE (not a task) as an installable artifact? (c) What calibration/personalization mechanisms exist for adapting a generic role template to an individual?\n\nIRON RULES: name products precisely, link sources per claim, don't fabricate, mark uncertainty explicitly. Structured English report. Your final message IS the deliverable.","agentId":"af409a47fc9079e7d","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Research Report: Encoding Professional Methodology into Agent Artifacts — What Exists (2025–2026)\n\nScope: what the NanoClaw-based startup can adopt or learn from, across (1) Anthropic Agent Skills, (2) workflow/playbook encoding in agent products, (3) \"teach the agent your process\" mechanisms, (4) role-based template libraries, (5) adoption evidence — then answers to the three key questions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Anthropic Agent Skills — the SKILL.md substrate\n\n### 1.1 Exact format (open spec)\n\nAnthropic published Agent Skills as an **open standard** (spec at [agentskills.io/specification](https://agentskills.io/specification), reference repo [github.com/agentskills/agentskills](https://github.com/agentskills/agentskills)). Per third-party reporting, the open standard was released Dec 18, 2025 and has been adopted by 26+ platforms including OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor, VS Code ([agensi.io overview](https://www.agensi.io/learn/agent-skills-open-standard) — adoption count is that source's claim, I could not independently verify \"26+\").\n\nA skill is a directory whose only required file is `SKILL.md`:\n\n```\nskill-name/\n├── SKILL.md          # required: YAML frontmatter + markdown instructions\n├── scripts/          # optional executable code (run, not loaded into context)\n├── references/       # optional docs loaded on demand\n└── assets/           # optional templates/data\n```\n\nFrontmatter fields (from the [spec](https://agentskills.io/specification)):\n- `name` (required, ≤64 chars, lowercase/digits/hyphens, must match directory name)\n- `description` (required, ≤1024 chars — must say *what it does AND when to use it*, with trigger keywords)\n- optional: `license`, `compatibility` (≤500 chars, env requirements), `metadata` (arbitrary k/v, e.g. author/version), `allowed-tools` (experimental, pre-approved tools)\n\n**Progressive disclosure** is the core design ([Anthropic engineering post](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills)):\n1. **Level 1 — metadata** (~100 tokens/skill): all skills' name+description injected into system prompt at startup.\n2. **Level 2 — SKILL.md body** (<5,000 tokens / <500 lines recommended): read via filesystem tool only when the model judges the skill relevant.\n3. **Level 3+ — bundled files**: references/scripts loaded or executed on demand; \"the amount of context that can be bundled into a skill is effectively unbounded\" because scripts execute without entering context.\n\n### 1.2 What makes a GOOD skill (Anthropic's own guidance)\n\nFrom the [engineering blog](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills) and the [skill authoring best-practices doc](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices) — the best-practices page is the single most relevant document for your \"methodology pack\" idea:\n\n- **Conciseness**: \"Claude is already very smart\" — only add context the model lacks; every loaded token competes with conversation history.\n- **Degrees of freedom matched to task fragility**: high freedom (heuristic text) for judgment tasks like code review; medium (parameterized pseudocode); low freedom (\"run exactly this script, do not modify\") for fragile sequences. This maps directly onto professional methodology: judgment steps vs. deterministic steps.\n- **Workflow + checklist pattern**: for complex multi-step processes, embed a copyable checklist the agent checks off as it progresses (their canonical multi-step workflow pattern — exactly the \"core moment chain\" shape).\n- **Feedback loops**: run validator → fix → repeat; \"plan-validate-execute\" with machine-verifiable intermediate artifacts (e.g. a `changes.json` validated by script before execution).\n- **Template pattern & examples pattern**: bundle output templates (strict or flexible) and input→output example pairs.\n- **Conditional workflow pattern**: decision points routing to sub-workflows, pushed into separate files if large.\n- **Description is the trigger**: written in third person, with explicit \"Use when…\" phrasing and keywords; Claude picks from potentially 100+ skills by description alone.\n- **Evaluation-driven development**: build 3+ evals *before* writing the skill; baseline without the skill; iterate. There is no built-in eval runner — you roll your own.\n- **Iterate with Claude itself**: \"Claude A\" authors/refines the skill, \"Claude B\" (fresh instance) tests it; capture successful approaches and mistakes back into the skill.\n- Anti-patterns: vague names (`helper`, `utils`), too many alternative approaches, nested reference chains >1 level deep, time-sensitive info, magic constants in scripts.\n\n### 1.3 Discovery/loading in Claude Code and the Agent SDK\n\n- **Agent SDK** ([docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/skills)): skills load from `.claude/skills/` (project) and `~/.claude/skills/` (user) only if `settingSources`/`setting_sources` includes those sources (SDK loads no filesystem settings by default), and `\"Skill\"` must be in `allowed_tools`. Plugin-bundled skills are namespaced `plugin-name:skill-name` ([plugins in the SDK](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/plugins)). Directly relevant to NanoClaw since it runs on the Agent SDK.\n- **Claude Code** ([skills doc](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills)) extends the open standard with features that matter a lot for methodology packs:\n  - **Invocation control**: `disable-model-invocation: true` (only user can trigger, e.g. `/deploy` — for side-effectful workflows where the human controls timing) and `user-invocable: false` (background knowledge only the model loads). Default: both can invoke.\n  - `when_to_use` field — extra trigger phrases appended to description (combined cap 1,536 chars in the listing).\n  - **Arguments**: `$ARGUMENTS`, `$0/$1`, named `arguments:` — a skill is parameterizable like a command (\"one sentence → whole chain runs\" is literally `/end-of-call <client>`).\n  - **Subagent execution**: `context: fork` + `agent:` runs the skill in a forked subagent context.\n  - `allowed-tools` (pre-approved permissions while active), `disallowed-tools`, `model`, `effort`, per-skill `hooks`, `paths` (activate only for matching files), dynamic context injection via `` !`command` `` blocks.\n  - **State/lifecycle limits**: invoked skill content enters the conversation once and is *not re-read*; on auto-compaction only the first 5,000 tokens of each recent skill are re-attached under a combined 25,000-token budget — older skills can drop out entirely. There is **no built-in persistent state machine**: state across a multi-step workflow must live in the conversation, in files the skill writes, or in checklists.\n  - Custom slash commands have been merged into skills (a `.claude/commands/deploy.md` and a skill are now the same mechanism).\n\n### 1.4 Marketplace situation\n\n- **Official**: [anthropics/skills GitHub repo](https://github.com/anthropics/skills) (doubles as a Claude Code plugin marketplace via `marketplace.json`; install e.g. `/plugin install document-skills@anthropic-agent-skills`); a curated [anthropics/claude-plugins-official](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-plugins-official) directory; and in the Claude apps a **unified in-product directory** (Customize > Skills > Browse skills) featuring partner-built skills from Notion, Figma, Atlassian etc. ([help center](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/14328846-browse-skills-connectors-and-plugins-in-one-directory), [skills announcement](https://claude.com/blog/skills)). I found no evidence that a standalone `skills.claude.com` site exists — the directory lives inside the product; treat \"skills.claude.com\" as unconfirmed. **No official paid marketplace exists**; Anthropic has only signaled interest in community contributions ([claudeskills.info](https://claudeskills.info/skills/) reporting).\n- **Third-party**: [awesome-claude-skills](https://github.com/travisvn/awesome-claude-skills), [claudeskills.info](https://claudeskills.info/skills/) (~140+ free skills), [skillsdirectory.com](https://www.skillsdirectory.com/), various personal marketplaces on GitHub. All small/curated.\n- **Cautionary tale — ClawHub (OpenClaw's skill marketplace)**: grew from ~2,857 to 10,700+ skills in weeks, and security audits found **341 malicious skills** (Koi Security, later 824+ as the marketplace grew), typosquatting, scanner-evasion (payloads hidden in oversized READMEs), and ranking manipulation ([The Hacker News](https://thehackernews.com/2026/02/researchers-find-341-malicious-clawhub.html), [Unit 42](https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/openclaw-ai-supply-chain-risk/), [Dark Reading](https://www.darkreading.com/cyber-risk/malicious-openclaw-skills-clawhub-threaten-ai-supply-chain), [Silverfort](https://www.silverfort.com/blog/clawhub-vulnerability-enables-attackers-to-manipulate-rankings-to-become-the-number-one-skill/)). Lesson: skills are prompt-injection + supply-chain surface; a curated, signed, first-party pack library beats an open marketplace for a trust-sensitive product.\n- **Expert assessment**: Simon Willison, [\"Claude Skills are awesome, maybe a bigger deal than MCP\"](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Oct/16/claude-skills/) (Oct 16, 2025): skills are \"markdown with a tiny bit of YAML,\" trivially shareable, and outsource the hard parts to the harness — vs. MCP's protocol weight and token cost.\n\n---\n\n## 2. How workflow/agent products package \"a multi-step professional workflow\"\n\n| Product | Unit of packaging | Trigger model | Template library |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lindy.ai** | A \"Lindy\" = agent with trigger + steps (flow-chart style), built in natural language; Lindies can call each other (\"societies of agents\") | Event triggers (new email, call ended, form, schedule); multi-trigger workflows, loops, conditions | 50+ pre-built templates organized by use case (sales, support, meetings, email) and industry ([templates docs](https://docs.lindy.ai/fundamentals/lindy-101/templates), [template categories](https://www.lindy.ai/template-categories/workflow-automation)) |\n| **Dust.tt** | An agent = plain-English **instructions** + tools + company data sources; new **\"Skills\"** feature packages instructions+knowledge+tools into reusable modules shared across all workspace agents — update once, all agents benefit ([changelog](https://docs.dust.tt/changelog/skills-share-expertise-across-all-your-agents)) | @-mention in Slack/chat, native triggers (schedule/webhook), or external orchestrators (Zapier/Make/n8n) ([blog](https://dust.tt/blog/automate-dust-agents-workflow-tools)) | Team-oriented agent examples rather than a big public template store; semantic layer over company knowledge is the differentiator ([product](https://dust.tt/home/product)) |\n| **Relevance AI** | **\"Workforce\"** = a team of agents that \"automate entire playbooks — triggered by events or signals,\" each agent owning a step and handing off context ([workforce page](https://relevanceai.com/workforce)); flagship Bosh (BDR) is a multi-agent system with delegating sub-agents ([Bosh docs](https://relevanceai.com/docs/agent/agent-examples/bosh-bdr-agent)) | Event/signal triggers or chat | Public [agent marketplace/templates](https://relevanceai.com/templates) organized **by role** (SDR, AE, RevOps, recruiter…) and by task |\n| **Zapier Agents** (successor to Zapier Central) | **\"Behavior\"** = trigger + natural-language instructions + connected apps; an agent bundles several behaviors + data sources | Any of Zapier's 7,000+ app triggers, chat, buttons, or invocation from a Zap ([build an agent](https://help.zapier.com/hc/en-us/articles/24393442652557-Build-an-agent-in-Zapier-Agents), [trigger setup](https://help.zapier.com/hc/en-us/articles/45394909914381-Set-up-your-agent-s-trigger)) | Growing template library with preset instructions+apps per business function (Lead Qualification & Scoring, Call Follow-Up Assistant, Candidate Ranking…) — editable after instantiation ([agents guide](https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-agents-guide/)) |\n| **Relay.app** | **\"Playbook\"** = trigger + step sequence with human-in-the-loop steps; an \"Agent\" is an AI teammate that *owns a set of workflows* (role = bundle of playbooks — closest existing model to your \"role pack\") ([docs](https://docs.relay.app/agents/agents), [how it works](https://www.relay.app/how-it-works)) | App triggers, schedules, manual; \"mini AI agents\" as steps inside deterministic flows ([blog](https://www.relay.app/blog/how-to-add-a-mini-ai-agent-to-your-workflow)) | Playbooks are explicitly \"repeatable, teachable processes\" — templated, shared, refined |\n| **Gumloop** | Flow (node graph); agents pick tools mid-task via MCP | Triggers via cloud platform, browser extension | Deep template library by domain: HR/hiring, sales/CRM, legal, SEO, docs ([comparison](https://www.gumloop.com/vs/n8n)) |\n| **n8n** | Workflow JSON with an AI Agent node + sub-nodes (model, memory, tools) | Any node trigger; chat requires a separate Chat Trigger node | ~10,400 community templates, ~7,100 in the AI category ([n8n workflows](https://n8n.io/workflows/), [AI category](https://n8n.io/workflows/categories/ai/)) |\n\n**Takeaway for the startup:** the market's dominant unit is *trigger + instructions + connected tools* (\"behavior\"/\"playbook\"), with role-level packaging appearing only as (a) Relay's \"agent owns a set of workflows,\" (b) Relevance's multi-agent \"workforce playbooks,\" and (c) Dust's cross-agent \"Skills\" modules. Nobody yet ships a portable, open-format *role methodology pack*; everyone's packaging is platform-locked. Anthropic's SKILL.md is the only open, portable substrate in the list.\n\n---\n\n## 3. \"Teach the agent your process\" mechanisms\n\n- **Demonstration capture (SOP tools)**: [Scribe](https://scribe.com/library/scribe-vs-tango) and [Tango](https://www.tango.ai/) record a user performing a workflow in the browser and auto-generate step-by-step guides with screenshots; Tango now ships \"Tango Agents\" on top of captured SOPs (product details thin — uncertain how agentic it really is). These produce *human-readable* SOPs, which are exactly the raw material a SKILL.md compiler could ingest.\n- **Demonstration → automation**: [Optexity](https://optexity.com/blog/the-best-production-ready-browser-automation-platforms-in-2026) generates deterministic browser automations from a screen recording (\"demonstrate once, deploy via API\"). The broader 2026 agentic-browser landscape treats \"teach mode\" (show once, agent repeats) as an established pattern ([landscape guide](https://nohacks.co/blog/agentic-browser-landscape-2026)). I did not find a mature *chat-agent* product that learns a professional's methodology purely from watching — this appears to be genuinely open ground.\n- **Interview-based teaching (Anthropic patterns)** — most directly adoptable for NanoClaw:\n  - **`/init`** bootstraps CLAUDE.md by having the agent explore the environment; an enhanced interactive multi-phase init flow exists (`CLAUDE_CODE_NEW_INIT=1`) ([best practices](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices)).\n  - **AskUserQuestion interview pattern**: Anthropic's own recommended practice is \"have Claude interview you first\" using the AskUserQuestion tool before big work — the exact mechanism for calibrating a generic role pack to an individual ([best practices](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices)).\n  - **Memory layering**: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (personal), `./CLAUDE.md` (shared), `CLAUDE.local.md` (private), plus auto-memory (MEMORY.md) written back autonomously ([memory guide](https://joseparreogarcia.substack.com/p/claude-code-memory-explained), [memory tool docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool)). Community consensus: keep CLAUDE.md ~80–120 high-signal lines; procedures belong in skills, facts in CLAUDE.md ([Claude Code skills doc](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills): \"create a skill… when a section of CLAUDE.md has grown into a procedure rather than a fact\").\n  - **Skill self-authoring**: Anthropic explicitly recommends \"ask Claude to capture its successful approaches and common mistakes into reusable context and code within a skill,\" and states plans for agents to create/edit/evaluate skills autonomously ([engineering post](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills)). So the \"teach by doing it once together, then crystallize into a skill\" loop (Claude A authors / Claude B tests) is Anthropic's own documented workflow — adopt it verbatim.\n- **Zapier Central** (predecessor of Agents) let users teach \"behaviors\" conversationally and correct the agent's drafts over time ([community post](https://community.zapier.com/p/zapier-central)); Zapier Agents kept the natural-language instructions + editable behaviors model.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role-based agent template libraries\n\n- **Relevance AI** — most explicit \"role as product\": named AI teammates (Bosh the BDR/SDR, Apla account research, Lima lifecycle marketing) plus a [template marketplace](https://relevanceai.com/templates) organized by role pages (e.g. [SDR agent templates](https://relevanceai.com/agent-templates-roles/sales-development-representative-sdr-ai-agents-1), recruiter templates). Templates instantiate into your workspace and are calibrated by connecting CRM/data sources and editing instructions.\n- **Beam.ai** — [library of production-ready agent templates](https://beam.ai/agents), each shipping with configured workflows, integration setups, and setup guidance; 1,500+ integrations ([template blog](https://beam.ai/agentic-insights/5-ready-to-use-ai-agent-templates-that-save-40-hours-per-week)).\n- **Lindy.ai** — 50+ role/use-case templates instantiated as editable Lindies ([docs](https://docs.lindy.ai/fundamentals/lindy-101/templates)).\n- **CrewAI** — role-based by construction (`role`, `goal`, `backstory` per agent, YAML-config recommended) ([agents docs](https://docs.crewai.com/en/concepts/agents)); recruiting crews (sourcing → screening → matching → evaluation agents) are a canonical community project ([ProjectPro](https://www.projectpro.io/article/crew-ai-projects-ideas-and-examples/1117)) — but it's a dev framework, not an end-user template product.\n- **MindStudio** — no-code visual builder with templates and NL-to-workflow generation ([vs CrewAI](https://www.mindstudio.ai/blog/mindstudio-vs-crewai)). **Voiceflow**: conversational-agent templates, mostly support/voice — I did not verify role-pack depth there (uncertain).\n- **Calibration mechanisms observed across all of these** (thin everywhere): (1) connect your data sources/apps (the template's variables), (2) edit the natural-language instructions after instantiation, (3) upload brand/knowledge docs, (4) chat-based refinement of drafts. Nobody I found does a structured *methodology interview* (\"walk me through what you do after a sales call, what does 'qualified' mean for you, who gets the brief\") that compiles answers into the template's parameters. Jotform/Pipefy market \"clarifying questions\" onboarding for narrow customer-onboarding agents ([Pipefy](https://www.pipefy.com/blog/customize-customer-onboarding-ai-agents/)), but that's for end-customer flows, not professional calibration.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Adoption evidence (what gets traction)\n\nHard public numbers are scarce; here's what exists:\n\n- **n8n**: ~10,400 community templates; ~7,100 in the AI category; per one tracker, **AI-agent templates ≈78% of new submissions** (30-day window, May 2026) and multi-agent templates doubled in two months to ~¼ of the AI category ([n8n workflows](https://n8n.io/workflows/), [connectsafely analysis](https://connectsafely.ai/articles/n8n-templates-workflow-automation-examples) — third-party analysis, treat percentages as approximate).\n- **Relevance AI**: $37M+ raised (Series B $24M, May 2025); **40,000 agents built in a single January**; customers incl. Canva, Databricks, KPMG, Activision ([SaaStr](https://www.saastr.com/saastr-ai-app-of-the-week-relevance-ai-the-platform-where-ops-teams-build-entire-ai-workforces-without-writing-a-line-of-code/)). Its flagship, most-marketed template is a *sales* role (Bosh BDR) — consistent with sales/SDR being the wedge.\n- **ClawHub**: ~2,800 → 10,700+ skills within weeks of launch — demonstrates huge appetite for installable agent skills, and equally the security downside ([eSecurity Planet](https://www.esecurityplanet.com/threats/hundreds-of-malicious-skills-found-in-openclaws-clawhub/)).\n- **Zapier's public template lists** skew to sales/revops (lead qualification, call follow-up) and recruiting (candidate ranking) ([guide](https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-agents-guide/)).\n- **Which roles win**: no rigorous public breakdown found. Directional evidence (what vendors lead with, template category sizes) points to **sales/SDR first, support second, recruiting third** — but I'm marking this as inference, not measured fact.\n- Gartner (via [IBM](https://www.ibm.com/think/ai-agents) roundup): 40% of enterprise apps to embed task-specific agents by end-2026, up from <5% in 2025 — context, not product-level evidence.\n- No public adoption numbers exist yet for Anthropic's skills directory or the anthropics/skills repo beyond GitHub activity (uncertain).\n\n---\n\n## Answers to the key questions\n\n**(a) Is SKILL.md the right substrate for \"role methodology packs\"? What are its limits?**\n\nYes, with a composition layer on top. Strengths: open standard, portable across 20+ harnesses, progressive disclosure keeps a fat methodology pack token-cheap, `scripts/` gives deterministic steps (CRM writes, formatting), `assets/` gives output templates, Claude Code/SDK extensions give you arguments (`/end-of-call acme`), invocation control, per-skill tool permissions, and subagent forking. Anthropic's checklist/feedback-loop/conditional-workflow patterns in the [best-practices doc](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices) are literally \"professional methodology encoding\" patterns.\n\nLimits to engineer around:\n1. **Triggering is probabilistic** — it hinges entirely on the 1,024-char description (1,536 incl. `when_to_use` in Claude Code). For \"core moments\" you should not rely on ambient triggering: use explicit invocation (one sentence → `/skill` with args), or NanoClaw-level event routing (message classifier → skill), and `disable-model-invocation` for side-effectful chains.\n2. **No native chaining/orchestration** — skills don't declare dependencies or call each other; composition happens implicitly via the model. A \"role pack\" therefore needs a top-level router skill (or CLAUDE.md section) mapping core moments → member skills, or plugin packaging to bundle them.\n3. **No state** — skill content is injected once, not re-read; compaction can truncate to 5k tokens per skill / 25k total. Multi-turn workflow state must live in workspace files (NanoClaw already has this pattern with CLAUDE.local.md) or checklists the agent writes.\n4. **No built-in eval/QA or paid distribution**; open marketplaces are a proven malware vector (ClawHub) — curate and sign your packs.\n\n**(b) State of the art in packaging a ROLE (not a task) as an installable artifact?**\n\nImmature — this is the gap. Closest existing models: Relay.app's *agent = owner of a set of playbooks*; Relevance AI's *workforce = playbook as a coordinated agent team* plus role-organized template marketplace; Dust's *Skills = shared expertise modules across agents*; Claude Code *plugins* (bundle of namespaced skills + commands + hooks + MCP config) — technically the best \"role pack\" container in the Anthropic ecosystem, since one install delivers a coherent set of skills. Nobody ships a portable \"AI Chief-of-Staff methodology, v1.2\" that installs across platforms; everything is walled-garden. A role pack = plugin{router + N core-moment skills + calibration file + scripts} on the Agent SDK would be genuinely novel packaging on an open substrate.\n\n**(c) What calibration/personalization mechanisms exist?**\n\nExisting products calibrate shallowly: connect apps, edit instructions, upload brand docs, refine via chat (Lindy/Zapier/Relevance/Beam). The strongest documented mechanisms are Anthropic's own: layered memory files (user/project/local + auto-memory), interactive `/init`, the **AskUserQuestion interview pattern**, and the **Claude-A/Claude-B skill-refinement loop** where observed failures get folded back into the skill. The winning design that no one has shipped: generic role pack + structured onboarding interview whose answers compile into a per-user calibration file (CLAUDE.local.md-style) that all the pack's skills reference, plus an observe-and-refine loop that proposes skill edits after real usage — every ingredient is documented and proven separately; the combination is open territory.\n\nSources (primary): [Anthropic engineering — Agent Skills](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills) · [Agent Skills spec](https://agentskills.io/specification) · [Skill authoring best practices](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices) · [Claude Code skills](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills) · [Agent SDK skills](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/skills) · [anthropics/skills](https://github.com/anthropics/skills) · [Skills announcement](https://claude.com/blog/skills) · [Simon Willison](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Oct/16/claude-skills/) · [Lindy templates](https://docs.lindy.ai/fundamentals/lindy-101/templates) · [Dust Skills changelog](https://docs.dust.tt/changelog/skills-share-expertise-across-all-your-agents) · [Relevance workforce](https://relevanceai.com/workforce) · [Relevance templates](https://relevanceai.com/templates) · [Zapier Agents guide](https://zapier.com/blog/zapier-agents-guide/) · [Relay.app agents](https://docs.relay.app/agents/agents) · [Beam templates](https://beam.ai/agents) · [CrewAI agents](https://docs.crewai.com/en/concepts/agents) · [SaaStr on Relevance](https://www.saastr.com/saastr-ai-app-of-the-week-relevance-ai-the-platform-where-ops-teams-build-entire-ai-workforces-without-writing-a-line-of-code/) · [n8n workflows](https://n8n.io/workflows/) · [Hacker News on ClawHub](https://thehackernews.com/2026/02/researchers-find-341-malicious-clawhub.html) · [Unit 42](https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/openclaw-ai-supply-chain-risk/) · [Claude Code best practices](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/best-practices) · [Tango](https://www.tango.ai/) · [Optexity](https://optexity.com/blog/the-best-production-ready-browser-automation-platforms-in-2026)"}],"resolvedModel":"claude-fable-5","totalDurationMs":358475,"totalTokens":89030,"totalToolUseCount":27,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7268,"cache_read_input_tokens":71354,"output_tokens":10406,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7268},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10406,"cache_read_input_tokens":71354,"c