{"type":"ai-title","aiTitle":"בדיקת פונקציונליות של מודל חדש","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73da"}
{"parentUuid":"aa9ca72a-9d08-4bd6-bced-527da6681c65","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"42d624f0-51b1-4086-a07f-c40f6806faec","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RCKZVQA5rRAeCeofALV9NK","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Buy-vs-Build: Agent–Tool Integration Layers (researched July 2026)\n\n## Executive summary\n\nThe market has matured fast: there are now at least four credible managed platforms (Composio, Pipedream Connect, Arcade.dev, Klavis) whose core architecture matches your OneCLI principle — **tokens live in the platform's vault, the agent only sends a `user_id` + tool call, and end-user auth is a hosted link click**. Given that, rebuilding a general credential-injecting proxy for Gmail/Calendar/CRMs in-house is hard to justify for a pilot. Recommended shortlist for per-end-user auth with Telegram users: **Pipedream Connect** (broadest coverage + Connect Link + predictable per-user pricing), **Arcade.dev** (purest \"auth for agents\" model, best security posture story), **Composio** (cheapest, biggest agent-native catalog, but suffered a real credential breach in May 2026). Details and caveats below.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Composio\n\n- **Coverage:** Marketing figures vary between \"500+\", \"800+ toolkits\" and \"900+ SaaS integrations\" ([composio.dev/toolkits](https://composio.dev/toolkits), [composio.dev](https://composio.dev/)). Covers Gmail, Google Calendar, HubSpot, Salesforce, Pipedrive, Slack, Notion.\n- **Auth:** Fully managed OAuth with token refresh. You create an \"Auth Config\" per toolkit; `connected_accounts.link(user_id, auth_config_id)` returns a **hosted `redirect_url` you send to the user — a pure link click**, ideal for Telegram ([docs](https://docs.composio.dev/docs/authenticating-tools)). You can use Composio's shared OAuth apps or bring your own client IDs.\n- **MCP:** Yes — hosted MCP servers per toolkit plus tool-execution SDKs (Python/TS) with adapters for OpenAI, LangChain, CrewAI, Vercel AI SDK, Claude ([composio.dev/toolkits](https://composio.dev/toolkits)).\n- **Pricing** ([composio.dev/pricing](https://composio.dev/pricing)): Free = 20k tool calls/mo; $29/mo = 200k calls (+$0.299/1k over); $229/mo = 2M calls; Enterprise = custom, VPC/on-prem option. Notably **priced per tool call, not per user**.\n- **Security model:** Tokens stored server-side in Composio's vault, encrypted at rest; SOC 2 Type 2. Agent never holds tokens.\n- **Criticisms/limitations:** **The big one: the May 2026 breach.** Disclosed May 21, 2026 — an attacker got in via a compromised employee Gmail OAuth token, achieved code execution in the tool-execution sandbox, and exfiltrated ~5,241 API keys and ~5,001 GitHub OAuth tokens (~0.3% of active connections) ([Composio's disclosure](https://composio.dev/blog/composio-may-2026-security-incident), [Material Security analysis](https://material.security/resources/the-composio-breach-one-token-10242-doors), [P0 Security analysis](https://p0.dev/blog/the-composio-breach-lets-stop-blaming-the-agents/)). This is the concrete downside of centralizing everyone's tokens in a third party. Community has also historically complained about SDK/API churn (v1→v3 breaking changes) — lower-severity, but real.\n\n## 2. Pipedream Connect / MCP\n\n- **Coverage:** ~3,000+ apps, 10,000+ pre-built tools — the largest catalog of the managed options ([docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/mcp)).\n- **Auth:** Managed OAuth per **`external_user_id`** (your user identifier). **Connect Link** is a Pipedream-hosted URL (no frontend SDK, no iframe) you generate per user and send over \"email, SMS, or direct redirect\" — i.e., paste into Telegram; scoped to that user, expires in 4h ([Connect Link docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/managed-auth/connect-link)). This is exactly your flow.\n- **MCP:** Dedicated MCP servers per app per user; \"credentials … never directly exposed to AI models\"; also a Connect **proxy** endpoint for raw API calls with injected auth — closest analog to OneCLI's proxy semantics ([docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/mcp)).\n- **Pricing:** Free in development mode. Production (per search results citing their pricing): **~$99/mo including 100 external users, then ~$2/external user/mo**, plus credits for tool executions ([pricing docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/pricing), [community thread confirming ~$2/user](https://pipedream.com/community/t/clarification-on-pricing-is-the-additional-2-for-an-extra-external-user-charged-monthly-or-is-it-a-one-time-fee/13692)). They bill only \"active\" users (ones you make calls for) ([community](https://pipedream.com/community/t/what-is-the-pricing-for-using-managed-oauth-with-mcp-server-in-a-macos-app-with-1000-users-and-5-connected-services/13480)). *Estimate — confirm with sales; per-user rate may have changed.*\n- **Security model:** Tokens encrypted at rest in Pipedream; per-user credential isolation; agent never holds tokens. SOC 2 (per their site; verify current report).\n- **Limitations:** Credit accounting (1 credit/30s compute) makes tool-call cost slightly opaque; per-user fee dominates at your scale (a user in that community thread estimated **~$2k/mo at 1,000 users**, consistent with $2/user).\n\n## 3. Zapier MCP / AI Actions\n\n- **Coverage:** 8,000–9,000 apps, ~30,000 actions ([zapier.com/mcp](https://zapier.com/mcp)).\n- **Auth & multi-tenancy — the dealbreaker:** Zapier MCP is designed around **a Zapier account owner's** connected apps. For your SaaS-like setup, community guidance is that *each end user needs their own Zapier account* ([Zapier community](https://community.zapier.com/how-do-i-3/multi-tenancy-functionality-49496)); there is no embedded per-external-user credential model like Pipedream's. Tokens live on Zapier's servers.\n- **Pricing/limits:** Each successful tool call = **2 Zapier tasks**, drawn from the plan's task quota; no per-session rate limit; failed calls free ([usage docs](https://docs.zapier.com/mcp/usage/overview)). Free plan ≈100 tasks/mo, Professional ≈750+ ([zapier.com/pricing](https://zapier.com/pricing)). Enterprise plans have MCP off by default.\n- **Verdict:** Great for one power user connecting their own stack to Claude; **wrong shape and wrong economics for 100–1,000 end users doing tens of actions/day** (900k calls = 1.8M tasks/mo — five figures monthly, *estimate*).\n\n## 4. Arcade.dev\n\n- **What it is:** \"MCP runtime for production AI agents\" founded 2024 by Alex Salazar (ex-Okta) — the most explicitly **agent-auth-first** platform ([arcade.dev](https://www.arcade.dev/), [WorkOS overview](https://workos.com/blog/what-is-arcade-dev-an-llm-tool-calling-platform)).\n- **How auth works:** You call `tools.authorize(tool, user_id)` → Arcade returns an **authorization URL** (link click for the user) → after consent, Arcade Engine stores and refreshes tokens; all subsequent tool executions just pass `user_id`. \"Developers never see tokens directly\" ([auth docs](https://docs.arcade.dev/en/home/auth/auth-tool-calling), [how Arcade helps](https://docs.arcade.dev/home/auth/how-arcade-helps)). Agents act **as the user** (delegated OAuth), not as a bot/service account. Custom Python tools still get Arcade-managed tokens.\n- **Coverage:** Pre-built MCP toolkits for Google Workspace, Slack, Microsoft, Salesforce, GitHub, Notion, etc. — a **curated catalog, meaningfully smaller than Composio/Pipedream** (roughly dozens of toolkits, not thousands of apps). Check the catalog for Pipedrive/HubSpot specifics before committing.\n- **Pricing** ([arcade.dev/pricing](https://www.arcade.dev/pricing)): Free tier (1,000 standard tool executions, 100 \"user challenges\" = auth flows, 1 hosted MCP server). Growth: $25/mo + $0.01/standard execution beyond 2,000 (+$0.05/auth challenge beyond 600, $0.50/\"pro\" execution). Enterprise: custom, dedicated tenant isolation, RBAC, SSO, audit logs. Startup program for <100-employee companies.\n- **Security model:** Tokens in Arcade Engine only; agent never holds them. Enterprise tier offers dedicated tenant isolation; they've historically offered self-hosted/VPC engine deployment (*verify current availability with them*).\n\n## 5. The rest, briefly\n\n| Product | What it is | Open source? | When it fits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **[Nango](https://nango.dev/)** | Auth/integration infra for 800+ APIs: managed OAuth, token refresh, request proxy, per-tenant connections ([GitHub](https://github.com/NangoHQ/nango)) | Yes (Elastic License; self-hostable) | You want to keep building your own tool layer but outsource *only* OAuth plumbing. Pricing: Free 10 connections; Starter from $50/mo (20 conns); Growth from $500/mo (100 conns); **$1/connection/mo overage** + $0.10/1k proxy requests ([pricing](https://nango.dev/pricing)). Note: Nango *can* hand raw tokens to your backend (breaks OneCLI principle) or you can route via its proxy (preserves it). |\n| **[Paragon / ActionKit](https://www.useparagon.com/)** | Embedded iPaaS; ActionKit = 1,000+ LLM-optimized actions across 130+ connectors, MCP-compatible | No | Enterprise SaaS embedding integrations; no public pricing, reportedly **five-figure annual contracts** + per-connected-user usage ([Nango's analysis](https://nango.dev/blog/paragon-pricing/)). Overkill for your pilot. |\n| **[Merge.dev](https://www.merge.dev/pricing)** | Unified API (normalized schemas) for HRIS/ATS/CRM/etc., **sync-and-store** — data cached, can be stale | No | Reading normalized B2B data across many customers' systems. Wrong shape for real-time agent *actions*. Launch: free 3 linked accounts, then $650/mo for 10, $65/additional linked account ([FAQ](https://help.merge.dev/en/articles/6641605-self-serve-with-merge-free-and-launch-plans-faq)). |\n| **[Klavis AI](https://www.klavis.ai/)** (YC X25) | Hosted production MCP servers (50+ apps) with built-in OAuth + multi-tenancy; **Strata** = open-source MCP router solving tool-overload via progressive discovery ([GitHub](https://github.com/Klavis-AI/klavis)) | Partially (Strata open source) | You want hosted MCP + multi-tenant auth from a smaller vendor. Free tier ≈500 API calls/mo; paid tiers not clearly published ([pricing](https://www.klavis.ai/pricing)). |\n| **[ACI.dev](https://github.com/aipotheosis-labs/aci)** (Aipolabs) | Open-source (Apache 2.0) tool-calling platform: 600+ integrations, multi-tenant OAuth, unified MCP server. Repo active (4.8k stars, ongoing commits) | **Yes, fully** | **The strongest self-host option**: run the whole vault+tool layer on your infra — OneCLI principle preserved, no third party holds tokens. Cost = your ops time. Smaller community than Nango. |\n| **[mcp.run](https://docs.mcp.run/tasks/)** | Registry + hosted runtime for WASM \"servlets\" and prompt-driven Tasks | Partially | Niche; more a hosted automation runtime than a per-user auth broker. Not a fit. |\n| **[Smithery](https://smithery.ai/)** | Registry/hosting for 6,000–7,000+ MCP servers (\"Docker Hub for MCP\") | Registry is a platform; servers vary | Discovery and one-off hosting; **no per-end-user credential brokering layer**. Not your integration layer. |\n\n## 6. Anthropic-native paths\n\n- **Claude Connectors Directory:** ~500+ Anthropic-reviewed remote MCP connectors, but this surfaces inside claude.ai/Claude Desktop — it's a consumer/product surface, **not an API you consume for your own multi-user product** ([FAQ](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11596036-anthropic-connectors-directory-faq)).\n- **Official vendor remote MCP servers** now exist for much of your list: HubSpot (`mcp.hubspot.com`, OAuth, GA — [docs](https://developers.hubspot.com/mcp)), Notion (hosted, OAuth — [GitHub](https://github.com/makenotion/notion-mcp-server)), Stripe, GitHub, Atlassian, Linear ([directory](https://mcpservers.org/remote-mcp-servers)). **Google now ships official per-product Workspace MCP servers** (Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Chat) ([Google docs](https://developers.google.com/workspace/guides/configure-mcp-servers), [Calendar MCP](https://developers.google.com/workspace/calendar/api/guides/configure-mcp-server)) plus a Workspace CLI. Pipedrive: no official remote MCP found — *gap; verify*.\n- **Is \"just use official vendor MCPs\" credible?** Increasingly yes for coverage, and it's free. **But the auth burden lands on you:** MCP OAuth 2.1 flows (dynamic client registration, PKCE, refresh) must be run by the MCP *client host*. The Claude Agent SDK \"doesn't handle OAuth flows automatically\" for programmatic use — you complete OAuth yourself and pass `Authorization: Bearer` headers per server, per user ([Agent SDK MCP docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/mcp), [MCP connector](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector)). So you'd be building: per-user token store, refresh daemon, consent-link generation for Telegram, and header injection per agent session. **That is… roughly OneCLI again, plus an MCP-OAuth client.** It keeps tokens on your infra (good) at the cost of owning N different OAuth quirks (the exact thing that's currently broken).\n\n## 7. Security comparison vs. your OneCLI principle\n\n**\"Agent never holds tokens\" — who preserves it?**\n\n| Option | Tokens live in | Agent sees tokens? | Per-END-USER auth | Link-click auth for Telegram |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Composio | Composio vault (3rd party) | No (sends `user_id` + tool call) | Yes (`user_id` / connected accounts) | Yes (`link()` → redirect_url) |\n| Pipedream Connect | Pipedream (3rd party) | No | Yes (`external_user_id`) | Yes (Connect Link) |\n| Arcade | Arcade Engine (3rd party; VPC option at enterprise) | No | Yes (`user_id`) | Yes (authorization URL) |\n| Klavis | Klavis (3rd party) | No | Yes | Yes (OAuth flows built in) |\n| Zapier MCP | Zapier | No | **No** (per-Zapier-account) | N/A |\n| Nango | Nango (or self-hosted) | **Optional** — proxy mode: no; credentials API: yes | Yes | Partial (Connect UI is embed-oriented; verify link flow) |\n| ACI.dev self-hosted | **Your infra** | No (unified MCP / function-call API) | Yes | Yes (OAuth flows included) |\n| Official vendor MCPs direct | **Your infra** (you build the store) | Yes, unless you keep OneCLI-style header injection in front | Yes (you implement) | Only if you build the consent-link plumbing |\n| Merge/Paragon | Their cloud | No | Per linked account / connected user (B2B-shaped) | Embed-oriented |\n\nKey trade-off: the managed platforms all *preserve the letter* of your principle (agent container is token-free) but *move the vault to a third party*. The Composio breach is the canonical realization of that risk. Self-hosted ACI.dev or keeping OneCLI keeps the vault yours; enterprise/VPC tiers of Composio and Arcade split the difference.\n\n## 8. Pricing reality check (all **estimates**, marked)\n\nAssume 100 users ≈ 90k tool calls/mo; 1,000 users ≈ 900k tool calls/mo (30 actions/day):\n\n| Option | ~100 users | ~1,000 users |\n|---|---|---|\n| Composio | **~$29/mo** | **~$229/mo** (2M calls included) — cheapest by far |\n| Pipedream Connect | ~$99/mo + credits | **~$1.9–2.5k/mo** ($99 + ~$2 × 900 users + credits) — matches a user's \"~$2k\" report |\n| Arcade (Growth) | ~$25 + ~$900 ≈ **~$0.9k/mo** | ~$9k/mo at list ($0.01/exec) → realistically **enterprise custom** |\n| Nango (auth only) | ~$250–400/mo (Starter + ~200–300 connections) | ~$2.5–3.5k/mo (Growth + ~2–3k connections @ $1) |\n| Zapier MCP | Not viable (multi-tenant) | ~1.8M tasks/mo → **five figures**; rule out |\n| ACI.dev self-host / fix OneCLI | Infra + your eng time | Same — the \"cost\" is the OAuth maintenance burden that already bit you |\n| Official vendor MCPs | $0 platform fees | $0 platform fees + you own the token layer |\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Don't rebuild the generic integration layer; keep the OneCLI *principle* and put a managed broker under it.** Your homegrown proxy's real value was the invariant \"agent never holds credentials\" — every shortlisted platform preserves that invariant while eliminating the part that's broken (auth routing, OAuth quirks, token refresh across N vendors).\n\nShortlist, in order:\n\n1. **Pipedream Connect** — best overall fit: 3,000+ apps (all five of your target categories), true per-end-user model, **Connect Link is literally \"send a link in Telegram\"**, credentials never exposed to the model, predictable ~$2/active-user pricing that's sane at 100–1,000 users.\n2. **Arcade.dev** — best security/auth story (delegated per-user OAuth as a first-class primitive, VPC options); pick it if the pilot's action volume is modest or you'll negotiate enterprise pricing; check catalog covers Pipedrive/HubSpot first.\n3. **Composio** — cheapest and broadest agent-native catalog with the same link-click flow; only adopt with eyes open about the May 2026 breach and ideally with your own OAuth client IDs + scoped-down permissions.\n\nHedges: if third-party token custody is unacceptable post-Composio-breach, **self-host ACI.dev** (Apache 2.0, 600+ tools, multi-tenant OAuth) or use **Nango in proxy mode** as the OAuth layer under a slimmed OneCLI — both keep the vault on your infra for ~$0–3.5k/mo. The \"official vendor MCP servers only\" strategy is real and free, but it recreates your current bug surface: you'd own per-user OAuth 2.1 flows, refresh, and header injection for each vendor — do it later, selectively (e.g., HubSpot's excellent official server), behind whichever broker you choose.\n\nSources: [Composio pricing](https://composio.dev/pricing) · [Composio auth docs](https://docs.composio.dev/docs/authenticating-tools) · [Composio incident](https://composio.dev/blog/composio-may-2026-security-incident) · [Material Security on the breach](https://material.security/resources/the-composio-breach-one-token-10242-doors) · [Pipedream Connect MCP](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/mcp) · [Pipedream Connect Link](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/managed-auth/connect-link) · [Pipedream pricing](https://pipedream.com/docs/pricing) · [Pipedream community pricing threads](https://pipedream.com/community/t/clarification-on-pricing-is-the-additional-2-for-an-extra-external-user-charged-monthly-or-is-it-a-one-time-fee/13692) · [Zapier MCP](https://zapier.com/mcp) · [Zapier MCP usage](https://docs.zapier.com/mcp/usage/overview) · [Arcade auth docs](https://docs.arcade.dev/en/home/auth/auth-tool-calling) · [Arcade pricing](https://www.arcade.dev/pricing) · [Nango pricing](https://nango.dev/pricing) · [Nango GitHub](https://github.com/NangoHQ/nango) · [Klavis](https://www.klavis.ai/) · [Klavis GitHub](https://github.com/Klavis-AI/klavis) · [ACI.dev GitHub](https://github.com/aipotheosis-labs/aci) · [Merge Launch pricing FAQ](https://help.merge.dev/en/articles/6641605-self-serve-with-merge-free-and-launch-plans-faq) · [Paragon pricing analysis (Nango)](https://nango.dev/blog/paragon-pricing/) · [Smithery](https://smithery.ai/) · [mcp.run Tasks](https://docs.mcp.run/tasks/) · [Anthropic Connectors Directory FAQ](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11596036-anthropic-connectors-directory-faq) · [Claude Agent SDK MCP docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/mcp) · [HubSpot MCP](https://developers.hubspot.com/mcp) · [Google Workspace MCP servers](https://developers.google.com/workspace/guides/configure-mcp-servers) · [Notion MCP](https://github.com/makenotion/notion-mcp-server) · [Remote MCP servers list](https://mcpservers.org/remote-mcp-servers)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1d0ca2e25639c7d7 (use SendMessage with to: 'a1d0ca2e25639c7d7', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 67901\ntool_uses: 34\nduration_ms: 362342</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"bfcda556-6653-42ea-868f-57a62b75d094","timestamp":"2026-07-02T15:14:30.872Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Context: a startup runs personal AI work-agents (Claude Agent SDK, containerized, chat via Telegram). They need their agents to act on users' work tools (Gmail, Google Calendar, CRMs like HubSpot/Salesforce/Pipedrive, Slack, Notion). They currently have a homegrown credential-injecting proxy (\"OneCLI vault\" — agent holds no keys, proxy injects at request time) but its auth-routing has a bug and the integration layer is deferred. Research the BUY-vs-BUILD landscape (2025-2026) for agent-tool integration layers:\n\n1. **Composio** — coverage (how many apps/tools), auth handling (managed OAuth?), MCP support, agent-framework SDKs, pricing tiers, security model (where do tokens live), known criticisms/limitations.\n2. **Pipedream (Connect / MCP)** — same questions; their MCP server offering for thousands of apps.\n3. **Zapier MCP / Zapier AI Actions** — same; limits (rate, actions), pricing.\n4. **Arcade.dev** — agent-native auth/tool-calling platform; how their \"auth for agents\" works (scoped tokens, user-level auth), pricing.\n5. **Nango, Paragon, Merge.dev, Klavis AI, ACI.dev (formerly Aipolabs), mcp.run, Smithery** — brief: what each is, when it fits, open-source options among them.\n6. **Anthropic-native paths**: MCP connectors directory (claude.ai connectors), remote MCP servers from vendors themselves (e.g., official HubSpot/Notion/Stripe MCP servers), Google Workspace MCP options. Is there a credible \"just use official vendor MCP servers\" strategy and what auth burden does it leave?\n7. Security comparison for THEIR architecture: which of these can act as a credential-brokering proxy where the AGENT NEVER HOLDS TOKENS (their current OneCLI principle)? Which require tokens in the agent's environment? Per-user (end-user auth) vs per-workspace auth support — critical: each of their users must connect THEIR OWN Gmail/CRM.\n8. Pricing reality check: for a pilot with ~100-1000 users each doing tens of actions/day, rough monthly cost per option (mark estimates clearly).\n\nKey question: should they fix/keep their homegrown OneCLI vault, or adopt one of these as the integration layer under it — and which shortlist makes sense for per-end-user auth with Telegram-based non-technical users (auth flow must be a simple link click)?\n\nIRON RULES: name things precisely, link sources, mark estimates/uncertainty, don't fabricate. Structured English report. Your final message IS the deliverable.","agentId":"a1d0ca2e25639c7d7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Buy-vs-Build: Agent–Tool Integration Layers (researched July 2026)\n\n## Executive summary\n\nThe market has matured fast: there are now at least four credible managed platforms (Composio, Pipedream Connect, Arcade.dev, Klavis) whose core architecture matches your OneCLI principle — **tokens live in the platform's vault, the agent only sends a `user_id` + tool call, and end-user auth is a hosted link click**. Given that, rebuilding a general credential-injecting proxy for Gmail/Calendar/CRMs in-house is hard to justify for a pilot. Recommended shortlist for per-end-user auth with Telegram users: **Pipedream Connect** (broadest coverage + Connect Link + predictable per-user pricing), **Arcade.dev** (purest \"auth for agents\" model, best security posture story), **Composio** (cheapest, biggest agent-native catalog, but suffered a real credential breach in May 2026). Details and caveats below.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Composio\n\n- **Coverage:** Marketing figures vary between \"500+\", \"800+ toolkits\" and \"900+ SaaS integrations\" ([composio.dev/toolkits](https://composio.dev/toolkits), [composio.dev](https://composio.dev/)). Covers Gmail, Google Calendar, HubSpot, Salesforce, Pipedrive, Slack, Notion.\n- **Auth:** Fully managed OAuth with token refresh. You create an \"Auth Config\" per toolkit; `connected_accounts.link(user_id, auth_config_id)` returns a **hosted `redirect_url` you send to the user — a pure link click**, ideal for Telegram ([docs](https://docs.composio.dev/docs/authenticating-tools)). You can use Composio's shared OAuth apps or bring your own client IDs.\n- **MCP:** Yes — hosted MCP servers per toolkit plus tool-execution SDKs (Python/TS) with adapters for OpenAI, LangChain, CrewAI, Vercel AI SDK, Claude ([composio.dev/toolkits](https://composio.dev/toolkits)).\n- **Pricing** ([composio.dev/pricing](https://composio.dev/pricing)): Free = 20k tool calls/mo; $29/mo = 200k calls (+$0.299/1k over); $229/mo = 2M calls; Enterprise = custom, VPC/on-prem option. Notably **priced per tool call, not per user**.\n- **Security model:** Tokens stored server-side in Composio's vault, encrypted at rest; SOC 2 Type 2. Agent never holds tokens.\n- **Criticisms/limitations:** **The big one: the May 2026 breach.** Disclosed May 21, 2026 — an attacker got in via a compromised employee Gmail OAuth token, achieved code execution in the tool-execution sandbox, and exfiltrated ~5,241 API keys and ~5,001 GitHub OAuth tokens (~0.3% of active connections) ([Composio's disclosure](https://composio.dev/blog/composio-may-2026-security-incident), [Material Security analysis](https://material.security/resources/the-composio-breach-one-token-10242-doors), [P0 Security analysis](https://p0.dev/blog/the-composio-breach-lets-stop-blaming-the-agents/)). This is the concrete downside of centralizing everyone's tokens in a third party. Community has also historically complained about SDK/API churn (v1→v3 breaking changes) — lower-severity, but real.\n\n## 2. Pipedream Connect / MCP\n\n- **Coverage:** ~3,000+ apps, 10,000+ pre-built tools — the largest catalog of the managed options ([docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/mcp)).\n- **Auth:** Managed OAuth per **`external_user_id`** (your user identifier). **Connect Link** is a Pipedream-hosted URL (no frontend SDK, no iframe) you generate per user and send over \"email, SMS, or direct redirect\" — i.e., paste into Telegram; scoped to that user, expires in 4h ([Connect Link docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/managed-auth/connect-link)). This is exactly your flow.\n- **MCP:** Dedicated MCP servers per app per user; \"credentials … never directly exposed to AI models\"; also a Connect **proxy** endpoint for raw API calls with injected auth — closest analog to OneCLI's proxy semantics ([docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/mcp)).\n- **Pricing:** Free in development mode. Production (per search results citing their pricing): **~$99/mo including 100 external users, then ~$2/external user/mo**, plus credits for tool executions ([pricing docs](https://pipedream.com/docs/pricing), [community thread confirming ~$2/user](https://pipedream.com/community/t/clarification-on-pricing-is-the-additional-2-for-an-extra-external-user-charged-monthly-or-is-it-a-one-time-fee/13692)). They bill only \"active\" users (ones you make calls for) ([community](https://pipedream.com/community/t/what-is-the-pricing-for-using-managed-oauth-with-mcp-server-in-a-macos-app-with-1000-users-and-5-connected-services/13480)). *Estimate — confirm with sales; per-user rate may have changed.*\n- **Security model:** Tokens encrypted at rest in Pipedream; per-user credential isolation; agent never holds tokens. SOC 2 (per their site; verify current report).\n- **Limitations:** Credit accounting (1 credit/30s compute) makes tool-call cost slightly opaque; per-user fee dominates at your scale (a user in that community thread estimated **~$2k/mo at 1,000 users**, consistent with $2/user).\n\n## 3. Zapier MCP / AI Actions\n\n- **Coverage:** 8,000–9,000 apps, ~30,000 actions ([zapier.com/mcp](https://zapier.com/mcp)).\n- **Auth & multi-tenancy — the dealbreaker:** Zapier MCP is designed around **a Zapier account owner's** connected apps. For your SaaS-like setup, community guidance is that *each end user needs their own Zapier account* ([Zapier community](https://community.zapier.com/how-do-i-3/multi-tenancy-functionality-49496)); there is no embedded per-external-user credential model like Pipedream's. Tokens live on Zapier's servers.\n- **Pricing/limits:** Each successful tool call = **2 Zapier tasks**, drawn from the plan's task quota; no per-session rate limit; failed calls free ([usage docs](https://docs.zapier.com/mcp/usage/overview)). Free plan ≈100 tasks/mo, Professional ≈750+ ([zapier.com/pricing](https://zapier.com/pricing)). Enterprise plans have MCP off by default.\n- **Verdict:** Great for one power user connecting their own stack to Claude; **wrong shape and wrong economics for 100–1,000 end users doing tens of actions/day** (900k calls = 1.8M tasks/mo — five figures monthly, *estimate*).\n\n## 4. Arcade.dev\n\n- **What it is:** \"MCP runtime for production AI agents\" founded 2024 by Alex Salazar (ex-Okta) — the most explicitly **agent-auth-first** platform ([arcade.dev](https://www.arcade.dev/), [WorkOS overview](https://workos.com/blog/what-is-arcade-dev-an-llm-tool-calling-platform)).\n- **How auth works:** You call `tools.authorize(tool, user_id)` → Arcade returns an **authorization URL** (link click for the user) → after consent, Arcade Engine stores and refreshes tokens; all subsequent tool executions just pass `user_id`. \"Developers never see tokens directly\" ([auth docs](https://docs.arcade.dev/en/home/auth/auth-tool-calling), [how Arcade helps](https://docs.arcade.dev/home/auth/how-arcade-helps)). Agents act **as the user** (delegated OAuth), not as a bot/service account. Custom Python tools still get Arcade-managed tokens.\n- **Coverage:** Pre-built MCP toolkits for Google Workspace, Slack, Microsoft, Salesforce, GitHub, Notion, etc. — a **curated catalog, meaningfully smaller than Composio/Pipedream** (roughly dozens of toolkits, not thousands of apps). Check the catalog for Pipedrive/HubSpot specifics before committing.\n- **Pricing** ([arcade.dev/pricing](https://www.arcade.dev/pricing)): Free tier (1,000 standard tool executions, 100 \"user challenges\" = auth flows, 1 hosted MCP server). Growth: $25/mo + $0.01/standard execution beyond 2,000 (+$0.05/auth challenge beyond 600, $0.50/\"pro\" execution). Enterprise: custom, dedicated tenant isolation, RBAC, SSO, audit logs. Startup program for <100-employee companies.\n- **Security model:** Tokens in Arcade Engine only; agent never holds them. Enterprise tier offers dedicated tenant isolation; they've historically offered self-hosted/VPC engine deployment (*verify current availability with them*).\n\n## 5. The rest, briefly\n\n| Product | What it is | Open source? | When it fits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **[Nango](https://nango.dev/)** | Auth/integration infra for 800+ APIs: managed OAuth, token refresh, request proxy, per-tenant connections ([GitHub](https://github.com/NangoHQ/nango)) | Yes (Elastic License; self-hostable) | You want to keep building your own tool layer but outsource *only* OAuth plumbing. Pricing: Free 10 connections; Starter from $50/mo (20 conns); Growth from $500/mo (100 conns); **$1/connection/mo overage** + $0.10/1k proxy requests ([pricing](https://nango.dev/pricing)). Note: Nango *can* hand raw tokens to your backend (breaks OneCLI principle) or you can route via its proxy (preserves it). |\n| **[Paragon / ActionKit](https://www.useparagon.com/)** | Embedded iPaaS; ActionKit = 1,000+ LLM-optimized actions across 130+ connectors, MCP-compatible | No | Enterprise SaaS embedding integrations; no public pricing, reportedly **five-figure annual contracts** + per-connected-user usage ([Nango's analysis](https://nango.dev/blog/paragon-pricing/)). Overkill for your pilot. |\n| **[Merge.dev](https://www.merge.dev/pricing)** | Unified API (normalized schemas) for HRIS/ATS/CRM/etc., **sync-and-store** — data cached, can be stale | No | Reading normalized B2B data across many customers' systems. Wrong shape for real-time agent *actions*. Launch: free 3 linked accounts, then $650/mo for 10, $65/additional linked account ([FAQ](https://help.merge.dev/en/articles/6641605-self-serve-with-merge-free-and-launch-plans-faq)). |\n| **[Klavis AI](https://www.klavis.ai/)** (YC X25) | Hosted production MCP servers (50+ apps) with built-in OAuth + multi-tenancy; **Strata** = open-source MCP router solving tool-overload via progressive discovery ([GitHub](https://github.com/Klavis-AI/klavis)) | Partially (Strata open source) | You want hosted MCP + multi-tenant auth from a smaller vendor. Free tier ≈500 API calls/mo; paid tiers not clearly published ([pricing](https://www.klavis.ai/pricing)). |\n| **[ACI.dev](https://github.com/aipotheosis-labs/aci)** (Aipolabs) | Open-source (Apache 2.0) tool-calling platform: 600+ integrations, multi-tenant OAuth, unified MCP server. Repo active (4.8k stars, ongoing commits) | **Yes, fully** | **The strongest self-host option**: run the whole vault+tool layer on your infra — OneCLI principle preserved, no third party holds tokens. Cost = your ops time. Smaller community than Nango. |\n| **[mcp.run](https://docs.mcp.run/tasks/)** | Registry + hosted runtime for WASM \"servlets\" and prompt-driven Tasks | Partially | Niche; more a hosted automation runtime than a per-user auth broker. Not a fit. |\n| **[Smithery](https://smithery.ai/)** | Registry/hosting for 6,000–7,000+ MCP servers (\"Docker Hub for MCP\") | Registry is a platform; servers vary | Discovery and one-off hosting; **no per-end-user credential brokering layer**. Not your integration layer. |\n\n## 6. Anthropic-native paths\n\n- **Claude Connectors Directory:** ~500+ Anthropic-reviewed remote MCP connectors, but this surfaces inside claude.ai/Claude Desktop — it's a consumer/product surface, **not an API you consume for your own multi-user product** ([FAQ](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11596036-anthropic-connectors-directory-faq)).\n- **Official vendor remote MCP servers** now exist for much of your list: HubSpot (`mcp.hubspot.com`, OAuth, GA — [docs](https://developers.hubspot.com/mcp)), Notion (hosted, OAuth — [GitHub](https://github.com/makenotion/notion-mcp-server)), Stripe, GitHub, Atlassian, Linear ([directory](https://mcpservers.org/remote-mcp-servers)). **Google now ships official per-product Workspace MCP servers** (Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Chat) ([Google docs](https://developers.google.com/workspace/guides/configure-mcp-servers), [Calendar MCP](https://developers.google.com/workspace/calendar/api/guides/configure-mcp-server)) plus a Workspace CLI. Pipedrive: no official remote MCP found — *gap; verify*.\n- **Is \"just use official vendor MCPs\" credible?** Increasingly yes for coverage, and it's free. **But the auth burden lands on you:** MCP OAuth 2.1 flows (dynamic client registration, PKCE, refresh) must be run by the MCP *client host*. The Claude Agent SDK \"doesn't handle OAuth flows automatically\" for programmatic use — you complete OAuth yourself and pass `Authorization: Bearer` headers per server, per user ([Agent SDK MCP docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/mcp), [MCP connector](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector)). So you'd be building: per-user token store, refresh daemon, consent-link generation for Telegram, and header injection per agent session. **That is… roughly OneCLI again, plus an MCP-OAuth client.** It keeps tokens on your infra (good) at the cost of owning N different OAuth quirks (the exact thing that's currently broken).\n\n## 7. Security comparison vs. your OneCLI principle\n\n**\"Agent never holds tokens\" — who preserves it?**\n\n| Option | Tokens live in | Agent sees tokens? | Per-END-USER auth | Link-click auth for Telegram |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Composio | Composio vault (3rd party) | No (sends `user_id` + tool call) | Yes (`user_id` / connected accounts) | Yes (`link()` → redirect_url) |\n| Pipedream Connect | Pipedream (3rd party) | No | Yes (`external_user_id`) | Yes (Connect Link) |\n| Arcade | Arcade Engine (3rd party; VPC option at enterprise) | No | Yes (`user_id`) | Yes (authorization URL) |\n| Klavis | Klavis (3rd party) | No | Yes | Yes (OAuth flows built in) |\n| Zapier MCP | Zapier | No | **No** (per-Zapier-account) | N/A |\n| Nango | Nango (or self-hosted) | **Optional** — proxy mode: no; credentials API: yes | Yes | Partial (Connect UI is embed-oriented; verify link flow) |\n| ACI.dev self-hosted | **Your infra** | No (unified MCP / function-call API) | Yes | Yes (OAuth flows included) |\n| Official vendor MCPs direct | **Your infra** (you build the store) | Yes, unless you keep OneCLI-style header injection in front | Yes (you implement) | Only if you build the consent-link plumbing |\n| Merge/Paragon | Their cloud | No | Per linked account / connected user (B2B-shaped) | Embed-oriented |\n\nKey trade-off: the managed platforms all *preserve the letter* of your principle (agent container is token-free) but *move the vault to a third party*. The Composio breach is the canonical realization of that risk. Self-hosted ACI.dev or keeping OneCLI keeps the vault yours; enterprise/VPC tiers of Composio and Arcade split the difference.\n\n## 8. Pricing reality check (all **estimates**, marked)\n\nAssume 100 users ≈ 90k tool calls/mo; 1,000 users ≈ 900k tool calls/mo (30 actions/day):\n\n| Option | ~100 users | ~1,000 users |\n|---|---|---|\n| Composio | **~$29/mo** | **~$229/mo** (2M calls included) — cheapest by far |\n| Pipedream Connect | ~$99/mo + credits | **~$1.9–2.5k/mo** ($99 + ~$2 × 900 users + credits) — matches a user's \"~$2k\" report |\n| Arcade (Growth) | ~$25 + ~$900 ≈ **~$0.9k/mo** | ~$9k/mo at list ($0.01/exec) → realistically **enterprise custom** |\n| Nango (auth only) | ~$250–400/mo (Starter + ~200–300 connections) | ~$2.5–3.5k/mo (Growth + ~2–3k connections @ $1) |\n| Zapier MCP | Not viable (multi-tenant) | ~1.8M tasks/mo → **five figures**; rule out |\n| ACI.dev self-host / fix OneCLI | Infra + your eng time | Same — the \"cost\" is the OAuth maintenance burden that already bit you |\n| Official vendor MCPs | $0 platform fees | $0 platform fees + you own the token layer |\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Don't rebuild the generic integration layer; keep the OneCLI *principle* and put a managed broker under it.** Your homegrown proxy's real value was the invariant \"agent never holds credentials\" — every shortlisted platform preserves that invariant while eliminating the part that's broken (auth routing, OAuth quirks, token refresh across N vendors).\n\nShortlist, in order:\n\n1. **Pipedream Connect** — best overall fit: 3,000+ apps (all five of your target categories), true per-end-user model, **Connect Link is literally \"send a link in Telegram\"**, credentials never exposed to the model, predictable ~$2/active-user pricing that's sane at 100–1,000 users.\n2. **Arcade.dev** — best security/auth story (delegated per-user OAuth as a first-class primitive, VPC options); pick it if the pilot's action volume is modest or you'll negotiate enterprise pricing; check catalog covers Pipedrive/HubSpot first.\n3. **Composio** — cheapest and broadest agent-native catalog with the same link-click flow; only adopt with eyes open about the May 2026 breach and ideally with your own OAuth client IDs + scoped-down permissions.\n\nHedges: if third-party token custody is unacceptable post-Composio-breach, **self-host ACI.dev** (Apache 2.0, 600+ tools, multi-tenant OAuth) or use **Nango in proxy mode** as the OAuth layer under a slimmed OneCLI — both keep the vault on your infra for ~$0–3.5k/mo. The \"official vendor MCP servers only\" strategy is real and free, but it recreates your current bug surface: you'd own per-user OAuth 2.1 flows, refresh, and header injection for each vendor — do it later, selectively (e.g., HubSpot's excellent official server), behind whichever broker you choose.\n\nSources: [Composio pricing](https://composio.dev/pricing) · [Composio auth docs](https://docs.composio.dev/docs/authenticating-tools) · [Composio incident](https://composio.dev/blog/composio-may-2026-security-incident) · [Material Security on the breach](https://material.security/resources/the-composio-breach-one-token-10242-doors) · [Pipedream Connect MCP](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/mcp) · [Pipedream Connect Link](https://pipedream.com/docs/connect/managed-auth/connect-link) · [Pipedream pricing](https://pipedream.com/docs/pricing) · [Pipedream community pricing threads](https://pipedream.com/community/t/clarification-on-pricing-is-the-additional-2-for-an-extra-external-user-charged-monthly-or-is-it-a-one-time-fee/13692) · [Zapier MCP](https://zapier.com/mcp) · [Zapier MCP usage](https://docs.zapier.com/mcp/usage/overview) · [Arcade auth docs](https://docs.arcade.dev/en/home/auth/auth-tool-calling) · [Arcade pricing](https://www.arcade.dev/pricing) · [Nango pricing](https://nango.dev/pricing) · [Nango GitHub](https://github.com/NangoHQ/nango) · [Klavis](https://www.klavis.ai/) · [Klavis GitHub](https://github.com/Klavis-AI/klavis) · [ACI.dev GitHub](https://github.com/aipotheosis-labs/aci) · [Merge Launch pricing FAQ](https://help.merge.dev/en/articles/6641605-self-serve-with-merge-free-and-launch-plans-faq) · [Paragon pricing analysis (Nango)](https://nango.dev/blog/paragon-pricing/) · [Smithery](https://smithery.ai/) · [mcp.run Tasks](https://docs.mcp.run/tasks/) · [Anthropic Connectors Directory FAQ](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11596036-anthropic-connectors-directory-faq) · [Claude Agent SDK MCP docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/mcp) · [HubSpot MCP](https://developers.hubspot.com/mcp) · [Google Workspace MCP servers](https://developers.google.com/workspace/guides/configure-mcp-servers) · [Notion MCP](https://github.com/makenotion/notion-mcp-server) · [Remote MCP servers list](https://mcpservers.org/remote-mcp-servers)"}],"resolvedModel":"claude-fable-5","totalDurationMs":362342,"totalTokens":67901,"totalToolUseCount":34,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2919,"cache_read_input_tokens":56904,"output_tokens":8076,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2919},"inferen