{"parentUuid":"46362501-3ba0-4026-9d72-0d37b722415e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"42d624f0-51b1-4086-a07f-c40f6806faec","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019HxPwgAkd2tJUwqsUP5i5z","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Adjacent-Space Research Report: \"One Agent Per Employee\" Landscape (2025–2026)\n\nCompiled July 2026 from five parallel research streams, ~130 web sources. Every claim carries a source URL; vendor-reported numbers are labeled; uncertainty flagged inline. Solela.ai was independently re-verified by direct fetch before finalizing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. \"Digital employee / AI coworker\" products — does the category work?\n\n### Per-product summary\n\n**11x.ai (Alice, AI SDR)** — Outbound SDR \"digital worker.\" Sales-led onboarding (2–4 weeks ICP/email-infra setup); pricing from ~$36K/yr per its own page (https://www.11x.ai/products/alice/pricing). **The category's cautionary tale**: TechCrunch's March 2025 investigation (https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/24/a16z-and-benchmark-backed-11x-has-been-claiming-customers-it-doesnt-have/) found inflated ARR (contracts with 3-month break clauses counted as full ARR; one employee estimated only ~$3M of $14M reported ARR survived trials), logo misuse (ZoomInfo threatened legal action, said the product \"performed significantly worse than our SDR employees\"; Airtable never went past a short trial), and employee-reported **70–80% customer churn**. Founder Hasan Sukkar stepped down as CEO in May 2025; still operating under CTO-turned-CEO Prabhav Jain (https://toarn.com/insights/11x-ai — single secondary source for details; CEO change widely reported).\n\n**Artisan (Ava, AI BDR)** — Outbound BDR with standard white-glove onboarding (dedicated AM, 2–4 weeks) (https://www.artisan.co/pricing). Quote-based pricing; third parties report ~$1.5K–$5K+/mo for real BDR replacement (unverified). $25M Series A April 2025 (https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/09/artisan-the-stop-hiring-humans-ai-agent-startup-raises-25m-and-is-still-hiring-humans/); self-reported ~$5–6M ARR — modest revenue relative to the enormous noise of the \"Stop Hiring Humans\" rage-bait campaign (which generated ~$2M ARR and death threats: https://sfstandard.com/2025/04/07/the-real-person-behind-san-franciscos-hated-anti-human-ad-campaign/). Reported LinkedIn account restrictions for automation/spam are a structural product risk (single-source, unverified: https://completeaitraining.com/news/from-stop-hiring-humans-to-linkedin-bans-artisan-ais-viral/). Reviewer consensus: an email-volume machine; personalization is template-grade.\n\n**Maisa AI** — Not an SDR: auditable \"digital workers\" for compliance-sensitive back-office processes, explicitly counter-positioned against the \"95% of AI pilots fail\" narrative. Self-serve \"Maisa Studio\" where citizen developers train workers in natural language. $25M seed Aug 2025 (https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/27/maisa-ai-gets-25m-to-fix-enterprise-ais-95-failure-rate/); Tracxn lists a May 2026 Series A (amount unconfirmed). No public pricing, no independent efficacy evidence yet.\n\n**Piper (Qualified)** — Inbound AI SDR (website visitors → qualified meetings); requires Salesforce; ~$40–68K/yr per third parties (https://www.knock-ai.com/blog/qualified-pricing). 500+ customers incl. Box, Brex, Asana (vendor-reported). **The category's cleanest success: Salesforce agreed to acquire Qualified in Dec 2025** (https://www.saastr.com/why-salesforce-acquiring-qualified-is-maybe-the-smartest-ai-gtm-deal-of-2025/). Key lesson: it succeeded on a *bounded* task (qualifying warm inbound), not open-ended judgment.\n\n**Beam.ai** — Back-office agentic process automation; template-led onboarding; ~$299 entry (single source, unverified). Low public signal either way — no controversy, no independent evidence of scale (https://beam.ai/, https://www.selecthub.com/p/ai-agent-builder-software/beam-ai/).\n\n**Ema (ema.co)** — \"Universal AI Employee\" via pre-built Personas (support, HR, sales, legal); no-code builder, enterprise motion, on-prem options. $61M+ raised (Accel/Section 32: https://venturebeat.com/ai/ema-raises-36m-to-build-universal-ai-employees-for-enterprises); GetLatka estimates ~$15M 2025 revenue (unverified). Mid-pack; efficacy claims entirely vendor-sourced.\n\n**Lyzr** — Really an agent-infrastructure platform (\"Agentic OS\") with \"AI employees\" marketing; IT/developer-led onboarding. $14.5M Series A+ led by Accenture at $250M valuation, reported >300% QoQ revenue growth from a small base (https://finovate.com/agentic-ai-infrastructure-firm-lyzr-ai-raises-14-5-million-at-250-million-valuation/).\n\n**Cykel AI (Lucy/Eve/Samson)** — LSE-listed named digital workers. The audited numbers are damning: **£4,107 revenue in the half-year to July 2025** (not a typo), £1.2M pretax loss, shares down 29% on results (https://www.ajbell.co.uk/news/articles/cykel-ai-narrows-interim-loss-slashes-administrative-expenses). The starkest narrative-vs-reality gap in the category.\n\n**MultiOn → Please** — The autonomous web-agent pioneer split and pivoted hard: founder Div Garg left Dec 2024 to found AGI Inc.; the company rebranded to Please Platforms and is now, verified directly, a consumer **\"thinking reminders app\"** in research preview (https://please.ai). A quiet negative data point for general-purpose autonomous agents.\n\n**Devin (Cognition)** — The paradox. Independently measured early performance was poor: Answer.AI's month-long review scored **3 successes / 14 failures / 3 inconclusive** on 20 real tasks (\"tasks it can do are so small and well-defined that I may as well do them myself\": https://www.answer.ai/posts/2025-01-08-devin.html), and the launch Upwork demo was debunked (https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40008109). Yet commercially: ARR $1M → $73M (Sep 2024→Jun 2025, Sacra), Windsurf acquisition, $10.2B valuation Sep 2025 (https://www.cnbc.com/2025/09/08/cognition-valued-at-10point2-billion-two-months-after-windsurf-.html), then **$1B+ raise at ~$25B pre-money May 2026** (https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/27/ai-coding-startup-cognition-raises-1b-at-25b-pre-money-valuation/). Pricing dropped from $500/mo to a $20/mo usage-based entry tier.\n\n### Category verdict\n\n- **The fully-autonomous AI SDR wave of 2024–25 broadly failed**: analyst posts report 50–70% annual churn for AI SDR tools and reply-rate decay as buyers pattern-match AI templates (https://www.digitalapplied.com/blog/case-against-ai-sdrs-contrarian-analysis-2026 — directionally consistent across sources, individually unaudited). AI-app retention is structurally worse than SaaS (~21% vs ~31% annual: https://www.technewsworld.com/story/ai-apps-generate-revenue-but-struggle-with-retention-180236.html).\n- **Winners are bounded-task, verifiable-output products** (Devin: code compiles or doesn't; Piper: warm inbound qualification). **Losers sold headcount replacement on open-ended judgment** (11x, Cykel, MultiOn).\n- The \"digital employee\" *metaphor* looks like a 2024 marketing artifact; surviving products are sold as tools with narrow measurable outcomes, humans in the loop, and the capability is being absorbed into existing sales stacks as a feature layer.\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: the vision — agent as *amplifier of an employee* rather than *replacement of an employee* — sits on the correct side of the documented failure line. The failures were all \"fire the human\"; nobody on this list productizes \"one agent per employee that makes the employee better.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. Post-call sales workflow automation — table-stakes vs differentiating\n\n### What each product automates after a call\n\n| Product | Post-call automation | Key detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Momentum.io** | The deepest CRM-write engine: \"Autopilot\" pipelines extract **MEDDIC/MEDDPICC/BANT and arbitrary custom fields** from transcripts into any Salesforce field/object; drafts follow-ups for approval; creates cases; Slack deal rooms (https://www.momentum.io/meddic-autopilot, https://docs.momentum.io/autopilot) | Admin-defined per-field prompts with save policies: **confirm / write-if-empty / fully-automatic** — a shipped mini trust-level model |\n| **Attention.com** | CRM auto-update agent, <60s follow-up email drafts in rep's voice, coaching scorecards, Slack deal-risk alerts (https://www.attention.com/product/ai-sales-agents) | $14M Series A; G2 4.9 but only ~61 reviews — small footprint |\n| **Gong** | 18 named agents (Apr 2025→Oct 2025): AI Composer (follow-ups), AI Tasker (next steps), **AI Deal Reviewer (scores deals against sales methodologies)**, Activity Mapper, trigger-based auto-briefs (https://www.gong.io/press/gong-redefines-ai-agents-to-deliver-real-revenue-impact) | **All agents included free with Gong licenses** — priced at zero, i.e., commoditized. Self-reported $500M+ ARR (unverified) |\n| **Salesforce Agentforce** | SDR agent (inbound nurture + warm-handoff brief) and Sales Coach (role-play scoring). Notably **no strong call→CRM-fields agent** (https://www.salesforce.com/news/stories/einstein-sales-agents-announcement/) | Documented adoption struggle: ~6% of customers *paid*; guidance cut citing slow adoption (https://www.salesforceben.com/where-are-we-really-at-with-agentforce-adoption/); pricing whiplash $2/conversation → $0.10/action Flex Credits |\n| **HubSpot Breeze** | **Smart Deal Progression** (Spring 2026, beta): after every call — *suggested* CRM updates using your pipeline definitions + drafted follow-up + action items (https://www.hubspot.com/company-news/spring-2026-spotlight) | Suggest-only, not autonomous writes; agents repriced to **pay-per-outcome** ($0.50/resolved conversation, $1/qualified lead, Apr 2026) |\n| **Day.ai** | AI-native CRM: calls/email auto-build and maintain the CRM itself, no data entry (https://www.bvp.com/atlas/lessons-from-day-ais-journey-to-becoming-the-waymo-of-crm) | $20M Sequoia Series A Feb 2026, ~120 customers — the \"post-call automation ceases to be a feature\" bet |\n| **Granola** | Notes + \"Recipes\" (repeatable post-call prompt workflows, Sept 2025); CRM sync to HubSpot/Affinity/Attio (not native Salesforce) (https://www.granola.ai/) | $125M Series C at **$1.5B**, Mar 2026 (https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/25/granola-raises-125m-hits-1-5b-valuation-as-it-expands-from-meeting-notetaker-to-enterprise-ai-app/) — pivoting to \"enterprise company memory\" |\n| **Fireflies.ai** | Transcripts/summaries/action items → HubSpot tasks, Salesforce/HubSpot record sync, Slack recaps at **$19/user/mo** (https://fireflies.ai/integrations) | Profitable since 2023, $1B valuation on ~$19M raised — the capital-efficiency benchmark |\n| **Fathom** | Free-tier summaries/action items/follow-up drafts; CRM field sync + coaching scorecards at $25/user/mo (https://www.fathom.ai/) | HubSpot's 2025 Most Used App; $10M→$30M revenue in a year (self-reported) |\n\n### Table-stakes vs differentiating (the market's answer)\n\n**Table-stakes (free-to-$25/user, nobody pays a premium):** transcription, summaries, action items, follow-up email *drafts*, Slack recaps, basic CRM note logging. Structural proof: Gong bundles all 18 agents at no extra cost and HubSpot builds suggested post-call CRM updates into the CRM natively — when both gorillas price it at zero, it's table-stakes (https://www.laxis.com/blog/state-of-meeting-note-taking-2026/ corroborates).\n\n**Differentiating (what still commands money):**\n1. **Trusted autonomous CRM writes with policy controls** — Momentum's confirm/write-if-empty/full-auto save policies; HubSpot stops at \"suggested,\" and that autonomy gap is the value line.\n2. **Methodology-specific extraction** (MEDDIC/BANT into real fields) — a RevOps, top-down sale.\n3. **Multi-step cross-system chains** (call → fields + case + Slack + email + warehouse).\n4. **Institutional memory across calls** (Gong Ask Anything, Granola's pivot).\n\n**Buyer-behavior signals:** two forced repricings in 12 months (Salesforce off per-conversation, HubSpot to pay-per-result) show buyers resist paying for AI *activity* and will pay for *outcomes*. Retention correlates with workflow embedding, not model quality — \"prompts are portable… the plumbing is harder to replicate\" (https://www.saastr.com/the-wave-of-ai-agent-churn-to-come-prompts-are-portable/).\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: the post-sales-call *chain* itself (summary → follow-up → CRM) is commoditized. What's not commoditized: methodology-aware extraction personally calibrated to the rep, and graduated write-autonomy — which is exactly the Momentum policy model generalized. Note Momentum's shipped confirm/write-if-empty/auto controls as prior art.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Onboarding-by-interview / calibration patterns + solela.ai\n\n### solela.ai — VERIFIED, exists and is live\nIndependently confirmed by direct fetch of https://www.solela.ai/ (plus https://elion.solela.ai/). A personal AI assistant across web/WhatsApp/Skills with unified memory (\"One AI. Four lives. The same memory — anywhere you write\"). Positioning is explicitly **\"Privacy by architecture\"**, verbatim claims: \"OS-level isolation per agent. No noisy neighbours,\" \"Keys live in a gateway. The agent never sees them,\" \"Approval before action,\" \"Full audit trail,\" \"Cross-workspace reads are architecturally impossible.\" Onboarding is ~60 seconds, three steps (Google sign-in → \"Name it\" → start writing on web/WhatsApp) — **notably no interview/calibration step is advertised**. No independent press, funding, or reviews found; company scale unverified. Do not conflate with a16z-backed Sola (sola.ai).\n\n### The three calibration patterns in the market\n1. **Interview-first** (rare): **Dot (New Computer)** was the purest consumer example — onboarding asked getting-to-know-you questions and probed deeper to build a psychological profile (https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/21/dots-ai-really-really-wants-to-get-to-know-you/) — **and it shut down Oct 2025** (https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/personalized-ai-companion-app-dot-is-shutting-down/). **OpenAI GPT Builder** is the canonical \"agent interviews you, then writes its own config\" mechanism (https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8770868-gpt-builder). Coaching products do it well: **BetterUp** (2–3 min intake survey + Whole Person Assessment), **Rocky.ai** (goal/obstacle intake), **Valence Nadia** (calibration *through* conversation; generates a user-controlled \"collaboration profile\" other Nadias can read — https://www.bvp.com/news/meet-nadia-valences-ai-coach-for-leadership-development-and-workplace-collaboration).\n2. **Template/segmentation-first** (dominant): **Lindy** asks role/industry, then lands you on role-matched templates with a working example (https://obviostudio.substack.com/p/ai-integration-lindy-from-onboarding); **Zapier Agents** builds config from a natural-language description via a prompt assistant.\n3. **Ingestion/ambient-first**: **Martin** (connect email/calendar, memory built through use), **ohai.ai** (forms + forward-your-emails), **Yohana** (human-guide intake — the concierge analog; medium confidence, pages partially unreachable), **Solela** (name it and go).\n\n### What retains non-technical users (UX research)\n- Analysis of 150+ AI onboarding flows: nearly all successful products converged on **template activation loops**; blank canvas kills conversion; **generated editable drafts beat static templates** (https://www.fishmanafnewsletter.com/p/how-ai-products-drive-adoption-in-onboarding-through-template-activation-loop).\n- Wow moment within ~3 minutes; light role/goal questions make users feel it's \"built for someone like you,\" but long interviews are absent from winners (https://www.growthmates.news/p/what-ai-products-get-right-and-wrong). Sub-60-second activation is the emerging bar (https://productled.com/blog/ai-onboarding).\n- The Valence/Gilead deployment finding: getting users into a live session within 10 minutes — where participants \"felt Nadia was getting to know them\" — produced the aha (https://www.valence.co/ai-and-the-workforce/how-gilead-sciences-is-scaling-ai-coaching-to-all-employees-kerry-okeeffe-gilead-valence).\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: a long upfront self-profiling interview is empirically risky (Dot died; winners activate in minutes). The evidence-backed hybrid: 1–3 light calibration questions + a role template that produces a *working draft workflow immediately*, with the deep profiling interview woven into the first real work sessions (Nadia model) rather than gating them.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Graduated trust / expanding autonomy\n\n**Bottom line: graduated trust exists as vocabulary and process everywhere, as an automated product mechanism almost nowhere.**\n\n- **What's shipped is static:** per-step approval toggles (Lindy HITL confirmations — https://docs.lindy.ai/testing/human-in-the-loop; Relay.app's approval/data-input/AI-output-review steps — https://docs.relay.app/human-in-the-loop/human-in-the-loop-steps; Zapier pause-for-review; LangGraph `interrupt` per-tool approve/edit/reject) and discrete global modes (Claude Code's default/plan/acceptEdits/bypass; Devin CLI modes; OpenAI Operator's fixed auto/watch/human-only tiers keyed to site sensitivity — https://openai.com/index/introducing-operator/). Microsoft's Agent 365 / Copilot Studio governance is org-level control of which agents exist, not per-agent graduation (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot/blog/copilot-studio/new-and-improved-agent-governance-intelligent-workflows-and-connected-app-experiences/).\n- **Closest shipped approximations:** (1) **Cursor Auto-review** (June 2026) — a classifier in the execution path modulates freedom per action, marketed as \"autonomy is a dial, not a switch\"; blocks ~4% of actions, cut user interruptions from ~40% to ~7% (https://cursor.com/blog/agent-autonomy-auto-review) — but it's *contextual*, not *earned over time*. (2) Claude Code's persistent allowlists — the envelope widens with tenure, but by manual grants. (3) Decagon's percentage-of-traffic ramps + shadow mode in support AI (https://decagon.ai/blog/ai-customer-support-setup) — real graduation, human-decided. (4) Momentum's confirm/write-if-empty/auto save policies (section 2).\n- **Frameworks exist and are current:** Feng/McDonald/Zhang, \"Levels of Autonomy for AI Agents\" (arXiv June 2025) — five SAE-style levels defined by user role (Operator→Observer), plus a proposed \"autonomy certificates\" governance mechanism (https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12469). Enterprise playbooks converge on \"autonomy must be earned\": Microsoft SOC crawl-walk-run guide; ManageEngine (\"expand execution privileges only after reversal rates decline\").\n- **The key empirical datum:** Anthropic's \"Measuring AI agent autonomy in practice\" (Feb 2026) — full auto-approve appears in ~20% of new users' sessions vs >40% at 750+ sessions, with oversight shifting from approval to monitoring (https://www.anthropic.com/research/measuring-agent-autonomy). **Earned trust demonstrably happens — in the user, not in the product.**\n- **No verified shipped product implements automatic earned autonomy** (permission scope widening automatically as track record improves — e.g., auto-promotion after N clean approvals). This is an absence-of-evidence finding after targeted search, not proof of nonexistence.\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: \"guardrails that expand as trust grows\" — if made *mechanical* (track-record-gated promotion from suggest → auto-with-review → auto, per action type, visible to both employee and org) — is genuine whitespace with academic legitimation (autonomy-certificates paper) and behavioral validation (Anthropic data). It is a real differentiator; per-step approval toggles alone are not.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The employee-development angle — mostly whitespace\n\n- **Crowded:** courses ABOUT agents (Coursera/Microsoft/IBM certs, Section, Salesforce **Agentblazer** gamified status ladder — https://trailhead.salesforce.com/agentblazer); agents that DELIVER training (Sana — acquired by Workday for ~$1.1B; Uplimit; Degreed Maestro; Microsoft Learning Agent); and rhetoric: Microsoft Work Trend Index 2025's **\"agent boss\"** (\"builds, delegates to, and manages agents… taking control of their career in the age of AI\"; 28% of managers considering hiring AI-workforce managers — https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index/2025-the-year-the-frontier-firm-is-born), McKinsey's \"agentic AI literacy,\" HBR's \"Create an Onboarding Plan for AI Agents\" (Mar 2026), Ethan Mollick's \"Management as AI superpower.\"\n- **The scientific anchor exists, unproductized:** Weidmann/Xu/**Deming**, NBER WP 33662 — leadership effectiveness with AI-agent teams correlates **0.81** with leading human teams; agent-based leadership assessment cost $23 vs $114/participant (https://www.nber.org/digest/202507/leadership-skills-managing-ai-agents-vs-humans). Managing agents ≈ managing people, empirically.\n- **Adjacent pieces exist separately:** Anthropic's AI Fluency 4Ds framework names **Delegation and Discernment** as teachable competencies (https://aifluencyframework.org/); Bryq scores per-employee AI fluency 0–100 (https://www.bryq.com/product/ai-fluency); Copilot Dashboard tracks adoption per cohort (framed by press as surveillance, not development); Shopify put AI use into performance reviews (Duolingo did too, then rolled it back by Apr 2026 — https://fortune.com/2026/04/13/duolingo-ceo-luis-von-ahn-ai-usage-requirement-employee-performance-evaluations/); Iro AI is literally \"Duolingo for AI\" with an agents path (https://tryiro.com/).\n- **Nobody found productizing \"the agent-work itself develops the employee\"** — no product scores your real delegation/feedback/verification behavior with your own work agent over time and maps it to a competency ladder or career progression. All the ingredients (science, framework, telemetry, gamification) exist unassembled. Flagged: a sub-visibility startup could exist here.\n\n---\n\n## Answers to the four key questions\n\n**(a) What onboarding UX retains non-technical users?** Not a long interview. The converged winning pattern: role-based template + generated editable draft + wow-moment inside ~3 minutes; deep calibration deferred into the first real work sessions where the user *feels* the agent getting to know them (Valence/Gilead). Dot — the purest interview-first product — is dead. NanoCo's self-profiling interview should produce a visibly working workflow fast, then keep interviewing incrementally.\n\n**(b) What does the sales market treat as proven value?** Table-stakes: transcription/summary/action-items/follow-up drafts/Slack recaps (priced at ~zero by Gong and HubSpot). Proven paid value: trusted autonomous CRM writes with policy controls, methodology-specific field extraction (MEDDIC etc.), and multi-step cross-system chains — sold to RevOps on pipeline-hygiene ROI. Buyers are forcing outcome-based pricing (two major repricings in 12 months). Retention comes from workflow embedding, not model quality.\n\n**(c) Is \"role methodology packs, personally calibrated\" occupied?** Substantially whitespace as a combination. Pieces exist: Gong's AI Deal Reviewer scores against methodologies; Momentum extracts MEDDIC to fields; Ema sells role Personas; coaching products calibrate to the person. Nobody found combines *role methodology encoded as multi-step workflows at core moments* + *personal calibration via interview* + *one persistent agent per employee*. Closest structural analogs are admin-configured RevOps tools (team-level, not person-level) — the person-level calibration is the open slot. Caveat: the \"digital employee\" graveyard (section 1) shows the framing must stay \"amplifier,\" not \"replacement.\"\n\n**(d) Is graduated-trust guardrails a real differentiator?** Yes — with precision. Per-step approvals and mode toggles are commodity. What no verified product ships is *automatic earned autonomy*: permission scope that widens mechanically with a clean track record. The demand is documented (enterprise playbooks all preach it), the behavior is measured (Anthropic: auto-approve doubles with user tenure), the framework is published (arXiv 2506.12469 \"autonomy certificates\") — and the mechanism is unbuilt. That, plus the fact that it doubles as the employee-development mechanic (earning your agent more autonomy *is* the skill ladder — question 5's whitespace), makes it arguably NanoCo's most defensible differentiator, since it uniquely fuses two gaps no one else connects.\n\n**Biggest overall caveat:** many category-level statistics (AI SDR churn rates, several ARR figures) come from vendor-adjacent content marketing — directionally corroborated across independent sources but individually unaudited; all such figures are flagged inline above."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aef68527762188ce4 (use SendMessage with to: 'aef68527762188ce4', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 71252\ntool_uses: 7\nduration_ms: 563881</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"c936af8a-da72-4154-80f9-3765237c3723","timestamp":"2026-07-02T15:18:14.103Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are a research agent. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Context: a startup (NanoCo) builds ONE personal AI agent per employee (Claude Agent SDK stack, Telegram channel). Vision: the agent encodes the methodology of the user's ROLE (multi-step workflows at \"core moments\", e.g. post-sales-call chain), calibrated to the individual via a self-profiling interview at onboarding; organizational guardrails expand as trust grows; \"managing your agent is how the employee grows\". Research what exists (2025-2026) in adjacent product space:\n\n1. **\"Digital employee / AI coworker\" products**: 11x.ai, Artisan (Ava), Maisa AI, Piper (Qualified), Beam.ai, Ema (ema.co), Lyzr, Cykel, MultiOn/please, Devin (as \"AI engineer\" framing). For each (briefly): what role(s), how the \"employee\" is configured/onboarded, pricing model, and — most important — evidence of whether the category works (public traction, churn stories, criticism like the 11x controversy).\n2. **Sales-specific post-call workflow products** (their first target role is sales): Momentum.io, Attention.com, Gong (call → CRM/followup automations), Salesforce Agentforce, HubSpot Breeze agents, Day.ai, Granola (meeting notes → actions), Fireflies/Fathom action items. What exactly do they automate after a call, and what does the market consider table-stakes vs differentiating?\n3. **Onboarding-by-interview / calibration patterns**: products where an AI interviews the user to build its own config/persona — e.g., personal-assistant products (Lindy onboarding, Martin, ohai.ai, yohana), AI coaching products, or \"agent interviews you then builds your workflow\" mechanisms. Also research \"solela.ai\" — a consumer product supposedly built on the same containerized-agent stack with \"privacy by architecture\" positioning; verify it exists and describe its onboarding/positioning (if not findable, say so).\n4. **\"Trust levels / expanding autonomy\" mechanisms**: any products or frameworks with graduated agent autonomy (human-in-the-loop → suggest → auto with review → full auto), e.g. approval workflows in Lindy/Zapier agents, \"confidence thresholds\", Microsoft Copilot agent governance, writeups on progressive agent autonomy in enterprises.\n5. **The employee-development angle**: anything that frames \"working with/managing your AI agent\" as a skill-building or career-development experience (L&D products with agents, \"AI upskilling through use\", prompts-to-skills gamification). Is anyone doing this explicitly, or is it whitespace?\n\nKey questions: (a) what onboarding/calibration UX actually retains non-technical users; (b) what the sales-role automation market treats as proven value; (c) is \"role methodology packs, personally calibrated\" occupied or whitespace; (d) is graduated-trust guardrails a real differentiator.\n\nIRON RULES: name products precisely, link sources per claim, verify before asserting (especially solela.ai), mark uncertainty. Structured English report. Your final message IS the deliverable.","agentId":"aef68527762188ce4","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Adjacent-Space Research Report: \"One Agent Per Employee\" Landscape (2025–2026)\n\nCompiled July 2026 from five parallel research streams, ~130 web sources. Every claim carries a source URL; vendor-reported numbers are labeled; uncertainty flagged inline. Solela.ai was independently re-verified by direct fetch before finalizing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. \"Digital employee / AI coworker\" products — does the category work?\n\n### Per-product summary\n\n**11x.ai (Alice, AI SDR)** — Outbound SDR \"digital worker.\" Sales-led onboarding (2–4 weeks ICP/email-infra setup); pricing from ~$36K/yr per its own page (https://www.11x.ai/products/alice/pricing). **The category's cautionary tale**: TechCrunch's March 2025 investigation (https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/24/a16z-and-benchmark-backed-11x-has-been-claiming-customers-it-doesnt-have/) found inflated ARR (contracts with 3-month break clauses counted as full ARR; one employee estimated only ~$3M of $14M reported ARR survived trials), logo misuse (ZoomInfo threatened legal action, said the product \"performed significantly worse than our SDR employees\"; Airtable never went past a short trial), and employee-reported **70–80% customer churn**. Founder Hasan Sukkar stepped down as CEO in May 2025; still operating under CTO-turned-CEO Prabhav Jain (https://toarn.com/insights/11x-ai — single secondary source for details; CEO change widely reported).\n\n**Artisan (Ava, AI BDR)** — Outbound BDR with standard white-glove onboarding (dedicated AM, 2–4 weeks) (https://www.artisan.co/pricing). Quote-based pricing; third parties report ~$1.5K–$5K+/mo for real BDR replacement (unverified). $25M Series A April 2025 (https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/09/artisan-the-stop-hiring-humans-ai-agent-startup-raises-25m-and-is-still-hiring-humans/); self-reported ~$5–6M ARR — modest revenue relative to the enormous noise of the \"Stop Hiring Humans\" rage-bait campaign (which generated ~$2M ARR and death threats: https://sfstandard.com/2025/04/07/the-real-person-behind-san-franciscos-hated-anti-human-ad-campaign/). Reported LinkedIn account restrictions for automation/spam are a structural product risk (single-source, unverified: https://completeaitraining.com/news/from-stop-hiring-humans-to-linkedin-bans-artisan-ais-viral/). Reviewer consensus: an email-volume machine; personalization is template-grade.\n\n**Maisa AI** — Not an SDR: auditable \"digital workers\" for compliance-sensitive back-office processes, explicitly counter-positioned against the \"95% of AI pilots fail\" narrative. Self-serve \"Maisa Studio\" where citizen developers train workers in natural language. $25M seed Aug 2025 (https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/27/maisa-ai-gets-25m-to-fix-enterprise-ais-95-failure-rate/); Tracxn lists a May 2026 Series A (amount unconfirmed). No public pricing, no independent efficacy evidence yet.\n\n**Piper (Qualified)** — Inbound AI SDR (website visitors → qualified meetings); requires Salesforce; ~$40–68K/yr per third parties (https://www.knock-ai.com/blog/qualified-pricing). 500+ customers incl. Box, Brex, Asana (vendor-reported). **The category's cleanest success: Salesforce agreed to acquire Qualified in Dec 2025** (https://www.saastr.com/why-salesforce-acquiring-qualified-is-maybe-the-smartest-ai-gtm-deal-of-2025/). Key lesson: it succeeded on a *bounded* task (qualifying warm inbound), not open-ended judgment.\n\n**Beam.ai** — Back-office agentic process automation; template-led onboarding; ~$299 entry (single source, unverified). Low public signal either way — no controversy, no independent evidence of scale (https://beam.ai/, https://www.selecthub.com/p/ai-agent-builder-software/beam-ai/).\n\n**Ema (ema.co)** — \"Universal AI Employee\" via pre-built Personas (support, HR, sales, legal); no-code builder, enterprise motion, on-prem options. $61M+ raised (Accel/Section 32: https://venturebeat.com/ai/ema-raises-36m-to-build-universal-ai-employees-for-enterprises); GetLatka estimates ~$15M 2025 revenue (unverified). Mid-pack; efficacy claims entirely vendor-sourced.\n\n**Lyzr** — Really an agent-infrastructure platform (\"Agentic OS\") with \"AI employees\" marketing; IT/developer-led onboarding. $14.5M Series A+ led by Accenture at $250M valuation, reported >300% QoQ revenue growth from a small base (https://finovate.com/agentic-ai-infrastructure-firm-lyzr-ai-raises-14-5-million-at-250-million-valuation/).\n\n**Cykel AI (Lucy/Eve/Samson)** — LSE-listed named digital workers. The audited numbers are damning: **£4,107 revenue in the half-year to July 2025** (not a typo), £1.2M pretax loss, shares down 29% on results (https://www.ajbell.co.uk/news/articles/cykel-ai-narrows-interim-loss-slashes-administrative-expenses). The starkest narrative-vs-reality gap in the category.\n\n**MultiOn → Please** — The autonomous web-agent pioneer split and pivoted hard: founder Div Garg left Dec 2024 to found AGI Inc.; the company rebranded to Please Platforms and is now, verified directly, a consumer **\"thinking reminders app\"** in research preview (https://please.ai). A quiet negative data point for general-purpose autonomous agents.\n\n**Devin (Cognition)** — The paradox. Independently measured early performance was poor: Answer.AI's month-long review scored **3 successes / 14 failures / 3 inconclusive** on 20 real tasks (\"tasks it can do are so small and well-defined that I may as well do them myself\": https://www.answer.ai/posts/2025-01-08-devin.html), and the launch Upwork demo was debunked (https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40008109). Yet commercially: ARR $1M → $73M (Sep 2024→Jun 2025, Sacra), Windsurf acquisition, $10.2B valuation Sep 2025 (https://www.cnbc.com/2025/09/08/cognition-valued-at-10point2-billion-two-months-after-windsurf-.html), then **$1B+ raise at ~$25B pre-money May 2026** (https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/27/ai-coding-startup-cognition-raises-1b-at-25b-pre-money-valuation/). Pricing dropped from $500/mo to a $20/mo usage-based entry tier.\n\n### Category verdict\n\n- **The fully-autonomous AI SDR wave of 2024–25 broadly failed**: analyst posts report 50–70% annual churn for AI SDR tools and reply-rate decay as buyers pattern-match AI templates (https://www.digitalapplied.com/blog/case-against-ai-sdrs-contrarian-analysis-2026 — directionally consistent across sources, individually unaudited). AI-app retention is structurally worse than SaaS (~21% vs ~31% annual: https://www.technewsworld.com/story/ai-apps-generate-revenue-but-struggle-with-retention-180236.html).\n- **Winners are bounded-task, verifiable-output products** (Devin: code compiles or doesn't; Piper: warm inbound qualification). **Losers sold headcount replacement on open-ended judgment** (11x, Cykel, MultiOn).\n- The \"digital employee\" *metaphor* looks like a 2024 marketing artifact; surviving products are sold as tools with narrow measurable outcomes, humans in the loop, and the capability is being absorbed into existing sales stacks as a feature layer.\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: the vision — agent as *amplifier of an employee* rather than *replacement of an employee* — sits on the correct side of the documented failure line. The failures were all \"fire the human\"; nobody on this list productizes \"one agent per employee that makes the employee better.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. Post-call sales workflow automation — table-stakes vs differentiating\n\n### What each product automates after a call\n\n| Product | Post-call automation | Key detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Momentum.io** | The deepest CRM-write engine: \"Autopilot\" pipelines extract **MEDDIC/MEDDPICC/BANT and arbitrary custom fields** from transcripts into any Salesforce field/object; drafts follow-ups for approval; creates cases; Slack deal rooms (https://www.momentum.io/meddic-autopilot, https://docs.momentum.io/autopilot) | Admin-defined per-field prompts with save policies: **confirm / write-if-empty / fully-automatic** — a shipped mini trust-level model |\n| **Attention.com** | CRM auto-update agent, <60s follow-up email drafts in rep's voice, coaching scorecards, Slack deal-risk alerts (https://www.attention.com/product/ai-sales-agents) | $14M Series A; G2 4.9 but only ~61 reviews — small footprint |\n| **Gong** | 18 named agents (Apr 2025→Oct 2025): AI Composer (follow-ups), AI Tasker (next steps), **AI Deal Reviewer (scores deals against sales methodologies)**, Activity Mapper, trigger-based auto-briefs (https://www.gong.io/press/gong-redefines-ai-agents-to-deliver-real-revenue-impact) | **All agents included free with Gong licenses** — priced at zero, i.e., commoditized. Self-reported $500M+ ARR (unverified) |\n| **Salesforce Agentforce** | SDR agent (inbound nurture + warm-handoff brief) and Sales Coach (role-play scoring). Notably **no strong call→CRM-fields agent** (https://www.salesforce.com/news/stories/einstein-sales-agents-announcement/) | Documented adoption struggle: ~6% of customers *paid*; guidance cut citing slow adoption (https://www.salesforceben.com/where-are-we-really-at-with-agentforce-adoption/); pricing whiplash $2/conversation → $0.10/action Flex Credits |\n| **HubSpot Breeze** | **Smart Deal Progression** (Spring 2026, beta): after every call — *suggested* CRM updates using your pipeline definitions + drafted follow-up + action items (https://www.hubspot.com/company-news/spring-2026-spotlight) | Suggest-only, not autonomous writes; agents repriced to **pay-per-outcome** ($0.50/resolved conversation, $1/qualified lead, Apr 2026) |\n| **Day.ai** | AI-native CRM: calls/email auto-build and maintain the CRM itself, no data entry (https://www.bvp.com/atlas/lessons-from-day-ais-journey-to-becoming-the-waymo-of-crm) | $20M Sequoia Series A Feb 2026, ~120 customers — the \"post-call automation ceases to be a feature\" bet |\n| **Granola** | Notes + \"Recipes\" (repeatable post-call prompt workflows, Sept 2025); CRM sync to HubSpot/Affinity/Attio (not native Salesforce) (https://www.granola.ai/) | $125M Series C at **$1.5B**, Mar 2026 (https://techcrunch.com/2026/03/25/granola-raises-125m-hits-1-5b-valuation-as-it-expands-from-meeting-notetaker-to-enterprise-ai-app/) — pivoting to \"enterprise company memory\" |\n| **Fireflies.ai** | Transcripts/summaries/action items → HubSpot tasks, Salesforce/HubSpot record sync, Slack recaps at **$19/user/mo** (https://fireflies.ai/integrations) | Profitable since 2023, $1B valuation on ~$19M raised — the capital-efficiency benchmark |\n| **Fathom** | Free-tier summaries/action items/follow-up drafts; CRM field sync + coaching scorecards at $25/user/mo (https://www.fathom.ai/) | HubSpot's 2025 Most Used App; $10M→$30M revenue in a year (self-reported) |\n\n### Table-stakes vs differentiating (the market's answer)\n\n**Table-stakes (free-to-$25/user, nobody pays a premium):** transcription, summaries, action items, follow-up email *drafts*, Slack recaps, basic CRM note logging. Structural proof: Gong bundles all 18 agents at no extra cost and HubSpot builds suggested post-call CRM updates into the CRM natively — when both gorillas price it at zero, it's table-stakes (https://www.laxis.com/blog/state-of-meeting-note-taking-2026/ corroborates).\n\n**Differentiating (what still commands money):**\n1. **Trusted autonomous CRM writes with policy controls** — Momentum's confirm/write-if-empty/full-auto save policies; HubSpot stops at \"suggested,\" and that autonomy gap is the value line.\n2. **Methodology-specific extraction** (MEDDIC/BANT into real fields) — a RevOps, top-down sale.\n3. **Multi-step cross-system chains** (call → fields + case + Slack + email + warehouse).\n4. **Institutional memory across calls** (Gong Ask Anything, Granola's pivot).\n\n**Buyer-behavior signals:** two forced repricings in 12 months (Salesforce off per-conversation, HubSpot to pay-per-result) show buyers resist paying for AI *activity* and will pay for *outcomes*. Retention correlates with workflow embedding, not model quality — \"prompts are portable… the plumbing is harder to replicate\" (https://www.saastr.com/the-wave-of-ai-agent-churn-to-come-prompts-are-portable/).\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: the post-sales-call *chain* itself (summary → follow-up → CRM) is commoditized. What's not commoditized: methodology-aware extraction personally calibrated to the rep, and graduated write-autonomy — which is exactly the Momentum policy model generalized. Note Momentum's shipped confirm/write-if-empty/auto controls as prior art.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Onboarding-by-interview / calibration patterns + solela.ai\n\n### solela.ai — VERIFIED, exists and is live\nIndependently confirmed by direct fetch of https://www.solela.ai/ (plus https://elion.solela.ai/). A personal AI assistant across web/WhatsApp/Skills with unified memory (\"One AI. Four lives. The same memory — anywhere you write\"). Positioning is explicitly **\"Privacy by architecture\"**, verbatim claims: \"OS-level isolation per agent. No noisy neighbours,\" \"Keys live in a gateway. The agent never sees them,\" \"Approval before action,\" \"Full audit trail,\" \"Cross-workspace reads are architecturally impossible.\" Onboarding is ~60 seconds, three steps (Google sign-in → \"Name it\" → start writing on web/WhatsApp) — **notably no interview/calibration step is advertised**. No independent press, funding, or reviews found; company scale unverified. Do not conflate with a16z-backed Sola (sola.ai).\n\n### The three calibration patterns in the market\n1. **Interview-first** (rare): **Dot (New Computer)** was the purest consumer example — onboarding asked getting-to-know-you questions and probed deeper to build a psychological profile (https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/21/dots-ai-really-really-wants-to-get-to-know-you/) — **and it shut down Oct 2025** (https://techcrunch.com/2025/09/05/personalized-ai-companion-app-dot-is-shutting-down/). **OpenAI GPT Builder** is the canonical \"agent interviews you, then writes its own config\" mechanism (https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8770868-gpt-builder). Coaching products do it well: **BetterUp** (2–3 min intake survey + Whole Person Assessment), **Rocky.ai** (goal/obstacle intake), **Valence Nadia** (calibration *through* conversation; generates a user-controlled \"collaboration profile\" other Nadias can read — https://www.bvp.com/news/meet-nadia-valences-ai-coach-for-leadership-development-and-workplace-collaboration).\n2. **Template/segmentation-first** (dominant): **Lindy** asks role/industry, then lands you on role-matched templates with a working example (https://obviostudio.substack.com/p/ai-integration-lindy-from-onboarding); **Zapier Agents** builds config from a natural-language description via a prompt assistant.\n3. **Ingestion/ambient-first**: **Martin** (connect email/calendar, memory built through use), **ohai.ai** (forms + forward-your-emails), **Yohana** (human-guide intake — the concierge analog; medium confidence, pages partially unreachable), **Solela** (name it and go).\n\n### What retains non-technical users (UX research)\n- Analysis of 150+ AI onboarding flows: nearly all successful products converged on **template activation loops**; blank canvas kills conversion; **generated editable drafts beat static templates** (https://www.fishmanafnewsletter.com/p/how-ai-products-drive-adoption-in-onboarding-through-template-activation-loop).\n- Wow moment within ~3 minutes; light role/goal questions make users feel it's \"built for someone like you,\" but long interviews are absent from winners (https://www.growthmates.news/p/what-ai-products-get-right-and-wrong). Sub-60-second activation is the emerging bar (https://productled.com/blog/ai-onboarding).\n- The Valence/Gilead deployment finding: getting users into a live session within 10 minutes — where participants \"felt Nadia was getting to know them\" — produced the aha (https://www.valence.co/ai-and-the-workforce/how-gilead-sciences-is-scaling-ai-coaching-to-all-employees-kerry-okeeffe-gilead-valence).\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: a long upfront self-profiling interview is empirically risky (Dot died; winners activate in minutes). The evidence-backed hybrid: 1–3 light calibration questions + a role template that produces a *working draft workflow immediately*, with the deep profiling interview woven into the first real work sessions (Nadia model) rather than gating them.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Graduated trust / expanding autonomy\n\n**Bottom line: graduated trust exists as vocabulary and process everywhere, as an automated product mechanism almost nowhere.**\n\n- **What's shipped is static:** per-step approval toggles (Lindy HITL confirmations — https://docs.lindy.ai/testing/human-in-the-loop; Relay.app's approval/data-input/AI-output-review steps — https://docs.relay.app/human-in-the-loop/human-in-the-loop-steps; Zapier pause-for-review; LangGraph `interrupt` per-tool approve/edit/reject) and discrete global modes (Claude Code's default/plan/acceptEdits/bypass; Devin CLI modes; OpenAI Operator's fixed auto/watch/human-only tiers keyed to site sensitivity — https://openai.com/index/introducing-operator/). Microsoft's Agent 365 / Copilot Studio governance is org-level control of which agents exist, not per-agent graduation (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot/blog/copilot-studio/new-and-improved-agent-governance-intelligent-workflows-and-connected-app-experiences/).\n- **Closest shipped approximations:** (1) **Cursor Auto-review** (June 2026) — a classifier in the execution path modulates freedom per action, marketed as \"autonomy is a dial, not a switch\"; blocks ~4% of actions, cut user interruptions from ~40% to ~7% (https://cursor.com/blog/agent-autonomy-auto-review) — but it's *contextual*, not *earned over time*. (2) Claude Code's persistent allowlists — the envelope widens with tenure, but by manual grants. (3) Decagon's percentage-of-traffic ramps + shadow mode in support AI (https://decagon.ai/blog/ai-customer-support-setup) — real graduation, human-decided. (4) Momentum's confirm/write-if-empty/auto save policies (section 2).\n- **Frameworks exist and are current:** Feng/McDonald/Zhang, \"Levels of Autonomy for AI Agents\" (arXiv June 2025) — five SAE-style levels defined by user role (Operator→Observer), plus a proposed \"autonomy certificates\" governance mechanism (https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12469). Enterprise playbooks converge on \"autonomy must be earned\": Microsoft SOC crawl-walk-run guide; ManageEngine (\"expand execution privileges only after reversal rates decline\").\n- **The key empirical datum:** Anthropic's \"Measuring AI agent autonomy in practice\" (Feb 2026) — full auto-approve appears in ~20% of new users' sessions vs >40% at 750+ sessions, with oversight shifting from approval to monitoring (https://www.anthropic.com/research/measuring-agent-autonomy). **Earned trust demonstrably happens — in the user, not in the product.**\n- **No verified shipped product implements automatic earned autonomy** (permission scope widening automatically as track record improves — e.g., auto-promotion after N clean approvals). This is an absence-of-evidence finding after targeted search, not proof of nonexistence.\n\n**Implication for NanoCo**: \"guardrails that expand as trust grows\" — if made *mechanical* (track-record-gated promotion from suggest → auto-with-review → auto, per action type, visible to both employee and org) — is genuine whitespace with academic legitimation (autonomy-certificates paper) and behavioral validation (Anthropic data). It is a real differentiator; per-step approval toggles alone are not.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The employee-development angle — mostly whitespace\n\n- **Crowded:** courses ABOUT agents (Coursera/Microsoft/IBM certs, Section, Salesforce **Agentblazer** gamified status ladder — https://trailhead.salesforce.com/agentblazer); agents that DELIVER training (Sana — acquired by Workday for ~$1.1B; Uplimit; Degreed Maestro; Microsoft Learning Agent); and rhetoric: Microsoft Work Trend Index 2025's **\"agent boss\"** (\"builds, delegates to, and manages agents… taking control of their career in the age of AI\"; 28% of managers considering hiring AI-workforce managers — https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index/2025-the-year-the-frontier-firm-is-born), McKinsey's \"agentic AI literacy,\" HBR's \"Create an Onboarding Plan for AI Agents\" (Mar 2026), Ethan Mollick's \"Management as AI superpower.\"\n- **The scientific anchor exists, unproductized:** Weidmann/Xu/**Deming**, NBER WP 33662 — leadership effectiveness with AI-agent teams correlates **0.81** with leading human teams; agent-based leadership assessment cost $23 vs $114/participant (https://www.nber.org/digest/202507/leadership-skills-managing-ai-agents-vs-humans). Managing agents ≈ managing people, empirically.\n- **Adjacent pieces exist separately:** Anthropic's AI Fluency 4Ds framework names **Delegation and Discernment** as teachable competencies (https://aifluencyframework.org/); Bryq scores per-employee AI fluency 0–100 (https://www.bryq.com/product/ai-fluency); Copilot Dashboard tracks adoption per cohort (framed by press as surveillance, not development); Shopify put AI use into performance reviews (Duolingo did too, then rolled it back by Apr 2026 — https://fortune.com/2026/04/13/duolingo-ceo-luis-von-ahn-ai-usage-requirement-employee-performance-evaluations/); Iro AI is literally \"Duolingo for AI\" with an agents path (https://tryiro.com/).\n- **Nobody found productizing \"the agent-work itself develops the employee\"** — no product scores your real delegation/feedback/verification behavior with your own work agent over time and maps it to a competency ladder or career progression. All the ingredients (science, framework, telemetry, gamification) exist unassembled. Flagged: a sub-visibility startup could exist here.\n\n---\n\n## Answers to the four key questions\n\n**(a) What onboarding UX retains non-technical users?** Not a long interview. The converged winning pattern: role-based template + generated editable draft + wow-moment inside ~3 minutes; deep calibration deferred into the first real work sessions where the user *feels* the agent getting to know them (Valence/Gilead). Dot — the purest interview-first product — is dead. NanoCo's self-profiling interview should produce a visibly working workflow fast, then keep interviewing incrementally.\n\n**(b) What does the sales market treat as proven value?** Table-stakes: transcription/summary/action-items/follow-up drafts/Slack recaps (priced at ~zero by Gong and HubSpot). Proven paid value: trusted autonomous CRM writes with policy controls, methodology-specific field extraction (MEDDIC etc.), and multi-step cross-system chains — sold to RevOps on pipeline-hygiene ROI. Buyers are forcing outcome-based pricing (two major repricings in 12 months). Retention comes from workflow embedding, not model quality.\n\n**(c) Is \"role methodology packs, personally calibrated\" occupied?** Substantially whitespace as a combination. Pieces exist: Gong's AI Deal Reviewer scores against methodologies; Momentum extracts MEDDIC to fields; Ema sells role Personas; coaching products calibrate to the person. Nobody found combines *role methodology encoded as multi-step workflows at core moments* + *personal calibration via interview* + *one persistent agent per employee*. Closest structural analogs are admin-configured RevOps tools (team-level, not person-level) — the person-level calibration is the open slot. Caveat: the \"digital employee\" graveyard (section 1) shows the framing must stay \"amplifier,\" not \"replacement.\"\n\n**(d) Is graduated-trust guardrails a real differentiator?** Yes — with precision. Per-step approvals and mode toggles are commodity. What no verified product ships is *automatic earned autonomy*: permission scope that widens mechanically with a clean track record. The demand is documented (enterprise playbooks all preach it), the behavior is measured (Anthropic: auto-approve doubles with user tenure), the framework is published (arXiv 2506.12469 \"autonomy certificates\") — and the mechanism is unbuilt. That, plus the fact that it doubles as the employee-development mechanic (earning your agent more autonomy *is* the skill ladder — question 5's whitespace), makes it arguably NanoCo's most defensible differentiator, since it uniquely fuses two gaps no one else connects.\n\n**Biggest overall caveat:** many category-level statistics (AI SDR churn rates, several ARR figures) come from vendor-adjacent content marketing — directionally corroborated across independent sources but individually unaudited; all such figures are flagged inline above."}],"resolvedModel":"claude-fable-5","totalDurationMs":563881,"totalTokens":71252,"totalToolUseCount":7,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16328,"cache_read_input_tokens":45153,"output_tokens":9769,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16328},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9769,"cache_read_input_tokens":45153,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16328,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16328,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"46362501-3ba0-4026-9d72-0d37b722415e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent","sessionId":"0bec9074-f0ee-4108-82bf-5bb584ae73d